var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"lib/cuda/#CUDA-extension","page":"CUDA extension","text":"Loaded with using CUDA. It accepts the sparse matrices of cuSPARSE and routes the dense operations of large fronts to cuBLAS and cuSOLVER.","title":"CUDA extension"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#SparseDirectSolver.CSR-Tuple{cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSC}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"CSR(A::CuSparseMatrixCSC)\n\nWrap A (m × n) without copying as the CSR of transpose(A) (n × m, transposed = true), sharing colPtr, rowVal and nzVal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.CSR"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#SparseDirectSolver.CSR-Tuple{cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSR}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"CSR(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR)\n\nWrap A without copying: the CSR shares rowPtr, colVal and nzVal (one-based, transposed = false).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.CSR"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#SparseDirectSolver.DirectSolver-Tuple{cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSR, Any, Any}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"DirectSolver(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR, structure::String, view::Char; index = 'O')\nDirectSolver(A::CuSparseMatrixCSC, structure::String, view::Char; index = 'O')\n\nSolver on A's arrays, without copies (≅ CudssSolver(A, structure, view)). A CuSparseMatrixCSC is read as the CSR of its transpose with the view flipped (MadNLP's lower-triangle CSC becomes an upper-triangle CSR), see DirectSolver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.DirectSolver"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSR-Union{Tuple{CSR{T, INT, <:CuArray{INT, 1}, <:CuArray{T}}}, Tuple{INT}, Tuple{T}} where {T, INT}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"CuSparseMatrixCSR(A::CSR)\n\nThe stored CSR matrix of A (the transposed flag is not applied) as a CuSparseMatrixCSR. One-based arrays are shared; zero-based index arrays are copied and rebased. Batches (nbatch(A) > 1) are rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSR"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/cuda/#LinearAlgebra.cholesky","page":"CUDA extension","text":"cholesky(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR, NoPivot(); view = 'F', check = false) -> DirectSolver\ncholesky(Symmetric(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR)) / cholesky(Hermitian(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR))\n\nLLᵀ/LLᴴ factorization of A on the GPU (≅ CUDSS.jl's cholesky); the wrappers pass their uplo as the view. See cholesky(::CSR).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cholesky"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#LinearAlgebra.ldlt-Tuple{cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSR}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"ldlt(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR; view = 'F', check = false) -> DirectSolver\nldlt(Symmetric(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR)) / ldlt(Hermitian(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR))\n\nLDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization of A on the GPU (≅ CUDSS.jl's ldlt); the wrappers pass their uplo as the view. See ldlt(::CSR).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.ldlt"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#LinearAlgebra.lu-Tuple{cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSR}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"lu(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR; check = false) -> DirectSolver\n\nL D U factorization (structure \"G\") of A on the GPU (≅ CUDSS.jl's lu). See lu(::CSR).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.lu"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#SparseDirectSolver.max_local_bytes-Tuple{CUDABackend}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"max_local_bytes(::CUDABackend) -> Int\n\n48 KiB: @localmem is static shared memory, and ptxas rejects more than 48 KiB of static shared data per kernel on every CUDA device (larger amounts need dynamic shared memory with an opt-in attribute). The regime-A kernels therefore stop at the 48 KiB class on CUDA (issue #60).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.max_local_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#SparseDirectSolver.to_backend-Tuple{SparseMatrixCSC, CUDABackend}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"to_backend(A::SparseMatrixCSC, ::CUDABackend; index = 'O') -> CSR\n\nCSR arrays of A built on the host, then uploaded as CuVectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.to_backend"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/cuda/#SparseDirectSolver.update!-Tuple{DirectSolver, Union{cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSC, cuSPARSE.CuSparseMatrixCSR}}","page":"CUDA extension","text":"update!(solver::DirectSolver, A::CuSparseMatrixCSR)\nupdate!(solver::DirectSolver, A::CuSparseMatrixCSC)\n\nPoint solver at the arrays of A (≅ cudss_update(solver, A)), see update!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.update!"},{"category":"section","location":"lib/internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"Docstrings of the non-exported functions and types: the host symbolic analysis, the numeric kernels, the solve and the dense interface. They describe the implementation and change without notice; the design is in PLAN.md.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ASSEMBLY_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the assembly kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ASSEMBLY_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.AlgorithmEnum","page":"Internals","text":"AlgorithmEnum\n\nUnion of the enums set through \"default\"/\"algoN\" strings (or the integer N).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.AlgorithmEnum"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.BUNCH_KAUFMAN_ALPHA","page":"Internals","text":"BUNCH_KAUFMAN_ALPHA\n\nα = (1 + √17)/8, the Bunch–Kaufman constant that minimizes the element growth bound of 1×1/2×2 pivoting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.BUNCH_KAUFMAN_ALPHA"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.CharEnum","page":"Internals","text":"CharEnum\n\nUnion of the enums spelled with characters (convert(E, ::AbstractChar), convert(Char, x)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.CharEnum"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.DEVICE_MAPS","page":"Internals","text":"DEVICE_MAPS\n\nNames of the index vectors of a Symbolic that live on the device (VI), in the order adapt moves them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.DEVICE_MAPS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.FGMRES_PROVIDER","page":"Internals","text":"FGMRES_PROVIDER\n\nRef to the FGMRES driver of the Krylov.jl extension (SparseDirectSolverKrylovExt), set in its __init__; nothing while Krylov.jl is not loaded (ir_mode = \"fgmres\" then raises NotSupportedError). Called as provider(cache, A, P, R, len; atol, itmax) -> (D, iterations): FGMRES for A D = R[1:len] with right preconditioner P (mul!(y, P, x)), absolute residual tolerance atol, at most itmax iterations, storage kept in cache (a Ref{Any}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.FGMRES_PROVIDER"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.FRONT_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the fused regime-B kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.FRONT_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.KA_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Default 1-D workgroup size of the single-workgroup kernels (potrf, getrf, trsm).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.KA_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_BLOCKED_MIN_FCLASS","page":"Internals","text":"Smallest row class of a regime-B bin that the LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization runs on the blocked regime-C path (ldlt_blocked_path) instead of the fused front kernel (together with LDLT_BLOCKED_MIN_WCLASS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_BLOCKED_MIN_FCLASS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_BLOCKED_MIN_WCLASS","page":"Internals","text":"Smallest width class of a regime-B bin that the LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization runs on the blocked regime-C path (ldlt_blocked_path) instead of the fused front kernel (together with LDLT_BLOCKED_MIN_FCLASS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_BLOCKED_MIN_WCLASS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_C_NB","page":"Internals","text":"Pivot columns per block of the regime-C LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ path (panel_ldlt_kernel!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_C_NB"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_C_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the regime-C LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ panel kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_C_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ front kernel (regimes B and C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.LDLT_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.LINEAR_ALGEBRA_IR_STEPS","page":"Internals","text":"Refinement steps of the solvers created by cholesky, ldlt and lu (PLAN §3.1; the handle layer uses 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.LINEAR_ALGEBRA_IR_STEPS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.LU_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the LU front kernel (regimes B and C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.LU_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ND_PROVIDER","page":"Internals","text":"ND_PROVIDER\n\nRef holding nothing or a function (nd_nlevels, nd_ubfactor) -> alg that returns the CliqueTrees nested-dissection algorithm. Set by the Metis extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ND_PROVIDER"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.PERMUTE_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the permutation kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.PERMUTE_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.PIVOT_PAIRS_MAX_ROUNDS","page":"Internals","text":"PIVOT_PAIRS_MAX_ROUNDS\n\nMaximum number of zero_pivot_pairs! rounds (each followed by a re-ordering) of the pivot_pairs = \"default\" analysis (8).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.PIVOT_PAIRS_MAX_ROUNDS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.PIVOT_PAIR_TOLERANCE","page":"Internals","text":"PIVOT_PAIR_TOLERANCE\n\nDefault relative tolerance of the 2×2 candidate test of pivot_pairs: row i is a candidate when |aᵢᵢ| ≤ τ · maxⱼ≠ᵢ |aᵢⱼ|, τ = the parameter \"pivot_pair_tolerance\" (default 1e-6).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.PIVOT_PAIR_TOLERANCE"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.REFINE_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the residual-norm reduction (one workgroup per right-hand side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.REFINE_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.REGIME_A","page":"Internals","text":"REGIME_A, REGIME_B, REGIME_C\n\nRegime codes (Int8) of Schedule.regime: fused subtree kernels, fused per-front level kernels, vendor dense calls on large fronts (PLAN §2.2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.REGIME_A"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.REGIME_B_MAX_WIDTH","page":"Internals","text":"Largest front width with a fused regime-B kernel; wider bins (raised regime_c_width) take the regime-C path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.REGIME_B_MAX_WIDTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.REGIME_B_WIDTHS","page":"Internals","text":"Width classes with a fused regime-B kernel (@localmem holds the packed W×W lower triangle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.REGIME_B_WIDTHS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SCHUR_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the Schur kernels (metadata, LU assembly, export).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SCHUR_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SOLVE_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the solve kernels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SOLVE_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.STATS_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the per-phase reduction of the front statistics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.STATS_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_LOCAL_RESERVE","page":"Internals","text":"Bytes of a regime-A kernel's local memory kept for its control words and pivot (not for fronts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_LOCAL_RESERVE"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_LOCAL_RESERVE_LDLT","page":"Internals","text":"Bytes of the regime-A LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ kernel's local memory kept for its control words, pivot block and the reduction slots of the cooperative pivot search (structures \"S\"/\"H\"; at most 944 bytes for ComplexF64 with Int64 maps).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_LOCAL_RESERVE_LDLT"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_LOCAL_SIZES","page":"Internals","text":"@localmem sizes (bytes) of the regime-A kernel instances; a budget uses the largest one it holds. @localmem is static shared memory, so the usable sizes depend on the backend (max_local_bytes): 48 KiB on CUDA (ptxas rejects more static shared data), 64 KiB on AMDGPU (LDS per workgroup); the analysis keeps the budgets within the backend's cap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_LOCAL_SIZES"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_PARALLELISM","page":"Internals","text":"Default subtree_parallelism: a regime-A subtree does at most this fraction (inverse) of the factorization flops.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_PARALLELISM"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_WORKGROUP","page":"Internals","text":"Workgroup size of the fused regime-A kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SUBTREE_WORKGROUP"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.StringEnum","page":"Internals","text":"StringEnum\n\nUnion of the enums spelled with strings (convert(E, ::AbstractString), convert(String, x)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.StringEnum"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.FullPatternMap, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"SparseMatrixCSC(F::FullPatternMap, nzval) -> SparseMatrixCSC\n\nThe full matrix M described by F with the values taken from the user's nzval (host copy; for tests and debugging).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern}","page":"Internals","text":"SparseMatrixCSC(P::SymmetricPattern) -> SparseMatrixCSC{Bool,Int}\n\nThe pattern as a Boolean sparse matrix (no diagonal), for tests and debugging.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC-Union{Tuple{CSR{T, INT, VI, VT} where {VI<:AbstractVector{INT}, VT<:AbstractVecOrMat{T}}}, Tuple{INT}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{CSR{T, INT, VI, VT} where {VI<:AbstractVector{INT}, VT<:AbstractVecOrMat{T}}, Integer}} where {T, INT}","page":"Internals","text":"SparseMatrixCSC(A::CSR[, k = 1])\n\nHost copy of batch member k of the stored CSR matrix (size(A), index type INT, base rebased to one). The transposed flag is not applied: for B = csr_of_transpose(M), SparseMatrixCSC(B) == transpose(M).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseArrays.SparseMatrixCSC"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.AmalgamationParams","page":"Internals","text":"AmalgamationParams\n\nType of the \"amalgamation\" parameter: (max_width, zero_fraction, min_width), the relaxed-amalgamation limits of PLAN §2.3 step 4 (maximum width of a merged panel; a fundamental supernode wider than that stays whole, maximum fraction of explicit zeros added to the factor, target minimum width).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.AmalgamationParams"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.BatchMap","page":"Internals","text":"BatchMap{V <: Union{Nothing, AbstractVector{Int32}}}\n\nBatch part of a kernel launch (an argument of every numeric and solve kernel): members[1:nact] are the active batch members (1-based, increasing; members = nothing: a single matrix, member 1), nbatch the batch size, first the launch's first entry in its node list (the former first argument of the kernels) and nrhs the right-hand sides per member of a solve (1 in the numeric phase). Adapted to the device with its members vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.BatchMap"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ColumnPartition","page":"Internals","text":"ColumnPartition\n\nA partition of the columns of an elimination tree into supernodes (contiguous column ranges after a renumbering), without row structure:\n\norder::Vector{Int}: column k of the supernodal numbering is column order[k] of the etree numbering (a topological order of the etree);\nsuper_ptr::Vector{Int}: supernode s owns columns super_ptr[s]:(super_ptr[s+1]-1) of the supernodal numbering;\nsnparent::Vector{Int}: parent supernode, 0 for a root.\n\nReturned by fundamental_supernodes and amalgamate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ColumnPartition"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.DenseCapabilities","page":"Internals","text":"DenseCapabilities\n\nResult of capabilities: one Bool per probe.\n\ngeneric_mul, generic_trsm (triangular ldiv!/rdiv!), generic_cholesky (cholesky!), generic_lu (lu!): LinearAlgebra entry points.\nvendor_gemm, vendor_syrk, vendor_herk, vendor_trsm, vendor_potrf, vendor_getrf, vendor_sytrf, vendor_gemm_strided_batched, vendor_trsm_batched, vendor_potrf_batched, vendor_getrf_batched: the vendor_* bindings of the backend.\nreshape_view_mul: mul! accepts a panel view reshape(view(buf, r), f, w).\natomic_add: Atomix.@atomic x[i] += v works on T in a KA kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.DenseCapabilities"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.FactorPreconditioner","page":"Internals","text":"FactorPreconditioner(ws, S, N, nrhs, ncols, conjugate, deterministic, interrupt, transpose = false,\n                     scaling = nothing)\n\nop(A)⁻¹ through the factorization in N (permutation, forward, diagonal and backward sweeps in the solve workspace ws; conj(M⁻¹ conj(x)) when conjugate; the sweeps of Mᵀ when transpose, LU only) on the stacked compact columns (nrhs per active member, ncols in all) of the RefinementOperator; with the matching permutations and scalings of a SolveScaling scaling (solve_io). mul!(y, P, x) polls interrupt first (InterruptedError); asynchronous otherwise. The right preconditioner of FGMRES-IR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.FactorPreconditioner"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.FactorizationAlg","page":"Internals","text":"FactorizationAlg\n\nfactorization_alg values (cuDSS cudssFactorizationAlg_t, reinterpreted, PLAN §1.3): \"default\" automatic, \"algo1\" very-sparse-factor path (regimes A/B only, no vendor calls), \"algo2\" vendor dense calls above the subtree regime.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.FactorizationAlg"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.FullPatternMap","page":"Internals","text":"FullPatternMap\n\nHost description of the full matrix M behind a user CSR input (PLAN §2.3 step 1, used by the residual SpMV of refinement): M as a 1-based CSR (rowptr, colval, sorted columns, duplicates merged) plus, for every entry e of M, the user nzval entries that make it up:\n\nM.nzval[e] = Σ_{s ∈ srcptr[e]:srcptr[e+1]-1}  conjflag[s] ? conj(nzval[src[s]]) : nzval[src[s]]\n\nsrc holds 1-based positions in the user's nzval (first batch member; member k adds (k - 1) * nnz). For symmetric structures the triangle given by the view is mirrored; for \"H\"/\"HPD\" the mirrored entries are conjugated. The map describes the stored CSR matrix; the transposed flag of a CSR is applied by the solver (through solve_mode).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.FullPatternMap"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.IRMode","page":"Internals","text":"IRMode\n\nir_mode values (PLAN §1.7): \"ir\" plain iterative refinement, \"fgmres\" FGMRES with the factorization as preconditioner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.IRMode"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.IndexBase","page":"Internals","text":"IndexBase\n\nIndex base of rowPtr/colVal: 'Z' zero-based, 'O' one-based (cuDSS cudssIndexBase_t).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.IndexBase"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Layout","page":"Internals","text":"Layout\n\nStatic memory layout of the numeric phase (PLAN §2.3 steps 6 and 8), element offsets, 1-based:\n\npanel_ptr (length ns + 1): panel of supernode s at panel_ptr[s]:(panel_ptr[s+1]-1), column-major f×w; factor_len entries in all;\nd_ptr (length ns + 1): D entries of supernode s at d_ptr[s]:(d_ptr[s+1]-1) (its columns); the subdiagonal of 2×2 pivots at n more; d_len = 2n;\ncb_ptr, cb_len: contribution block of s on the update stack (0/0 if it has none there); cb_first, cb_last: the steps it lives in (step 0 is regime A, see Schedule);\nstep_top[t + 1]: last update-stack entry in use during step t (t = 0:nsteps); stack_len = maximum(step_top) is the high-water mark;\nwork_len: entries of the regime-C syrk workspace (largest m^2 of a front on the regime-C path, takes_c_path, with a block on the stack; LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ: the largest sum of ldlt_c_work_len over a chunk of concurrent fronts on the blocked path, ldlt_blocked_path and ldlt_c_chunk_end);\nlocal_front, local_cb (regime A, 0 elsewhere): local-memory offset of the packed front of s and of its contribution block after the move (0 for a subtree root, whose block goes to the update stack); local_len[t]: entries of the serial stack of subtree t (its peak).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Layout"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Matching","page":"Internals","text":"Matching\n\nResult of compute_matching (host):\n\nalg::MatchingAlg: the job that ran (MATCHING_AUTO resolved to job 5);\nperm::Vector{Int}: the matching permutation, row i matched to column perm[i] (the \"perm_matching\" data parameter; for a structurally singular matrix the unmatched rows get the unmatched columns in increasing order);\nmatched::BitVector: row i has a matched entry (false only for a structurally singular matrix);\nrscale, cscale::Vector{Float64}: row and column scaling factors (\"scale_row\", \"scale_col\"; ones for jobs 1–4, equal for the symmetric structures);\nsymmetric::Bool: symmetric scaling only (structures other than \"G\"): the factored matrix is D A D, not permuted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Matching"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.MatchingAlg","page":"Internals","text":"MatchingAlg\n\nmatching_alg values (cuDSS cudssMatchingAlg_t): \"default\" no matching, \"algo1\"–\"algo5\" MC64 jobs 1–5, \"algo6\" automatic (job 5).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.MatchingAlg"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.MatchingState","page":"Internals","text":"MatchingState{VI, VR, VT}\n\nDevice state of the matching and scaling of a DirectSolver (matching_alg ≠ \"default\"), built by \"symbolic_factorization\" from the host Matching: io (the SolveScaling of the solve phases), weights (rᵢ cⱼ of every stored entry, entry_scaling) and values (the scaled values of every batch member, the input of the numeric phase, scale_values!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.MatchingState"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.MatrixView","page":"Internals","text":"MatrixView\n\nWhich part of the matrix is read: 'F' full, 'L' lower, 'U' upper triangle (cuDSS cudssMatrixViewType_t).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.MatrixView"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.MemberPanels","page":"Internals","text":"MemberPanels{V}\n\nThe panel pointers of member k of an nb-member batch in the interleaved factor layout: P[s] = nb (front_ptr[s] - 1) + (k - 1)(front_ptr[s+1] - front_ptr[s]) + 1 (the first entry of the f×w panel of supernode s of member k). Passed to the kernels in place of front_ptr for the factor offsets; P.ptr is the analysis's front_ptr. Note that P[s + 1] - P[s] is not a panel length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.MemberPanels"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Numeric","page":"Internals","text":"Numeric{T, VT <: AbstractVector{T}, VS <: AbstractVector{Int64}, VI <: AbstractVector{Int32},\n        VK <: AbstractVector{Int8}}\n\nNumeric storage of a factorization (PLAN §3.2), laid out by the Layout of a Symbolic:\n\nfactor (layout.factor_len entries): the panel of supernode s is the column-major f×w block factor[panel_ptr[s]:(panel_ptr[s+1]-1)] (leading dimension f, rows snrows(s), the upper triangle of its diagonal block is unused and kept zero);\nd (layout.d_len = 2n entries): D of LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ (unused by Cholesky): d[k] is the diagonal of D at factor column k, d[n + k] the subdiagonal entry D[k+1, k] of a 2×2 block starting at k (zero otherwise);\npiv (n Int32, LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ only): the local pivot order, piv[k] is the column of P A Pᵀ (supernodal numbering before pivoting) eliminated at factor column k; pivoting stays inside a supernode, so piv permutes each sncols(s) (see ref_ldlt!);\npivot_kind (n Int8, LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ only): PIVOT_KIND_1X1, PIVOT_KIND_PERTURBED, PIVOT_KIND_2X2_FIRST, PIVOT_KIND_2X2_SECOND (0: not factored);\nstack (layout.stack_len entries): the update stack of the device path (contribution block of s at cb_ptr[s], packed lower triangle of the m×m block, m(m+1)/2 entries);\nwork (layout.work_len entries): the full m×m syrk/herk result of a regime-C front before it is packed into its block;\nstats (FRONT_STATS_FIELDS × ns Int64, column s = front s): (npos, nneg, nzero, nperturbed, n2x2, info), info = the local column of the first failed pivot of the front (0 = none);\ntotals (FRONT_STATS_FIELDS Int64): the sums of stats over the fronts (reduce_stats!; info field: number of failed fronts);\npsign (n Int8, LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ): the pivot_sign request per original row (0 = none), copied from the options at every factorization;\naux (1 entry): max |aᵢⱼ| of the values of the last LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization with pivot_epsilon_alg = \"algo1\" (scaled perturbation);\ninfo (ns + 1 Int32): device status of the numeric phase, the potrf info of front s at s, the reduced result (smallest failed factor column, 0 = none) at ns + 1;\nplan: the host NumericPlan;\nmembers (nbatch Int32): the active batch members of the last phase (first plan.nact[] entries, see BatchMap);\nnbatch: the number of matrices of the uniform batch (PLAN §3.5);\nufactor, ustack (structure \"G\" only, empty otherwise): the Uᵀ structure of LU (src/numeric/lu.jl), laid out exactly as factor and stack: the panel of s holds U[1:w, :]ᵀ (unit diagonal stored), the packed contribution block the transposed strict upper triangle of the m×m block (its diagonal stays zero). For LU, factor holds the unit-lower L, d the diagonal D of L D U and piv the local row pivot order.\n\nA uniform batch of nbatch members multiplies every length above by nbatch, in the layouts of src/numeric/batch.jl: interleaved per front for factor (panel_offset), interleaved per entry for info, member after member for the others (the regime-C workspace holds the m×m blocks of the members of one front).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Numeric"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.NumericPlan","page":"Internals","text":"NumericPlan\n\nHost launch plan of the level driver (factorize!), derived from the Schedule once at allocation: for every step t of regimes B/C, step_first[t]:step_last[t] is its range in schedule.group_nodes (empty when step_first[t] > step_last[t]) and step_maxchild[t] the largest number of children of one of its fronts (the trip count of the owner-pull extend-add). The launch groups of regimes B/C, in execution order: group k covers group_first[k]:group_last[k] of group_nodes, its fronts have at most group_maxchild[k] children, and group_width[k] is the width class W of its fused regime-B kernel (factorize_fronts_b!), or 0 when the group takes the regime-C path (regime C, or a regime-B bin wider than REGIME_B_MAX_WIDTH). The regime-A launch groups (one per budget class, run first): group k covers the subtree ids sub_first[k]:sub_last[k] of group_nodes and its kernel has sub_local[k] bytes of local memory (subtree_local_bytes of its budget). info_host is the host staging buffer of the one info read per phase (one entry per batch member). Batch state (PLAN §3.5): members_host[1:nact[]] are the active members of the last phase (mirrored in numeric.members), runs the starts of their runs of consecutive members (member_runs!, the strided batches of regime C). vendor_ptrs[s] (regime-C front s of a batch on a backend with batched vendor potrf/trsm, else nothing): the device vectors of member pointers (F11, F21) of its nbatch panels, built once at allocation (vendor_batch_pointers). Schur complement mode (PLAN §3.6): the launch group of the Schur root (schedule.schur) is not one of the B/C groups; schur_first is its position in group_nodes (0 = no Schur root) and schur_maxchild its number of children (assemble_schur!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.NumericPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Ordering","page":"Internals","text":"Ordering\n\nResult of compute_ordering:\n\nperm, iperm: the fill-reducing permutation (perm[k] is the original index of the k-th pivot, so the factorized matrix is A[perm, perm]) and its inverse;\nalg_used: :natural, :amd, :mmd, :nd or :user;\nstats::NamedTuple: nnz_L, flops, nlevels, cost of the chosen ordering, candidates::Vector{OrderingCandidate} (every ordering evaluated, the chosen one included), auto::Bool (whether the automatic choice ran) and nd_available::Bool;\npairs::Vector{Tuple{Int,Int}}: the 2×2 pivot candidate pairs (partner, candidate) the ordering was computed with (empty for no pair): perm puts each candidate right after its partner, and the symbolic factorization runs on factor_pattern(P, ordering). stats.pair_rounds is the number of zero_pivot_pairs! rounds that added pairs for the chosen ordering (0 without that pairing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Ordering"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.OrderingCandidate","page":"Internals","text":"OrderingCandidate\n\nEvaluation of one ordering by the cost model of compute_ordering: fields alg::Symbol, nnz_L::Int, flops::Float64, nlevels::Int, cost::Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.OrderingCandidate"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.PairCandidates","page":"Internals","text":"PairCandidates\n\n2×2 pivot candidates of a matrix, from pivot_candidates (host arrays):\n\ncandidate::BitVector: row i has an absent diagonal or |aᵢᵢ| ≤ τ maxⱼ≠ᵢ |aᵢⱼ|, τ = PIVOT_PAIR_TOLERANCE;\nw::Vector{R}: |aᵢⱼ| (duplicates summed first), aligned with P.rowval of the pattern P it was computed on (R = real(T));\namax::Vector{R}: maxⱼ≠ᵢ |aᵢⱼ| per row (0 for a row without off-diagonal entries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.PairCandidates"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Phase","page":"Internals","text":"Phase\n\nExecution phases with the cuDSS bit values (cudssPhase_t). Composite phases are the bitwise OR of their parts: \"analysis\" = reordering | symbolic factorization, \"solve\" = all six solve sub-phases, and the CUDSS.jl shorthands \"solve_fwd_schur\" = fwdperm | fwd and `\"solvebwdschur\"` = bwd | bwdperm. See phase_includes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Phase"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.PivotEpsilonAlg","page":"Internals","text":"PivotEpsilonAlg\n\npivot_epsilon_alg values (cuDSS cudssPivotEpsilonAlg_t): \"default\", \"algo1\" scaled perturbation, \"algo2\" static perturbation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.PivotEpsilonAlg"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.PivotPairsMode","page":"Internals","text":"PivotPairsMode\n\npivot_pairs values (beyond cuDSS), for the analysis of \"S\"/\"H\" matrices: \"default\" (2×2 pivot pairs for the candidates whose pivot is structurally zero in the ordering, see zero_pivot_pairs!), \"all\" (every candidate paired, see pivot_pairs) and \"none\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.PivotPairsMode"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.PivotType","page":"Internals","text":"PivotType\n\npivot_type values (cuDSS cudssPivotType_t): 'A' auto, 'N' none, 'C'/'R' global column/row pivoting (not supported, PLAN §3.3), 'D' diagonal, 'L' local block, 'B' Bunch–Kaufman 1×1/2×2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.PivotType"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.RefinementOperator","page":"Internals","text":"RefinementOperator(W, nzval, n, ncols, bm, conjugate, roff = 0)\n\nop(A) (the full matrix M of the RefinementWorkspace W with the values nzval, conj(M) when conjugate; roff = n: Mᵀ from the rows of a \"G\" map) acting on the n × ncols compact columns stacked into vectors of length n ncols (column r uses the values of the member of compact column r of the BatchMap bm). Supports size, eltype and mul!(y, op, x) (one launch, asynchronous): the matrix operator of FGMRES-IR.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.RefinementOperator"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.RefinementWorkspace","page":"Internals","text":"RefinementWorkspace{T, R, VI, MT, VR}\n\nDevice storage of iterative refinement, allocated at the first solve that refines (allocate_refinement) and grown with the number of right-hand sides:\n\nrowptr, colval, src (INT vectors): the full matrix M as a CSR over the contributions of the user's nzval (src[c] > 0: nzval[src[c]], src[c] < 0: conj(nzval[-src[c]])), from refinement_map; for \"G\" the rows n+1:2n of rowptr are the rows of Mᵀ;\nR (n × nrhs): the residual, then the correction;\nBc (n × nrhs): a copy of the right-hand side when X and B alias;\nnorms (2 nrhs, real): ‖Rₖ‖₂² and ‖Bₖ‖₂² per right-hand side, and norms_host, its host copy (the early-exit test of ir_tol);\nkrylov: the storage of FGMRES-IR (ir_mode = \"fgmres\"), owned by the Krylov.jl extension (nothing until its first solve).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.RefinementWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ReorderingAlg","page":"Internals","text":"ReorderingAlg\n\nreordering_alg values (cuDSS cudssReorderingAlg_t): \"default\" automatic AMD/ND choice, \"algo1\" BTF+COLAMD and \"algo2\" COLAMD (both fall back to the symmetric-pattern path), \"algo3\" AMD, \"algo4\" nested dissection, \"algo5\" natural ordering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ReorderingAlg"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Schedule","page":"Internals","text":"Schedule\n\nRegime assignment and launch order of the supernodes of a SupernodePartition for element type T (budgets are bytes):\n\nwidth[s], rows[s]: front width w and number of rows f;\nlevel[s]: height of supernode s above the leaves of the supernodal tree (leaves 1, level[parent] > level[child]), nlevels the maximum;\nregime[s] ∈ (REGIME_A, REGIME_B, REGIME_C);\nslevel[s]: schedule level of a B/C front (height counting only B/C fronts), 0 in regime A; nslevels the maximum;\nstep[s]: launch step of a B/C front (1:nsteps, one per (level, chunk)), 0 in regime A; step_level[t], step_chunk[t] describe step t;\nbin[s]: regime-B bin id ((wi - 1) * length(fclasses) + fi for width class wclasses[wi] and row class fclasses[fi]), 0 otherwise;\nregime A subtrees: subtree[s] (0 outside A), subtree_ptr/subtree_nodes (the nodes of subtree t, in processing order: a postorder whose children are visited by decreasing peak - cb, which minimizes the stack), subtree_root, subtree_peak (bytes of the serial stack of packed fronts and contribution blocks), subtree_class (index of the smallest budget of budgets whose subtree_capacity holds the peak);\ngroups and group_nodes: the launch groups in execution order (regime A classes first, then step by step: B bins in increasing bin id, then C);\nwclasses, fclasses, budgets, regime_c_width, regime_c_rows, memory_budget, elsize (bytes per packed entry, sizeof(T); 2 sizeof(T) for LU), vendor_c (whether regime C uses vendor calls; factorization_alg = \"algo1\" turns them off);\nschur: the Schur complement root (schur_mode = 1, PLAN §3.6), a regime-C front alone in the last step that the numeric phase assembles but does not factor, or 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Schedule"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ScheduleGroup","page":"Internals","text":"ScheduleGroup\n\nOne launch group of a Schedule: step (0 for regime A), schedule level and chunk (0 for regime A), regime, class (regime A: index into budgets; regime B: bin id; regime C: 0) and the range first:last of Schedule.group_nodes it covers. For regime A the range holds subtree ids, for B and C supernode ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ScheduleGroup"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ScheduleKind","page":"Internals","text":"ScheduleKind\n\nschedule values (PLAN §1.7): \"auto\", \"subtree+level\" (portable baseline), \"syncfree\" (CUDA/ROCm only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ScheduleKind"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SchurState","page":"Internals","text":"SchurState\n\nHost state of the Schur complement mode of a DirectSolver (PLAN §3.6), set by \"reordering\" when schur_mode = 1: flags (the Schur rows and columns of user_schur_indices), indices (them, increasing: row k of S is row indices[k] of A), rowptr/colval (the symbolic pattern of S, schur_pattern: 1-based CSR, both triangles, diagonal included) and dest, dest_view (the destination registered with setparam!(solver, \"schur_matrix\", …), or nothing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SchurState"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SolveAlg","page":"Internals","text":"SolveAlg\n\nsolve_alg values (cuDSS cudssSolveAlg_t, reinterpreted, PLAN §1.3): \"default\" level-batched sweeps, \"algo1\" partitioned-inverse diagonal blocks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SolveAlg"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SolvePlan","page":"Internals","text":"SolvePlan\n\nHost launch plan of the sweeps, in forward order (the backward sweep runs it in reverse): launch k has kind kind[k] (SOLVE_SUBTREES: the subtree ids group_nodes[first[k]:last[k]], all regime-A subtrees in one launch; SOLVE_FRONTS: the regime-B fronts of one step; SOLVE_DENSE: the regime-C-path fronts of one step, solved one after the other with dense calls; SOLVE_SCHUR: the unfactored Schur root of schur_mode = 1, skipped by the sweeps except for the owner-pull of its children's updates in the deterministic forward sweep). maxm is the largest m = f - w of a regime-C-path front.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SolvePlan"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SolveScaling","page":"Internals","text":"SolveScaling{VI, VR}\n\nDevice permutations and scalings of the solve with matching (matching_alg ≠ \"default\", Matching): the factored matrix is M = Dr A Dc Q (\"G\"; Q the matching column permutation) or D A D (symmetric structures), so A x = b is solved as M y = Dr b, x = Dc Q y:\n\nrperm: factor row k is original row rperm[k] (the symbolic perm);\ncperm: factor column k is original column cperm[k] (q[perm[k]] for \"G\", perm otherwise);\nrscale, cscale: the row and column scalings Dr, Dc (original numbering, real(T) vectors).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SolveScaling"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace","page":"Internals","text":"SolveWorkspace{T, MT <: AbstractMatrix{T}}\n\nDevice storage of the solve phase for up to nrhs right-hand sides, allocated once by allocate_solve:\n\nY (n × nrhs): the permuted right-hand side, overwritten by the solution;\nU (length(rowval) × nrhs): the per-front update buffers of the deterministic forward sweep (front s uses rows rowptr[s] + w : rowptr[s+1] - 1);\ntmp (maxm × nrhs): the gemm result of a regime-C-path front;\natomic: whether the backend has an atomic add for T (capabilities); without one the forward sweep is always the deterministic variant;\nplan: the host SolvePlan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Structure","page":"Internals","text":"Structure\n\nMatrix structure, \"G\", \"S\", \"H\", \"SPD\" or \"HPD\" (cuDSS cudssMatrixType_t).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Structure"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition","page":"Internals","text":"SupernodePartition\n\nSupernodes of the factor with their row structure (PLAN §2.3 steps 3–4), in the supernodal numbering: column k of the factor is original column perm[k].\n\nn, perm, iperm: size, final fill-reducing permutation (the T05 ordering composed with the supernodal renumbering) and its inverse;\nparent, counts: column elimination tree and true column counts (diagonal included) in the supernodal numbering;\nsuper_ptr: supernode s owns columns super_ptr[s]:(super_ptr[s+1]-1); col2sn[j] is the supernode of column j;\nsnparent (0 = root) and snpost, a postorder of the supernodal tree;\nrowptr, rowval: the rows of supernode s are rowval[rowptr[s]:(rowptr[s+1]-1)] (see snrows), sorted, so its own columns come first; the panel is length(rows) × width;\nnnz_stored: entries of the stored lower-trapezoidal panels, explicit zeros of amalgamation included; nnz_L: true nonzeros of L; flops: Cholesky flop count on the stored panels (Σ over panel columns of rows², real arithmetic, the cholesky_flops convention);\namalgamated: whether amalgamate ran.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, SparseDirectSolver.ColumnPartition}","page":"Internals","text":"SupernodePartition(P::SymmetricPattern, perm, parent, counts, cp::ColumnPartition; amalgamated)\n\nSupernodal symbolic factorization: the rows of every supernode of cp are its own columns plus the union of the rows of A[perm, perm] in its columns and of the rows of its children, restricted to rows after its last column. perm is the T05 ordering and parent, counts the etree and column counts in its numbering.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic","page":"Internals","text":"Symbolic{INT, VI <: AbstractVector{INT}}\n\nResult of the analysis of one sparsity pattern (PLAN §2.3, §3.2): host tree data, schedule and layout, plus the device maps as VI vectors.\n\nHost fields: n, nnz (stored entries of the user's CSR), structure, view, index, elsize (sizeof(T) the schedule was built for), partition::SupernodePartition, schedule::Schedule, layout::Layout.\n\nDevice maps (DEVICE_MAPS; supernodal numbering, 1-based, element offsets):\n\nperm, iperm (length n): the fill-reducing permutation of the factor and its inverse (column k of L is original column perm[k]);\nsuper_ptr, snparent: supernode column ranges and parents (0 = root);\nsolve gather lists rowptr, rowval: the (permuted) rows of supernode s are rowval[rowptr[s]:(rowptr[s+1]-1)], its own columns first;\nper-front descriptors: front_ptr (= layout.panel_ptr, length ns + 1), front_nrows (f), front_ncols (w), cb_ptr (update-stack offset of the contribution block, 0 if it does not go through the stack), snparent;\nchild_ptr, child_list: the children of s in the fixed order their contribution blocks are pulled (increasing id), for owner-pull extend-add;\nrelind_ptr, relind: for a child c with m = f_c - w_c > 0, relind[relind_ptr[c]:(relind_ptr[c+1]-1)] are the positions (1-based) of its rows w_c+1:f_c among the rows of its parent;\namap (length nnz): for every entry p of the user's nzval, the factor-buffer offset it is added to; 0 when the view ignores the entry, and -offset when the conjugate is added (a \"H\"/\"HPD\" entry that lands in the upper triangle of P A Pᵀ and is mirrored; for \"G\": an upper-triangle entry that goes to the Uᵀ structure). Duplicated entries map to the same offset and are summed;\namap_ptr, amap_src: the same map grouped by supernode for owner-pull assembly: supernode s receives the entries amap_src[amap_ptr[s]:(amap_ptr[s+1]-1)] (positions in nzval, sorted by destination, then by position);\nsubtree_ptr, subtree_nodes: regime-A subtree descriptors (the supernodes of subtree t in processing order, see Schedule);\nlocal_front, local_cb: local-memory offsets of the packed front and of the contribution block of a regime-A supernode (see Layout);\ngroup_ptr, group_nodes: the launch groups of the schedule (group g covers group_nodes[group_ptr[g]:(group_ptr[g+1]-1)]: subtree ids for regime A, supernode ids otherwise).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, SparseDirectSolver.Schedule, SparseDirectSolver.Layout, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Integer, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"Symbolic(sp::SupernodePartition, schedule, layout, rowptr, colval, n, structure;\n         view = 'F', index = 'O') -> Symbolic{Int, Vector{Int}}\n\nAssemble the host Symbolic from the supernodes, schedule and layout of a pattern and its user CSR arrays (only the pattern is read).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern","page":"Internals","text":"SymmetricPattern\n\nAdjacency structure of an n × n symmetric sparsity pattern on the host: colptr (length n + 1) and rowval hold, for every column j, the sorted row indices i ≠ j with (i, j) in the pattern, 1-based, without duplicates and without the diagonal, both triangles stored ((i, j) present iff (j, i) present).\n\nSymmetricPattern(n, colptr, rowval)\nSymmetricPattern(A::CSR, structure; view = 'F')\nSymmetricPattern(rowptr, colval, n, structure; view = 'F', index = 'O')\n\nThe first form wraps arrays that already satisfy the invariants (checked). The others build the pattern from a CSR pattern, following PLAN §2.3 step 1:\n\nstructure \"S\", \"H\", \"SPD\", \"HPD\" (or a Structure): only the triangle selected by view is read ('L': col ≤ row, 'U': col ≥ row; 'F' reads the lower triangle, as cuDSS does) and mirrored;\n\"G\": the pattern of A + Aᵀ; the view is ignored (every stored entry is read, as in cuDSS);\nduplicates are dropped, index ('O'/'Z') is the base of rowptr/colval, non-square matrices and out-of-range column indices raise InvalidValueError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.abs_max!","page":"Internals","text":"abs_max!(aux, nzval[, bm]) -> aux\n\naux[k] = max |nzval of member k| for the active members k of the BatchMap bm (default: a single matrix, aux[1]), one workgroup per member, @localmem tree reduction; the scale of pivot_epsilon_alg = \"algo1\". Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.abs_max!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.active_members-Tuple{Integer, Integer, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"active_members(nbatch, ubatch_index, ubatch_mask) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe batch members a phase processes (1-based, increasing): all of them for ubatch_index = -1 and no mask, member ubatch_index + 1 (ubatch_index is 0-based, as in cuDSS), or the members whose ubatch_mask flag is 1 (the mask has nbatch entries; with both set, their intersection). Raises InvalidValueError for an index or mask that does not fit the batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.active_members"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.add_correction!-Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"add_correction!(X, Y, perm; nrhs, transposed = false, conjugate = false, bm = nothing, scale = nothing) -> X\n\nX[perm[k], r] += Y[k, r] (conj(Y[k, r]) when conjugate; Y[k, r] times scale[perm[k]] first with a matching scale vector): the solution of the correction system, in factor order in the workspace Y, added to the user array X of nrhs right-hand sides (uniform batch: the compact columns of Y to the active members' columns of X, BatchMap bm). One launch, conflict-free (perm is a permutation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.add_correction!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.allocate_numeric-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{INT}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic{INT, VI} where VI<:AbstractVector{INT}, Type{T}}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic{INT, VI} where VI<:AbstractVector{INT}, Type{T}, KernelAbstractions.Backend}} where {INT, T}","page":"Internals","text":"allocate_numeric(symbolic, T, backend = CPU(); nbatch = 1) -> Numeric{T}\n\nAllocate (zero-filled) the factor panels, D, update stack, regime-C workspace, per-front statistics and their totals, status vector, pivot order, pivot kinds, pivot sign requests, aux (and for \"G\" the Uᵀ panels and update stack) of symbolic's Layout for element type T on the KernelAbstractions backend, for a uniform batch of nbatch matrices (every length times nbatch, all members active), and build its NumericPlan. This is the only allocation of the numeric phase. Raises InvalidValueError when the panels or the regime-C workspace of the batch do not fit the index type INT of symbolic, or when a regime-A kernel of the analysis needs more local memory than backend has (max_local_bytes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.allocate_numeric"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.allocate_refinement-Union{Tuple{INT}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Tuple{Vector{Int64}, Vector{Int64}, Vector{Int64}}, Type{T}, Type{INT}, KernelAbstractions.Backend, Integer}} where {T, INT}","page":"Internals","text":"allocate_refinement(map, T, INT, backend, nrhs; n = rows of map) -> RefinementWorkspace\n\nMove the host refinement_map map to backend as INT vectors (overflow-checked) and allocate the residual storage for nrhs right-hand sides of element type T and n rows (pass n for a \"G\" map, which holds 2n rows).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.allocate_refinement"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.allocate_solve-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, Type{T}}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, Type{T}, KernelAbstractions.Backend}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, Type{T}, KernelAbstractions.Backend, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"allocate_solve(symbolic, T, backend = CPU(), nrhs = 1) -> SolveWorkspace{T}\n\nAllocate (zero-filled) the solve storage of SolveWorkspace for nrhs right-hand sides of element type T on backend, build the SolvePlan and look up the atomic capability once. This is the only allocation of the solve phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.allocate_solve"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.amalgamate-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.ColumnPartition, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"amalgamate(sn::ColumnPartition, parent, counts, params) -> ColumnPartition\n\nGPU-tuned relaxed amalgamation (Ashcraft–Grimes) of the exact supernodes sn (fundamental supernodes, see fundamental_supernodes); parent and counts are in the etree numbering. params = (max_width, zero_fraction, min_width) is opts.amalgamation:\n\nthe units are the fundamental supernodes themselves. A supernode wider than max_width stays whole: it is a dense separator that goes to regime C as a single front; splitting it into a chain of max_width panels multiplied the update-stack footprint by the chain length and added tree levels (issue #48);\nbottom-up over the supernodal tree, the children c of each supernode p are tried in decreasing order of their off-diagonal row count (the order of increasing explicit zeros per column, which merging does not change) and merged into p when the merged width is ≤ max_width, the explicit zeros of the whole factor stay ≤ zero_fraction · nnz(L), and either the merged panel has at most zero_fraction explicit zeros per true nonzero or its width is still ≤ min_width (tiny panels are merged more eagerly).\n\nMerging c (width w_c, f_c rows) into p (f_p rows) gives a panel of width w_c + w_p and w_c + f_p rows, adding w_c (w_c + f_p - f_c) explicit zeros. The result is renumbered so that every merged supernode is contiguous (postorder of the merged tree, columns in their previous order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.amalgamate"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.analysis_pairs-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, Any, Any, Any, Integer, Any, Options}","page":"Internals","text":"analysis_pairs(P::SymmetricPattern, rowptr, colval, nzval, n, structure, opts; view, index)\n    -> (; pairs, candidates)\n\nThe 2×2 pivot pair input of compute_ordering for the analysis: nothing when pairs_enabled is false; pivot_candidates for pivot_pairs = \"default\" (pairs for the structurally zero pivots); the fixed pivot_pairs for \"all\". Reads nzval on the host once. Pairs are decided from the values present at \"analysis\" (the first batch member): an all-zero nzval gives no pairs and an undefined one arbitrary pairs, so run \"analysis\" after the first assembly of the matrix (MadNLP: after the first KKT assembly); \"all\" reads the values as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.analysis_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.assemble_schur!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"assemble_schur!(numeric, symbolic, nzval) -> numeric\n\nSchur complement mode (PLAN §3.6): assemble the Schur root front (symbolic.schedule.schur, run by factorize! after every other front) without factoring it: zero its panel, scatter the values nzval of A into it and extend-add its children's contribution blocks (zero_fronts!, scatter_A!, extend_add!; for LU one kernel doing the same on the L and Uᵀ structures). The panel then holds the Schur complement S (schur_matrix!). The root's factor columns get the identity local pivot order, d = 1 (unit 1×1 pivots, so diagonal_sweep! leaves the Schur block unchanged), zero statistics and status 0. A no-op without a Schur root. Single matrices only (Schur mode refuses uniform batches). Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.assemble_schur!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.assembly_map-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, SparseDirectSolver.Layout, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Integer, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"assembly_map(sp, layout, rowptr, colval, n, structure; view = 'F', index = 'O') -> amap\n\nThe amap of Symbolic: for every stored entry p of the user's CSR pattern (rowptr, colval, base index), its offset in the factor buffer of layout, -offset when its conjugate is stored instead, 0 when view ignores it. Entry (r, c) lands at (i, j) = (iperm[r], iperm[c]) of P A Pᵀ, or at (j, i) when i < j. Structure \"G\" (LU, two lower-triangular structures L and Uᵀ with the same layout, see src/numeric/lu.jl): an entry with i ≥ j goes to offset off of the L structure, an entry with i < j to the offset of (j, i) in the Uᵀ structure, encoded as -off.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.assembly_map"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.backward_sweep!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric}","page":"Internals","text":"backward_sweep!(ws, symbolic, numeric; nrhs = max_rhs(ws), impl = :auto, transpose = false) -> ws\n\nBackward sweep Lᴴ X = W (Lᵀ for complex symmetric LDLᵀ) in place on the first nrhs columns of ws.Y, the forward plan in reverse: per step the regime-C-path fronts (gather kernel, gemm with L21ᴴ, trsm with L11ᴴ) and one launch over the regime-B fronts, then one launch over all regime-A subtrees (supernodes in reverse processing order). Gather-based and conflict-free: no atomics, deterministic. Uniform batch as in forward_sweep!. LU (\"G\"): U X = W, or Lᵀ X = W followed by the inverse local row order with transpose = true. Schur complement mode: the rows of the Schur root hold its solution and are only read. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.backward_sweep!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.batch_map-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric}","page":"Internals","text":"batch_map(numeric; first = 1, nrhs = 1) -> BatchMap\n\nThe BatchMap of the active members of numeric for a launch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.batch_map"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.build_layout-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, SparseDirectSolver.Schedule}","page":"Internals","text":"build_layout(sp::SupernodePartition, schedule::Schedule; ldlt = false) -> Layout\n\nPanel offsets in supernode order, D offsets, and the update-stack offsets of the contribution blocks that leave their front through global memory (B/C fronts and regime-A subtree roots that have a parent; packed lower triangles). Blocks whose lifetimes [step(s), step(parent)] overlap never share entries; the offsets are the placement with the lowest high-water mark among a step-by-step first fit and two offline placements (lowest free offset, largest blocks first and largest size × lifetime first; issue #48). Also the regime-C workspace (with ldlt, structures \"S\"/\"H\", the larger one of the blocked LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ path, ldlt_c_work_len) and the local-memory offsets of the regime-A subtrees (subtree_local_layout).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.build_layout"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.build_schedule-Union{Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, Options}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, Options, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"build_schedule(sp::SupernodePartition, opts::Options = Options(), ::Type{T} = Float64;\n               elsize = sizeof(T), reserve = SUBTREE_LOCAL_RESERVE, max_local = typemax(Int)) -> Schedule\n\nRegime assignment, binning, levels and launch groups (PLAN §2.3 step 5) for the supernodes of sp, with byte budgets for the element type T (elsize bytes per packed entry: 2 sizeof(T) for LU, whose fronts and contribution blocks are two packed triangles, see src/numeric/lu.jl):\n\nregime C: fronts with w > opts.regime_c_width or f > opts.regime_c_rows (every non-A front with factorization_alg = \"algo2\");\nregime A: a front is eligible when it is not C, its children are eligible and its subtree's serial stack peak (packed lower-triangular f×f fronts plus the packed m×m contribution blocks waiting for their parent, m = f - w) fits the subtree_capacity of the largest of opts.subtree_budgets (local memory minus reserve bytes, subtree_local_reserve of the structure; the budgets are those of resolve_subtree_budgets for the backend's max_local bytes of local memory, max_local_bytes), and its subtree's flops (front_flops) are at most total ÷ opts.subtree_parallelism (0: no flop limit), so that a large tree of small fronts is split into enough subtrees to fill the device instead of running on one workgroup; the maximal eligible subtrees are the regime-A subtrees, each with the smallest budget class that holds it (empty subtree_budgets disables regime A);\nregime B: the rest, binned by the smallest width class in 8, 16, 32, 64, … ≥ w and row class in 64, 128, 256, 512, … ≥ f (up to the regime-C thresholds);\nschedule levels of the B/C fronts, split into chunks whose produced (packed) contribution-block bytes stay ≤ opts.memory_budget (a single front larger than the budget gets its own chunk; a negative budget means no chunking).\n\nschur (a root supernode, 0 = none): the Schur complement root of schur_mode = 1 (PLAN §3.6), forced into regime C and onto a schedule level of its own above every other front, so that it is the only front of the last launch group (assembled, never factored, see assemble_schur!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.build_schedule"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.capabilities-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"capabilities(backend, T) -> DenseCapabilities\n\nDense-layer capabilities of backend (a KernelAbstractions backend) for element type T, probed on first use with small try/catch calls and cached per (backend, T). A probe counts as available only if it neither throws nor returns a wrong result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.capabilities"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.cholesky_flops-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"cholesky_flops(counts) -> Float64\n\nFloating-point operation count of the Cholesky factorization with column counts counts (diagonal included), Σⱼ cⱼ² as reported by CHOLMOD (one division and cⱼ - 1 multiply–adds per entry of the update, real arithmetic). Multiply by 4 for complex element types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.cholesky_flops"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.cholesky_stats!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic}","page":"Internals","text":"cholesky_stats!(numeric, symbolic) -> numeric\n\nFill the per-front statistics of a Cholesky factorization from the per-front potrf status numeric.info[1:ns] (npos = w, or the failed local column minus one; info = the failed local column; the unfactored Schur root counts nothing) and reduce the smallest failed factor column into numeric.info[ns + 1] (0 = none), for every active batch member. One workgroup per member, no atomics. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.cholesky_stats!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.colcounts-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"colcounts(P::SymmetricPattern, perm, parent, post) -> counts::Vector{Int}\n\nNumber of nonzeros in each column of the Cholesky factor L of A[perm, perm] (diagonal included), by the Gilbert–Ng–Peyton algorithm in O(nnz(A) α). parent comes from etree and post from postorder.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.colcounts"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.compressed_pattern-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"compressed_pattern(P::SymmetricPattern, pairs) -> (Pc::SymmetricPattern, ptr, members)\n\nThe pattern compressed by the 2×2 pairs (partner, candidate): a pair is one vertex whose adjacency is the union of both rows (self-loops removed), every other column is one vertex. Compressed vertex g stands for the columns members[ptr[g]:(ptr[g+1]-1)] (partner first); vertices are numbered by increasing first column (the partner for a pair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.compressed_pattern"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.compute_matching-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Integer, Any, Options}","page":"Internals","text":"compute_matching(rowptr, colval, nzval, n, structure, opts::Options; view = 'F', index = 'O')\n    -> Union{Nothing, Matching}\n\nThe Matching of the analysis for opts.matching_alg (nothing for \"default\"), from the CSR matrix (rowptr, colval, nzval; the first batch member when nzval is a matrix) read with the view and index rules of SymmetricPattern:\n\n\"G\": mc64 on the stored rows (rscale, cscale from job 5);\nthe symmetric structures: mc64 on the full matrix (both triangles), then the symmetric scaling dᵢ = sqrt(rᵢ cᵢ) of Duff & Pralet (2005), so |dᵢ aᵢⱼ dⱼ| ≤ 1 (rscale = cscale = d). An index left unmatched by a structurally singular matrix gets dᵢ = 1 / maxⱼ |aᵢⱼ| dⱼ over its matched neighbours (1 without one).\n\nReads nzval on the host once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.compute_matching"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.compute_ordering-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, Options}","page":"Internals","text":"compute_ordering(P::SymmetricPattern, opts::Options; T = Float64, alg = nothing, pairs = [], candidates = nothing)\n    -> Ordering\n\nFill-reducing ordering of the pattern P (PLAN §2.3 step 2):\n\nopts.user_perm set: that permutation, validated, accepted 0- or 1-based and returned 1-based (alg_used = :user);\nreordering_alg = \"algo5\": natural ordering; \"algo3\": AMD (CliqueTrees.AMD()); \"algo4\": nested dissection with Metis (needs using Metis, otherwise NotSupportedError): METIS_NodeND with ufactor = nd_ubfactor (-1: METIS default); nd_nlevels is the cuDSS minimum number of dissection levels, which METIS' full recursion meets on every graph large enough to be split that often; \"algo1\"/\"algo2\": AMD on the symmetric pattern;\n\"default\": AMD and, if Metis is loaded, ND are both evaluated with evaluate_ordering and the one with the lower flops × (1 + nlevels / n) is chosen; stats.candidates lists both.\n\nThe keyword alg (:natural, :amd, :mmd, :nd, :auto) overrides reordering_alg (MMD has no cuDSS spelling); T scales the flop counts in stats (complex: 4×) and does not change the choice.\n\n2×2 pivot pairs (\"S\"/\"H\", issues #64 and #66), from analysis_pairs in the analysis when pairs_enabled:\n\npairs: disjoint (partner, candidate) pairs fixed in advance (pivot_pairs = \"all\", pivot_pairs);\ncandidates: a PairCandidates (pivot_pairs = \"default\"): for every ordering algorithm evaluated, the plain ordering is computed, the candidates whose pivot is structurally zero in it are paired (zero_pivot_pairs!, partners accepted down to opts.pivot_threshold of the row maximum) and the graph compressed by the pairs is re-ordered, until a round adds no pair (at most PIVOT_PAIRS_MAX_ROUNDS). Each algorithm keeps its own pairs.\n\nBoth come from the values of the matrix at analysis: an all-zero nzval gives no pairs and an undefined one arbitrary pairs, so the analysis must run after the first assembly of the values (MadNLP: after the first KKT assembly), for \"default\" and \"all\" alike.\n\nAMD, MMD and ND then order the graph compressed by the pairs (compressed_pattern: a pair is one vertex with the union adjacency), and the order is expanded with each partner right before its candidate (expand_permutation). Every ordering is evaluated, and the factor is built, on pair_pattern, where both columns of a pair have the union structure, so the pair lies in one fundamental supernode (never split by amalgamate) and in-front Bunch–Kaufman can take it as a 2×2 pivot. With user_perm (and with the natural ordering) the pairs are not applied, the user owns the order: ordering.pairs is empty. Without pairs (no candidate, or no structurally zero pivot) the result is the one of the plain ordering, bitwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.compute_ordering"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.compute_schur_ordering-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, Options, AbstractVector{Bool}}","page":"Internals","text":"compute_schur_ordering(P::SymmetricPattern, opts::Options, schur::AbstractVector{Bool}; T = Float64) -> Ordering\n\nSchur-constrained ordering (PLAN §3.6): the rows and columns flagged in schur (schur_flags) are ordered last, in increasing original order, and the others first. The fill-reducing ordering of compute_ordering (reordering_alg) runs on the pattern induced by the other vertices (induced_pattern); since the Schur vertices come last they are never intermediate vertices of a fill path, so this is the ordering of the factored part. A user_perm keeps its relative order of the other vertices. The 2×2 pivot pairs of \"S\"/\"H\" are not used (pairs empty). stats describe the whole ordering (the Schur block included).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.compute_schur_ordering"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.copy_rhs!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVecOrMat}","page":"Internals","text":"copy_rhs!(C, B; nrhs, transposed = false) -> C\n\nC[:, 1:nrhs] = the right-hand sides of the user array B (column-major copy; the refinement keeps B when the solution overwrites it). One launch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.copy_rhs!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.dense_impls-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Symbol, Any, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"dense_impls(op::Symbol, backend, T) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nThe implementations of dense op op (:gemm, :syrk, :herk, :trsm, :potrf, :getrf, :laswp, :gemm_strided_batched, :trsm_strided_batched, :potrf_batched) available for element type T on backend according to capabilities, in :auto preference order (:vendor, :generic, :ka). :ka is always present.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.dense_impls"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.device_map_bytes-Union{Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic{INT, VI} where VI<:AbstractVector{INT}}, Tuple{J}, Tuple{INT}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic{INT, VI} where VI<:AbstractVector{INT}, Type{J}}} where {INT, J}","page":"Internals","text":"device_map_bytes(symbolic, INT = eltype of its maps) -> Int64\n\nBytes of the device maps (DEVICE_MAPS) as INT vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.device_map_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.diagonal_sweep!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric}","page":"Internals","text":"diagonal_sweep!(ws, symbolic, numeric; nrhs = max_rhs(ws)) -> ws\n\nDiagonal step D W = Z between the sweeps, in place on the first nrhs columns of ws.Y: the identity for Cholesky (\"SPD\"/\"HPD\"); for LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ one launch, one work item per (factor column, right-hand side), dividing by the 1×1 pivots of numeric.d and solving the 2×2 blocks (d[k], d[n + k], d[k + 1]; Hermitian or complex symmetric as the structure; LU: the pivots of L D U, transpose is accepted for symmetry). Uniform batch: nrhs per active member, each with its member's D. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.diagonal_sweep!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.entry_scaling-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Matching, Any, Any, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"entry_scaling(m::Matching, rowptr, colval, n; index = 'O') -> Vector{Float64}\n\nThe factor rᵢ cⱼ of every stored entry (i, j) of the CSR pattern (original column numbering), aligned with colval: the factored values are rᵢ aᵢⱼ cⱼ (scale_values!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.entry_scaling"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.enum_spellings","page":"Internals","text":"enum_spellings(E) -> Tuple{Vararg{Pair}}\n\nThe CUDSS.jl spellings of every instance of the enum type E, as spelling => instance pairs. Spellings are Strings, except for MatrixView, IndexBase and PivotType, which use Chars.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.enum_spellings"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.etree-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"etree(P::SymmetricPattern, perm) -> parent::Vector{Int}\n\nElimination tree of A[perm, perm] for the symmetric pattern P (Liu's algorithm with path compression). parent[k] is the parent of column k of the permuted matrix, 0 for a root; parent[k] > k for every non-root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.etree"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.evaluate_ordering-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"evaluate_ordering(P::SymmetricPattern, perm; T = Float64) -> (; nnz_L, flops, nlevels, cost)\n\nElimination tree, column counts and tree height of A[perm, perm], condensed into the quantities of the cost model (ordering_cost). flops counts real operations (4× for complex T).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.evaluate_ordering"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.expand_permutation-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"expand_permutation(cperm, ptr, members) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe ordering of the original columns given by an ordering cperm of the compressed vertices of compressed_pattern: the columns of each vertex in turn, partner before candidate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.expand_permutation"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.extend_add!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, Integer, Integer, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"extend_add!(numeric, symbolic, first, count, maxchild) -> numeric\n\nOwner-pull extend-add: the workgroup of each front s of symbolic.group_nodes[first:(first + count - 1)] adds its children's packed contribution blocks (update stack, cb_ptr) to its panel and its own packed contribution block through relind, children in child_list order (maxchild ≥ the largest child count of these fronts). No atomics; deterministic. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.extend_add!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.extract_L-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"extract_L(symbolic, numeric) -> SparseMatrixCSC\n\nThe Cholesky factor L of P A Pᵀ = L Lᴴ (P = symbolic.partition.perm, supernodal numbering) as a sparse lower-triangular matrix with every stored panel entry, explicit zeros of amalgamation included (nnz == nnz_stored). A device numeric.factor is copied to the host once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.extract_L"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.extract_ldlt-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"extract_ldlt(symbolic, numeric) -> (L, D, p)\n\nThe factors of an LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization (ref_ldlt! or a device numeric, copied to the host once) as sparse matrices with A[p, p] ≈ L D Lᴴ (Lᵀ for complex symmetric \"S\"): p is the analysis permutation composed with the local pivot orders, L unit lower triangular with every stored panel entry, D block diagonal with 1×1 and 2×2 blocks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.extract_ldlt"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.extract_lu-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"extract_lu(symbolic, numeric) -> (L, D, U, p, q)\n\nThe LU factor in numeric (host or device, batch member 1) as host sparse matrices in factor order: L unit lower and U unit upper triangular, D the pivots, p and q the row and column permutations (original indices, p[k] = perm[piv[k]], q = perm), so that A[p, q] ≈ L * Diagonal(D) * U (with the perturbed pivots, A + E). Rows of L below a diagonal block are the rows of later supernodes, which their own local row interchanges moved to ipiv[row].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.extract_lu"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.factor_pattern-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, SparseDirectSolver.Ordering}","page":"Internals","text":"factor_pattern(P::SymmetricPattern, ord::Ordering) -> SymmetricPattern\n\nThe pattern of the symbolic factorization for the ordering ord: pair_pattern(P, ord.pairs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.factor_pattern"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.factorize_cholesky!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"factorize_cholesky!(numeric, symbolic, nzval; impl = :auto, opts = Options()) -> info::Int\n\nThe Cholesky path of factorize! (structures \"SPD\" and \"HPD\"), callable without compiling the LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ path (factorize_ldlt!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.factorize_cholesky!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.factorize_fronts_b!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector, Vararg{Integer, 4}}","page":"Internals","text":"factorize_fronts_b!(numeric, symbolic, nzval, first, count, maxchild, W) -> numeric\n\nRegime-B launch: assemble and factor the count fronts symbolic.group_nodes[first:(first + count - 1)] (widths ≤ W, W ∈ (8, 16, 32, 64), at most maxchild children each) of every active batch member with one front_cholesky_kernel! launch, one workgroup per (front, member); info[s] receives each front's status. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.factorize_fronts_b!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.factorize_ldlt!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"factorize_ldlt!(numeric, symbolic, nzval; impl = :auto, opts = Options(), nb = LDLT_C_NB) -> info::Int\n\nMultifrontal P A Pᵀ = L D Lᴴ (structure \"H\", or \"S\" with real T) or L D Lᵀ (complex symmetric \"S\") on the device, with the in-front pivoting and the static perturbation of the reference ref_ldlt! (same pivot choice, same storage): opts.pivot_type, pivot_threshold, pivot_epsilon, pivot_epsilon_alg and pivot_sign (copied to numeric.psign; a length other than n raises InvalidValueError). Regime-A groups are one subtree_ldlt_kernel! launch per budget class; a regime-B launch group is one front_ldlt_kernel! launch (fused assembly and factorization, one workgroup per front, F₁₁ in local memory above the narrow width classes); regime-C groups and the wide regime-B bins of ldlt_blocked_path take the blocked path of src/numeric/ldlt_c.jl (panel_ldlt_kernel! per block of nb pivot columns, then GEMMs through the dense interface: impl = :auto means vendor GEMMs when the analysis chose vendor calls for regime C, else :ka; other values as in select_impl; nb < LDLT_C_NB is for tests). Then reduce_stats!. opts.user_host_interrupt is polled before every launch group (InterruptedError). With pivot_epsilon_alg = \"algo1\" abs_max! computes the scale first. Fills numeric.factor (unit-lower panels), d, piv, pivot_kind, stats and totals.\n\nReturns info: an exactly zero 1×1 pivot is always perturbed, and with an effective ε = 0 it stays zero; the factorization then fails there (the following divisions are left to IEEE arithmetic) and info is the original column of the failed pivot with the smallest factor column, as for ref_ldlt!. With ε > 0 the factorization always completes (info = 0). The phase allocates nothing on the device and is deterministic; it synchronizes with the host only to read info when the factorization can fail (pivot_epsilon = 0, or pivot_epsilon_alg = \"algo1\"), otherwise never.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.factorize_ldlt!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.factorize_lu!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"factorize_lu!(numeric, symbolic, nzval; opts = Options()) -> info::Int\n\nMultifrontal P_r P A Pᵀ = L D U of a general matrix (structure \"G\") on the device, with the in-front row pivoting and the static perturbation of the reference ref_lu! (same pivot choice, same storage): opts.pivot_type (lu_pivoting), pivot_threshold, pivot_epsilon and pivot_epsilon_alg. Regime-A groups are one subtree_lu_kernel! launch per budget class; every regime-B and regime-C launch group is one front_lu_kernel! launch (fused assembly and factorization, one workgroup per front; no vendor calls); then reduce_stats!. opts.user_host_interrupt is polled before every launch group (InterruptedError). With pivot_epsilon_alg = \"algo1\" abs_max! computes the scale first. Fills numeric.factor (L), numeric.ufactor (Uᵀ), d, piv (local row order), pivot_kind, stats and totals. Returns info as factorize_ldlt!: an exactly zero pivot stays zero with an effective ε = 0 and the factorization fails there (info = the original column, as ref_lu!); with ε > 0 it always completes (info = 0). The phase allocates nothing on the device, is deterministic, and synchronizes with the host only to read info when the factorization can fail (pivot_epsilon = 0, or pivot_epsilon_alg = \"algo1\"). Uniform batch as factorize!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.factorize_lu!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.factorize_subtrees!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector, Integer, Integer, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"factorize_subtrees!(numeric, symbolic, nzval, first, count, local_bytes) -> numeric\n\nRegime-A launch: assemble and factor the count subtrees symbolic.group_nodes[first:(first + count - 1)] (subtree ids of one budget class) of every active batch member with one subtree_cholesky_kernel! launch, one workgroup per (subtree, member), local_bytes (subtree_local_bytes of the class) of local memory per workgroup. Panels and statuses of every supernode of the subtrees are written, and each subtree root's contribution block goes to the update stack. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.factorize_subtrees!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.fgmres_available-Tuple{}","page":"Internals","text":"fgmres_available() -> Bool\n\nWhether the Krylov.jl extension is loaded (ir_mode = \"fgmres\" works).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.fgmres_available"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.fgmres_refine!-Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, AbstractVecOrMat, SparseDirectSolver.RefinementWorkspace, SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"fgmres_refine!(X, B, W, ws, symbolic, numeric, nzval; nsteps, tol = 0, transposed = false,\n               b_transposed = transposed, conjugate = false, deterministic = false,\n               interrupt = nothing, progress = Ref(0), transpose_matrix = false, scaling = nothing)\n    -> iterations\n\nFGMRES-IR (ir_mode = \"fgmres\", needs Krylov.jl) of the solution X of op(A) X = B, with the arguments of refine!:\n\nR₀ = B - op(A) X                         (residual!)\nstop if tol > 0 and maxₖ ‖R₀ₖ‖₂ / ‖Bₖ‖₂ ≤ tol\nD = FGMRES(op(A), R₀; right preconditioner op(A)⁻¹ by the factor,\n           at most nsteps iterations, ‖R₀ - op(A) D‖₂ ≤ tol minₖ ‖Bₖ‖₂)\nX += D                                   (add_correction!)\n\non the stacked compact columns (RefinementOperator, FactorPreconditioner). Each iteration costs one SpMV and one solve, as a step of plain refinement. tol = 0 runs nsteps iterations (FGMRES stops earlier only on an exact residual). The joint criterion bounds every column's relative residual by tol. Returns the iterations performed (also in progress[]); an interrupt (polled before every preconditioner application) leaves X unchanged and progress[] = 0. FGMRES synchronizes with the host every iteration (Krylov.jl's dot products and norms).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.fgmres_refine!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.finish_ldlt_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"finish_ldlt_kernel!(backend, WG)(factor, d, pivot_kind, info, nodes, qa, super_ptr, front_ptr, k, nbatch,\n                                 front_nrows, front_ncols, Val(HERM), Val(WG); ndrange = WG * count)\n\nAfter the blocks of the regime-C fronts nodes[qa:(qa + count - 1)], one workgroup each: scale the pivot columns into L (_lt_finalize!), clear the upper triangle of F₁₁, set the unit diagonal and the front's status.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.finish_ldlt_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.forward_sweep!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric}","page":"Internals","text":"forward_sweep!(ws, symbolic, numeric; nrhs = max_rhs(ws), deterministic = false, impl = :auto,\n               transpose = false) -> ws\n\nForward sweep L Z = Y in place on the first nrhs columns of ws.Y (the permuted right-hand side, permute_rhs!), with the factor of factorize! in numeric and the device maps of symbolic: one launch over all regime-A subtrees, then step by step one launch over the regime-B fronts and, for each regime-C-path front, trsm + gemm through the dense interface (impl as in factorize!) and a scatter kernel. The atomic variant runs unless deterministic is set or the backend lacks an atomic add for T (complex T, issue #36); the deterministic variant goes through the per-front buffers ws.U and an owner-pull of the children (one extra launch per regime-C step). Uniform batch: nrhs right-hand sides per active member of numeric (nrhs × nact columns of ws.Y, each solved with its member's factor; regime-C-path fronts use strided-batched trsm/gemm over runs of consecutive members). LU (\"G\"): L Z = Y with the local row order, or Uᵀ Z = Y with transpose = true (the solve of Aᵀ). Schur complement mode (schedule.schur > 0): the Schur root is not solved, its rows end as the condensed right-hand side Y₂ − L₂₁ Z₁. Asynchronous; allocates nothing on the device.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.forward_sweep!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.front_cholesky!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector{Int32}, AbstractVector, Integer, Integer, Vararg{AbstractVector, 4}}","page":"Internals","text":"front_cholesky!(factor, stack, info, nodes, first, count, front_ptr, front_nrows, front_ncols, cb_ptr;\n                width = 64, workgroup = Val(FRONT_WORKGROUP)) -> info\n\nDense part of the regime-B kernel on already assembled fronts (benchmarks and tests): for each front s = nodes[first + k - 1], k = 1:count, with panel factor[front_ptr[s]:(front_ptr[s + 1] - 1)] (f×w, f = front_nrows[s], w = front_ncols[s] ≤ width, width ∈ (8, 16, 32, 64)) and contribution block stack[cb_ptr[s]:(cb_ptr[s] + m(m+1)/2 - 1)] (m = f - w, packed lower triangle, skipped when cb_ptr[s] == 0): F11 = L11 L11ᴴ, F21 ← F21 L11⁻ᴴ, F22 ← F22 − F21 F21ᴴ, info[s] = status. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.front_cholesky!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.front_cholesky_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"front_cholesky_kernel!(backend, WG)(factor, stack, info, nzval, amap, amap_ptr, amap_src, nodes, bm,\n                                    front_ptr, front_nrows, front_ncols, cb_ptr, child_ptr, child_list,\n                                    relind_ptr, relind, maxchild, Val(ASM), Val(W), Val(NL), Val(WG);\n                                    ndrange = WG * count * bm.nact)\n\nFused regime-B kernel: workgroup G takes front s = nodes[bm.first + g - 1] (width w ≤ W) of batch member k (g, k from the BatchMap bm), and, when ASM, zeroes and assembles it (A through the amap, then the maxchild children's contribution blocks in child_list order), then factors F11 = L11 L11ᴴ in @localmem (NL = W(W+1)/2 entries), solves F21 ← F21 L11⁻ᴴ, updates its contribution block on the update stack (cb_ptr[s] > 0, packed lower triangle) and sets info[s] (0, or the local column of the first non-positive pivot; the front is then left partially factored). Without ASM the panels and contribution blocks must already be assembled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.front_cholesky_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.front_flops-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"front_flops(f, w) -> Int64\n\nMultiply-add count of the partial dense factorization of an f×f front with w pivot columns: Σ_{j=f-w+1}^{f} j² (the elimination of column k updates the trailing (f-k)×(f-k) block). The work measure of the regime-A split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.front_flops"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.front_ldlt_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"front_ldlt_kernel!(backend, WG)(factor, stack, info, stats, d, piv, pivot_kind, psign, perm, aux, nzval, amap,\n                                amap_ptr, amap_src, nodes, bm, super_ptr, front_ptr, front_nrows,\n                                front_ncols, cb_ptr, child_ptr, child_list, relind_ptr, relind, maxchild, prm,\n                                Val(W), Val(WG); ndrange = WG * count * bm.nact)\n\nLDLᵀ/LDLᴴ kernel of regime B: workgroup G takes front s = nodes[bm.first + g - 1] of batch member k (g, k from the BatchMap bm; the per-member arrays are the member's), zeroes and assembles it (A through the amap, then the maxchild children's packed contribution blocks in child_list order), factors its w fully-summed columns in place with in-block Bunch–Kaufman pivoting (W = 0, the narrow width classes ≤ _LT_GLOBAL_MAX_W: in the panel in global memory; width class W ≥ w above that: F₁₁ staged as a packed W×W lower triangle in @localmem, the rows below it in the panel; the pivot search is a workgroup reduction), threshold acceptance and perturbation (prm, a _LDLTDevice{R, HERM}; HERM: Hermitian or real symmetric, else complex symmetric), writes D, the local pivot order piv, the pivot kinds and the front's statistics, updates its packed contribution block on the update stack (cb_ptr[s] > 0) with F₂₂ − (L₂₁ D) L₂₁ᴴ and leaves a unit-lower panel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.front_ldlt_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.front_lu_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"front_lu_kernel!(backend, WG)(factor, ufactor, stack, ustack, info, stats, d, piv, pivot_kind, aux, nzval, amap,\n                              amap_ptr, amap_src, nodes, bm, super_ptr, front_ptr, front_nrows, front_ncols,\n                              cb_ptr, child_ptr, child_list, relind_ptr, relind, maxchild, prm, Val(WG);\n                              ndrange = WG * count * bm.nact)\n\nLU kernel of regimes B and C: workgroup G takes front s = nodes[bm.first + g - 1] of batch member k (g, k from the BatchMap bm; the per-member arrays are the member's), zeroes and assembles its L and Uᵀ structures (A through the amap, then the maxchild children's two packed contribution blocks in child_list order), factors its w fully-summed columns in place in the two panels with in-block row pivoting, threshold test and perturbation (prm, an _LUDevice), writes D, the local row order piv, the pivot kinds and the front's statistics, updates its two packed contribution blocks (cb_ptr[s] > 0) with F₂₂ − (L₂₁ D) U₁₂ and leaves unit-lower panels of L and Uᵀ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.front_lu_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.full_pattern_map-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Integer, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"full_pattern_map(A::CSR, structure; view = 'F') -> FullPatternMap\nfull_pattern_map(rowptr, colval, n, structure; view = 'F', index = 'O') -> FullPatternMap\n\nBuild the FullPatternMap of a CSR input with the same structure and view rules as SymmetricPattern (for \"G\" the matrix is taken as is). Duplicated user entries are summed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.full_pattern_map"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.full_values-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.FullPatternMap, AbstractVector{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"full_values(F::FullPatternMap, nzval::AbstractVector) -> Vector\n\nHost evaluation of the map: the values of the full matrix M in the order of F.colval (see FullPatternMap).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.full_values"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.fundamental_supernodes-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"fundamental_supernodes(parent, post, counts) -> ColumnPartition\n\nFundamental supernodes of the elimination tree parent with column counts counts (both in the etree numbering, from etree and colcounts) and its postorder post (postorder). The columns are renumbered by post (order == post); column k + 1 joins the supernode of column k when k is its only child and counts[k] == counts[k+1] + 1, so the row structure of every supernode is exactly that of its first column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.fundamental_supernodes"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.gemm!","page":"Internals","text":"gemm!(C, A, B, α = 1, β = 0; transA = :N, transB = :N, impl = :auto) -> C\n\nC ← α op(A) op(B) + β C with op from :N, :T, :C.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.gemm!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.gemm_strided_batched!","page":"Internals","text":"gemm_strided_batched!(C, A, B, α = 1, β = 0; transA = :N, transB = :N, impl = :auto) -> C\n\nUniform strided batch of gemm!: C[:, :, i] ← α op(A[:, :, i]) op(B[:, :, i]) + β C[:, :, i] for every member i of the 3-D arrays (see strided_batch). Implementations: :vendor, :ka.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.gemm_strided_batched!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.getrf!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"getrf!(A, ipiv; impl = :auto) -> info::Int\n\nLU factorization with partial pivoting of the m × n matrix A in place, P A = L U (unit L), LAPACK getrf: ipiv[1:min(m, n)] receives the row interchanges (1-based, see laswp!); info is 0 or the first column with an exactly zero pivot. ipiv lives on the backend of A. Reading info synchronizes with the device.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.getrf!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.herk!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, Real, Real}","page":"Internals","text":"herk!(C, A, α, β; uplo = :L, impl = :auto) -> C\n\nHermitian rank-k update of the uplo triangle of C: C ← α A Aᴴ + β C with real α, β (BLAS herk; the diagonal of the result is real). For real T this is syrk!. Triangle semantics as in syrk!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.herk!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.host_numeric-Union{Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, Vector{T}, Vector{Int64}, Vector{Int32}, Vector{Int8}}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"host_numeric(numeric) -> Numeric{T, Vector{T}}\n\nHost copy of numeric (panels, D, update stack, workspace, statistics and totals, status, pivot order, kinds and sign requests, aux, the Uᵀ panels and stack of LU; the plan is shared), usable by ref_solve! and extract_L. A host numeric is returned as is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.host_numeric"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.induced_pattern-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"induced_pattern(P::SymmetricPattern, vertices) -> SymmetricPattern\n\nThe pattern of P restricted to vertices (sorted, distinct), renumbered 1:length(vertices) in their order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.induced_pattern"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.inertia-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric}","page":"Internals","text":"inertia(numeric) -> (npos, nneg)\n\nInertia of D of an LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization (PLAN §1.4 inertia). With perturbed pivots this is the inertia of A + E; read it together with npivots.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.inertia"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_chol_check_info!-Tuple{AbstractVector{Int32}, Integer, AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_chol_check_info!(info, offset, L::AbstractArray{T,3}) -> info\n\nBatched form: the check of ka_chol_check_info!(info, offset + b, L[:, :, b]) for every member b of the 3-D strided batch L, one launch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_chol_check_info!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_chol_check_info!-Tuple{AbstractVector{Int32}, Integer, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_chol_check_info!(info, idx, L) -> info\n\nDevice-side validation of the info[idx] a vendor or generic Cholesky left for the computed factor L (a matrix): when info[idx] == 0, it is replaced by the first j whose diagonal entry L[j, j] is not a finite positive real (still 0 if there is none); a nonzero info[idx] is kept. Asynchronous (see potrf_info!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_chol_check_info!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_chol_diag_info!-Tuple{AbstractVector{Int32}, AbstractArray}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_chol_diag_info!(info, L) -> info\n\nPivot check of a computed Cholesky factor L (matrix or 3-D strided batch): info[b] is set to the first j whose diagonal entry L[j, j] is not a finite positive real, 0 if there is none. Used to validate the info = 0 of vendor and generic factorizations (see potrf!). Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_chol_diag_info!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_gemm!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_gemm!(C, A, B, α, β; transA = 'N', transB = 'N', tile = Val(16) or Val(32)) -> C\n\nKernelAbstractions fallback for C ← α op(A) op(B) + β C (op from 'N', 'T', 'C'). Tiled, one TILE × TILE tile of C per 1-D workgroup of TILE² work items; tile defaults to Val(32) when both dimensions of C are at least 64. C is not read when β == 0. Asynchronous: no host synchronization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_gemm!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_gemm_strided_batched!-Tuple{AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_gemm_strided_batched!(C, A, B, α, β; transA = 'N', transB = 'N', tile) -> C\n\nStrided batched ka_gemm!: A, B, C are 3-D arrays (for example strided_batch views of a flat buffer) whose third dimension is the batch; one launch, the batch index is the second ndrange dimension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_gemm_strided_batched!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_getrf!-Union{Tuple{WG}, Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractVecOrMat{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{Int32}}} where WG","page":"Internals","text":"ka_getrf!(A, ipiv, info; workgroup = Val(128)) -> info\n\nKernelAbstractions fallback LU with partial pivoting of the m × n matrix A in place (P A = L U, unit L below the diagonal, U on and above), one 1-D workgroup per matrix. A is a matrix (ipiv a vector of length min(m, n)) or a 3-D strided batch (ipiv a min(m, n) × count matrix). info is a device Int32 vector with one entry per matrix: 0, or the first column with an exactly zero pivot, as LAPACK getrf. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_getrf!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_laswp!-Tuple{AbstractArray, AbstractVecOrMat{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_laswp!(A, ipiv; npiv = size(ipiv, 1), reverse = false, workgroup = 128) -> A\n\nKernelAbstractions row interchanges: for i = 1:npiv (in reverse order if reverse), swap rows i and ipiv[i] of A, as LAPACK laswp with k1 = 1, k2 = npiv; entries of ipiv beyond npiv are not read. Applied after ka_getrf! this computes P A; reverse = true applies Pᵀ. One work item per column; A may be a 3-D strided batch with ipiv a matrix (one column per member).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_laswp!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_potrf!-Union{Tuple{WG}, Tuple{Any, AbstractArray, AbstractVector{Int32}}} where WG","page":"Internals","text":"ka_potrf!(uplo, A, info; workgroup = Val(128), offset = 0) -> info\n\nKernelAbstractions fallback Cholesky of the uplo triangle of A in place (A = LLᴴ for 'L', UᴴU for 'U'), unblocked, one 1-D workgroup per matrix. A is a matrix or a 3-D strided batch; info is a device vector of Int32 with one entry per matrix, set to 0 on success or to the first column j (1-based) whose pivot is not positive, as LAPACK potrf; matrix b of the batch writes info[offset + b]. Asynchronous: the caller reads info at a phase boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_potrf!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_syrk!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_syrk!(C, A, α, β; uplo = 'L', conjugate = false, tile) -> C\n\nKernelAbstractions fallback for the uplo triangle of C ← α A Aᵀ + β C (conjugate = true: α A Aᴴ + β C with real α, β and a real diagonal, as BLAS herk). The other triangle is not touched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_syrk!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_trsm!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_trsm!(side, uplo, trans, diag, α, A, B; workgroup = Val(128)) -> B\n\nKernelAbstractions fallback for BLAS trsm: B ← α op(A)⁻¹ B (side = 'L') or B ← α B op(A)⁻¹ (side = 'R'), A triangular (uplo), unit diagonal if diag = 'U', op from trans ∈ ('N', 'T', 'C'). One 1-D workgroup per right-hand side column (row for side = 'R') runs the forward/back substitution. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_trsm!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ka_trsm_strided_batched!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}}","page":"Internals","text":"ka_trsm_strided_batched!(side, uplo, trans, diag, α, A, B; workgroup = Val(128)) -> B\n\nStrided batched ka_trsm! on 3-D arrays (batch = third dimension, the second ndrange dimension of the launch).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ka_trsm_strided_batched!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.laswp!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"laswp!(A, ipiv; npiv = length(ipiv), reverse = false, impl = :auto) -> A\n\nApply the row interchanges of ipiv (as returned by getrf!) to A: for i = 1:npiv swap rows i and ipiv[i], so that laswp!(A, ipiv) computes P A; reverse = true applies them backwards (Pᵀ A). npiv is LAPACK's k2 (with k1 = 1): pass npiv = min(m, n) when ipiv is an oversized buffer filled by getrf!. Only :ka exists (no generic entry point; vendor bindings may be added per backend).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.laswp!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ldlt_blocked_path-Tuple{Any, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"ldlt_blocked_path(sc, s) -> Bool\n\nFront s takes the blocked LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ path (panel_ldlt_kernel! and GEMMs, src/numeric/ldlt_c.jl): it is on the regime-C path (takes_c_path), or in a regime-B bin of width class ≥ LDLT_BLOCKED_MIN_WCLASS and row class ≥ LDLT_BLOCKED_MIN_FCLASS, where the GEMMs beat the fused kernel's in-kernel updates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ldlt_blocked_path"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ldlt_c_chunk_end-Tuple{Any, AbstractVector, Integer, Integer, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"ldlt_c_chunk_end(sc, cb_len, q, last, cap) -> Int\n\nThe fronts sc.group_nodes[q:e] of a regime-C launch group (e ≤ last) that the blocked LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ path factors concurrently, one workgroup each: the longest run from q whose ldlt_c_work_len slices, laid out one after another, fit cap entries (at least one front). cb_len[s] > 0 when front s has a contribution block on the update stack.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ldlt_c_chunk_end"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ldlt_c_work_len","page":"Internals","text":"ldlt_c_work_len(f, w, cb::Bool, nb = LDLT_C_NB) -> Int\n\nWorkspace entries of a regime-C LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ front with f rows and w fully-summed columns: the m×m contribution block (m = f - w, when cb), Lb and Wb (f × (nb + 1) each), the saved column, the next pivot column of pass 1 and the rejection flags of the fallback scan (f each).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ldlt_c_work_len"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.lu_pivoting-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.PivotType}","page":"Internals","text":"lu_pivoting(pivot_type::PivotType) -> Bool\n\nWhether the LU of structure \"G\" interchanges rows inside the fully-summed block of a front: 'A' (default), 'L' and 'B' mean in-block threshold partial pivoting; 'N' and 'D' keep the diagonal pivots (perturbed when tiny). Global pivoting ('C', 'R') is not planned (PLAN §3.3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.lu_pivoting"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.matched_colval-Union{Tuple{INT}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Matching, AbstractVector{INT}, Any}} where INT","page":"Internals","text":"matched_colval(m::Matching, colval, index) -> Vector\n\nThe column indices of the CSR pattern of the matched matrix M = (Dr A Dc)[:, q] of \"G\" (q = m.perm): column c of the stored matrix becomes column q⁻¹[c], in the index base index and the element type of colval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.matched_colval"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.matching_job-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.MatchingAlg}","page":"Internals","text":"matching_job(alg::MatchingAlg) -> Int\n\nThe MC64 job of matching_alg (PLAN §1.3): \"algo1\"–\"algo5\" are jobs 1–5, \"algo6\" (auto) is job 5, \"default\" is 0 (no matching).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.matching_job"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.matching_pairs-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Matching, Any, Any, Any, Integer, Any, Options}","page":"Internals","text":"matching_pairs(m::Matching, rowptr, colval, nzval, n, structure, opts; view = 'F', index = 'O')\n    -> Vector{Tuple{Int,Int}}\n\nThe 2×2 pivot pairs (partner, candidate) of \"S\"/\"H\" derived from the symmetric matching m (issue #67, Duff & Pralet 2005), sorted by candidate, for compute_ordering(…; pairs):\n\nthe matching permutation is split into cycles (only cycles of matched indices); a 2-cycle (i, j) is a pair; a longer cycle is split into pairs of consecutive indices (adjacent in the matrix, since aᵢ,perm[i] ≠ 0): for an even length the alternative with the larger product of scaled |dᵢ aᵢⱼ dⱼ|, for an odd length leaving out the index with the largest scaled diagonal;\nopts.pivot_pairs = \"default\" keeps a pair only when one of its indices is a 2×2 candidate of the scaled matrix: |dᵢ² aᵢᵢ| ≤ τ maxⱼ≠ᵢ |dᵢ aᵢⱼ dⱼ|, τ = opts.pivot_pair_tolerance; \"all\" keeps every pair; \"none\" none;\nthe candidate of a pair is its index with the smaller relative scaled diagonal, the other one the partner (eliminated first).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.matching_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.max_local_bytes-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"max_local_bytes(backend) -> Int\n\nLargest static @localmem size (bytes) one workgroup of a KernelAbstractions kernel may declare on backend (issue #60). The regime-A subtree kernels pick their local-memory class from SUBTREE_LOCAL_SIZES up to this cap: the analysis clamps the default subtree_budgets to it and rejects an explicit budget above it with an InvalidValueError.\n\n49152 (48 KiB) for a backend without a method, the static shared-memory limit of CUDA; the CPU backend has no limit (typemax(Int)). The backend extensions add their own methods (CUDA: 48 KiB; AMDGPU: the device's LDS size per workgroup, 64 KiB on CDNA).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.max_local_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.max_rhs-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace}","page":"Internals","text":"max_rhs(ws::SolveWorkspace) -> Int\n\nNumber of right-hand sides ws was allocated for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.max_rhs"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.mc64-Tuple{AbstractVector{Int64}, AbstractVector{Int64}, AbstractVector{Float64}, Int64, Int64}","page":"Internals","text":"mc64(ptr, adj, w, n, job) -> (lmate, rscale, cscale)\n\nMC64-style matching of the rows 1:n of a square matrix (CSR ptr/adj, absolute values w) to its columns:\n\njob 1: maximum cardinality (structural; every stored entry counts);\njobs 2 and 3: maximize the smallest matched |aᵢⱼ| (bottleneck);\njob 4: maximize the sum of the matched |aᵢⱼ|;\njob 5: maximize their product, with the scalings rᵢ = exp(uᵢ) / maxⱼ |aᵢⱼ|, cⱼ = exp(vⱼ) from the duals of the assignment on the costs log maxₖ |aᵢₖ| - log |aᵢⱼ|, so that |rᵢ aᵢⱼ cⱼ| ≤ 1, with equality on the matched entries.\n\nJobs 2–5 use the nonzero entries only. lmate[i] is the column matched to row i (0 for an unmatched row of a structurally singular matrix); the scalings are ones for jobs 1–4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.mc64"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.member_info!-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic}","page":"Internals","text":"member_info!(info, numeric, symbolic) -> info\n\nWrite the info of the last factorize! of every active batch member k into info[k] (the original column of the member's first failed pivot: non-positive for Cholesky, zero with pivot_epsilon = 0 for LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ and LU; 0 = success; inactive members keep their entries). Host data only: the statuses were read by factorize!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.member_info!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.member_numeric-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"member_numeric(numeric, symbolic, k) -> Numeric{T, Vector{T}}\n\nHost copy of batch member k of numeric as a single-matrix storage (the layout of allocate_numeric(symbolic, T)), for the reference comparisons and extract_L.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.member_numeric"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.memory_estimates-Union{Tuple{INT}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{INT0}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic{INT0, VI} where VI<:AbstractVector{INT0}, Type{T}}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic{INT0, VI} where VI<:AbstractVector{INT0}, Type{T}, Type{INT}}} where {INT0, T, INT}","page":"Internals","text":"memory_estimates(symbolic, T[, INT]) -> Vector{Int64}\n\nThe \"memory_estimates\" data parameter (16 entries, bytes) for factors of element type T and device indices INT (default: the index type of symbolic's maps, Int for a host analysis):\n\nslot content\n1 permanent device memory: factor panels + D + device maps + per-front statistics + regime-C workspace\n2 peak device memory: slot 1 + update stack high-water mark\n3 permanent host memory (the host analysis data)\n4 peak host memory (= slot 3)\n5 minimum device memory of the hybrid memory mode (= slot 2 until M12)\n6 maximum host memory of the hybrid memory mode (0 until M12)\n7 factor panels (Layout.factor_len entries of T; twice that for \"G\": L and Uᵀ)\n8 D (2n entries of T)\n9 update stack (Layout.stack_len entries of T; twice that for \"G\")\n10 device maps (INT)\n11 per-front statistics (6 Int64 per supernode) and their totals (6 Int64), status (ns + 1 Int32), pivot order (n Int32), pivot kinds and sign requests (n Int8 each), aux (one T)\n12 largest regime-A local memory in use (per workgroup, subtree_local_bytes of its class)\n13–16 0 (reserved)\n\nSlots 1–6 follow cuDSS's CUDSS_DATA_MEMORY_ESTIMATES. getparam(solver, \"memory_estimates\") adds to slots 1, 2 and 5 the device buffers of the matching when there is one (matching_alg): the scaled values (nnz · nbatch of T), the entry weights (nnz) and the two scale vectors (n each) of real(T), and for \"G\" the column permutation (n of INT).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.memory_estimates"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.min_cost_matching-Tuple{AbstractVector{Int64}, AbstractVector{Int64}, AbstractVector{Float64}, Int64}","page":"Internals","text":"min_cost_matching(ptr, adj, cost, n) -> (lmate, u, v)\n\nMinimum-cost matching of maximum cardinality between the left vertices 1:n (adjacency adj[ptr[i]:ptr[i+1]-1], costs cost ≥ 0 aligned with adj) and the right vertices 1:n, by successive shortest augmenting paths (Dijkstra on the reduced costs cost - u[i] - v[j] ≥ 0, a binary heap; the sparse Hungarian method of MC64). Returns lmate[i] (the right vertex matched to i, 0 if none) and the dual variables u (left) and v (right): u[i] + v[j] ≤ cost on every edge, with equality on the matched ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.min_cost_matching"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.nd_available-Tuple{}","page":"Internals","text":"nd_available() -> Bool\n\nWhether nested dissection can be used (Metis.jl is loaded, which activates SparseDirectSolverMetisExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.nd_available"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.neighbors-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"neighbors(P::SymmetricPattern, j)\n\nSorted indices i ≠ j adjacent to j (a view into P.rowval).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.neighbors"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.nlaunches-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Schedule}","page":"Internals","text":"nlaunches(schedule) -> Int\n\nNumber of kernel and vendor launches of one numeric factorization following schedule: one per regime-A budget class, one per regime-B group (fused assemble + factor + update kernel), and per regime-C group one batched assembly/extend-add launch plus, per front, potrf and, when the front has a contribution block, trsm, syrk/herk and the pack_add! of the workspace product into the packed contribution block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.nlaunches"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.nnz_L-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"nnz_L(counts) -> Int\n\nNumber of nonzeros of the Cholesky factor L (diagonal included): sum(counts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.nnz_L"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.npivots-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric}","page":"Internals","text":"npivots(numeric) -> Int64\n\nNumber of perturbed pivots of an LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization (PLAN §1.4 npivots).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.npivots"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.nsubtrees-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Schedule}","page":"Internals","text":"nsubtrees(schedule) -> Int\n\nNumber of regime-A subtrees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.nsubtrees"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.nsupernodes-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.ColumnPartition}","page":"Internals","text":"nsupernodes(cp) -> Int\n\nNumber of supernodes of a ColumnPartition or SupernodePartition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.nsupernodes"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.nsuperpanels-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition}","page":"Internals","text":"nsuperpanels(sp::SupernodePartition) -> Int\n\nNumber of supernodes after amalgamation (the \"nsuperpanels\" data parameter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.nsuperpanels"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ordering_cost-Tuple{Real, Integer, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"ordering_cost(flops, nlevels, n) -> Float64\n\nCost model of the automatic ordering choice (PLAN §2.3 step 2): predicted flops weighted by the relative critical path, flops × (1 + nlevels / n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ordering_cost"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.pack_add!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Integer, AbstractVector, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"pack_add!(stack, c0, work, m) -> stack\npack_add!(stack, c0, work, m, members, j0, count, nbatch) -> stack\n\nAdd the lower triangle of the column-major m×m matrix work[1:m^2] to the packed contribution block stack[c0:(c0 + m(m+1)/2 - 1)] (the regime-C step after the vendor syrk/herk into the workspace). Batched form: the count blocks work[(b-1)m² + 1 : b m²] go to the stacks of batch members members[j0 + b] (device vector) of an nbatch-member update stack. One launch, one work item per entry. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.pack_add!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.packed_length-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"packed_length(m) -> Int\n\nEntries of the packed lower triangle of an m×m matrix, m(m+1)/2: the storage of a contribution block on the update stack and of a regime-A front in local memory (column-major packed, see _packed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.packed_length"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.pair_pattern-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"pair_pattern(P::SymmetricPattern, pairs) -> SymmetricPattern\n\nThe pattern the symbolic factorization runs on when pairs is not empty (P itself otherwise): the expansion of compressed_pattern, where both columns of a pair have the union structure of the pair (explicit structural zeros of the factor) and are adjacent to each other. With the partner ordered right before its candidate, the candidate is the only child of the partner in the elimination tree and their columns have the same structure below the pair, so the pair is (part of) one fundamental supernode; amalgamation only merges supernodes, so the pair is never split.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.pair_pattern"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.pairs_enabled-Tuple{Any, Options}","page":"Internals","text":"pairs_enabled(structure, opts::Options) -> Bool\n\nWhether the analysis looks for 2×2 pivot candidate pairs: opts.pivot_pairs is \"default\" or \"all\", structure \"S\" or \"H\", no user_perm and not the natural ordering (reordering_alg = \"algo5\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.pairs_enabled"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.panel-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}, Integer}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}, Integer, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"panel(symbolic, numeric, s, k = 1) -> Matrix\n\nHost copy of the f×w panel of supernode s (rows snrows(s), the factor columns of s; the strict upper triangle of its first w rows is zero) of batch member k.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.panel"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.panel_ldlt_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"panel_ldlt_kernel!(backend, WG)(factor, lw, d, piv, pivot_kind, psign, perm, aux, stats, nodes, qa, b, nb,\n                                super_ptr, front_ptr, k, nbatch, front_nrows, front_ncols, cb_ptr, prm, Val(WG);\n                                ndrange = WG * count)\n\nBlock b (columns (b - 1) nb + 1 : min(b nb, w)) of the regime-C LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ pivot steps of the count fronts nodes[qa:(qa + count - 1)], one workgroup each (panel of batch member k of nbatch at member_panels(front_ptr, k, nbatch)[s], workspace slice in lw after those of the fronts before it: Lb, Wb, the saved column, the pass-1 column): the reference's pivot sequence on the lazily updated panel (see the top of src/numeric/ldlt_c.jl), D, piv, the pivot kinds and the front's statistics (added to those of the earlier blocks). Fronts with fewer blocks do nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.panel_ldlt_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.panel_offset-Tuple{AbstractVector, Integer, Integer, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"panel_offset(panel_ptr, s, k, nb) -> Int\n\nFirst factor index of the panel of supernode s of batch member k in the interleaved layout (host side, panel_ptr of the Layout).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.panel_offset"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.parameter_spec-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Internals","text":"parameter_spec(name) -> ParameterSpec\n\nLook up a parameter name; unknown names raise ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.parameter_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.permute_rhs!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractVecOrMat, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"permute_rhs!(Y, B, perm; transposed = false, conjugate = false, bm = nothing, scale = nothing) -> Y\n\nY[k, r] = B[perm[k], r] (conjugated when conjugate, for the solves with conj(A) of solve_mode; times scale[perm[k]] when a real scale vector is given, the row or column scaling of matching, SolveScaling) for the nrhs right-hand sides of the user array B (rhs_count: vector, matrix, strided vector; row-major when transposed) into the first nrhs columns of the n × ≥ nrhs device matrix Y. With a BatchMap bm (uniform batch), B holds bm.nrhs right-hand sides for each of the bm.nbatch members and only those of the active members are copied, member slot j to the columns (j - 1) bm.nrhs + 1 : j bm.nrhs of Y. One launch; asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.permute_rhs!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.phase_includes-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Phase, SparseDirectSolver.Phase}","page":"Internals","text":"phase_includes(phase::Phase, part::Phase) -> Bool\n\nWhether the bits of part are all set in phase, e.g. phase_includes(PHASE_SOLVE, PHASE_SOLVE_DIAG) == true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.phase_includes"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.pivot_candidates-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, AbstractArray, Integer, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"pivot_candidates(P::SymmetricPattern, rowptr, colval, nzval, n, structure; view = 'F', index = 'O')\n    -> PairCandidates\npivot_candidates(P::SymmetricPattern, A::CSR, structure; view = 'F') -> PairCandidates\n\nThe 2×2 pivot candidates (PairCandidates) of the CSR matrix (rowptr, colval, nzval; the first batch member when nzval is a matrix) whose symmetric pattern is P, read with the view and index rules of SymmetricPattern (duplicates summed), with the candidate tolerance τ = tolerance (the analysis passes opts.pivot_pair_tolerance). The only read of the values at analysis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.pivot_candidates"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.pivot_pairs-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, SparseDirectSolver.PairCandidates}","page":"Internals","text":"pivot_pairs(P::SymmetricPattern, rowptr, colval, nzval, n, structure; view = 'F', index = 'O')\n    -> Vector{Tuple{Int,Int}}\npivot_pairs(P::SymmetricPattern, A::CSR, structure; view = 'F')\npivot_pairs(P::SymmetricPattern, cands::PairCandidates)\n\nThe pairs of pivot_pairs = \"all\": every 2×2 pivot candidate of pivot_candidates matched to a neighbour, as (partner, candidate), sorted by candidate.\n\nPairing: a greedy matching of candidates to non-candidate neighbours with aᵢⱼ ≠ 0. Candidates are taken by increasing number of such neighbours (then by index); each takes the free neighbour with the largest |aᵢⱼ|, ties by smaller degree in P, then smaller index. Each non-candidate is matched at most once; unmatched candidates stay single columns.\n\nOn KKT systems about half of the rows are candidates, and ordering with all of them paired roughly doubles nnz(L) (issue #66); \"default\" pairs only the candidates that need it, see zero_pivot_pairs!. Does not look at structure beyond the view rules; pairs_enabled decides whether the analysis calls it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.pivot_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.pivot_stats-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric}","page":"Internals","text":"pivot_stats(numeric) -> (npos, nneg, nzero, nperturbed, n2x2)\n\nSum of the per-front statistics of an LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization (PLAN §1.7 pivot_stats): positive and negative eigenvalues of D (after perturbation; a 2×2 block with negative determinant counts one of each), pivots that were exactly zero, perturbed pivots, 2×2 blocks. A device numeric.stats is copied to the host. npos/nneg are 0 for complex symmetric (\"S\") matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.pivot_stats"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.pivot_totals","page":"Internals","text":"pivot_totals(numeric, k = 1) -> (npos, nneg, nzero, nperturbed, n2x2)\n\nThe statistics of batch member k reduced on the device by reduce_stats! (one copy of numeric.totals to the host: a synchronization, done at the phase boundary by getparam).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.pivot_totals"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.postorder-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"postorder(parent) -> post::Vector{Int}\n\nA postorder of the forest parent (0 = root): post[k] is the node visited k-th; children are visited in increasing index order and every node comes after all its descendants. Non-recursive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.postorder"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.potrf!-Tuple{Any, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Internals","text":"potrf!(uplo, A; impl = :auto) -> info::Int\n\nCholesky factorization of the uplo triangle of the square matrix A in place (A = LLᴴ or UᴴU). Returns 0 on success, or the first column j (1-based) whose leading minor is not positive definite, as LAPACK potrf. Reading info synchronizes with the device.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.potrf!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.potrf_batched_info!","page":"Internals","text":"potrf_batched_info!(uplo, A, info, offset = 0; impl = :auto) -> info\n\nUniform strided batch of potrf_info!: Cholesky of the uplo triangle of every member A[:, :, b] of the 3-D array A in place, its status (0, or the first non-positive pivot column) into info[offset + b]. Implementations: :vendor (vendor_potrf_batched!, validated on the device as potrf_info!) and :ka (ka_potrf!, one workgroup per member). Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.potrf_batched_info!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.potrf_info!","page":"Internals","text":"potrf_info!(uplo, A, info, idx = 1; impl = :auto) -> info\n\nAsynchronous potrf!: Cholesky of the uplo triangle of A in place, with the LAPACK info (0, or the first non-positive pivot column) written to info[idx] of the device Int32 vector info instead of being returned, so the numeric phase can factor many fronts and read their status once (PLAN §3.9). :vendor (vendor_potrf_info!) and :generic results are validated on the device by ka_chol_check_info! (cuSOLVER can miss a non-positive last pivot, see potrf!); :ka is ka_potrf!. The :vendor and :ka paths do not synchronize with the host; :generic does when the backend's cholesky! reads its info (CUDA).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.potrf_info!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.print_capabilities-Tuple{IO, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"print_capabilities([io = stdout], backend; eltypes = (Float32, Float64, ComplexF32, ComplexF64))\n\nPrint the capabilities table of backend: one row per probe, one column per element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.print_capabilities"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.reduce_stats!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic}","page":"Internals","text":"reduce_stats!(numeric, symbolic) -> numeric\n\nSum the per-front statistics numeric.stats into numeric.totals (npos, nneg, nzero, nperturbed, n2x2, and the number of fronts with a failed pivot) of every active batch member, and the smallest failed factor column (super_ptr[s] + info - 1 over the fronts with a nonzero info statistic, 0 = none) into numeric.info[ns + 1]: one workgroup per member, @localmem tree reduction, no atomics. Asynchronous; pivot_totals reads the result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.reduce_stats!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ref_factorize!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, Vector{T}}, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"ref_factorize!(numeric::Numeric{T, Vector{T}}, symbolic, nzval) -> info\n\nReference multifrontal Cholesky P A Pᵀ = L Lᴴ of the matrix whose stored values are nzval (same CSR pattern, view and index base as the analysis; a host vector or anything Array converts) into the host numeric. The supernodes are processed in snpost order: zero the f×f front, add A through amap_ptr/amap_src/amap (conjugating negative offsets), extend-add the children's contribution blocks through relind in child_list order, then potrf on the w×w block, trsm on the (f-w)×w block and syrk/herk on the contribution block. The front's first w columns are stored in its panel.\n\nReturns info = 0 on success, else the first non-positive pivot (the smallest failed column of the factor, as the column of the original matrix); the factorization stops there and later panels stay zero. Requires structure \"SPD\" (real T) or \"HPD\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ref_factorize!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ref_ldlt!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, Vector{T}}, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"ref_ldlt!(numeric::Numeric{T, Vector{T}}, symbolic, nzval; opts = Options()) -> info\n\nReference multifrontal LDLᵀ (real T, or complex T with structure \"S\", complex symmetric) or LDLᴴ (structure \"H\") of the matrix whose stored values are nzval (same CSR pattern, view and index base as the analysis), into the host numeric. Assembly and extend-add are those of ref_factorize!; each front is then factored densely with pivots chosen inside its fully-summed block (its first w columns), and the contribution block is updated once with F₂₂ ← F₂₂ − (L₂₁ D) L₂₁ᴴ (ᵀ for complex symmetric).\n\nPivoting (opts.pivot_type; 'A', 'B' and 'L' are Bunch–Kaufman, the default):\n\n'B': Bunch–Kaufman with α = (1+√17)/8 on the remaining block, choosing a 1×1 pivot at the current column, a 1×1 pivot at the row r of the largest block entry of that column, or the 2×2 pivot on both. The choice is acceptable when it is not tiny (|d| ≥ ε, or for a 2×2 block a nonzero determinant and an entry ≥ ε) and passes the threshold test with u = opts.pivot_threshold against the whole remaining column of the front, fully-summed or not (|d| ≥ u maxᵢ |aᵢc|; for a 2×2 block, every entry of the resulting L columns bounded by 1/u through |D⁻¹|). Otherwise the acceptable 1×1 pivot of the block with the largest |aⱼⱼ| is taken; if there is none, the Bunch–Kaufman choice is used anyway when it is not tiny (stable inside the block, only the bound on the rows outside fails); else the column is perturbed.\n'D': 1×1 pivots only: the current column if acceptable, else the acceptable diagonal with the largest |aⱼⱼ|, else the largest diagonal if not tiny, else the current column, perturbed.\n'N': no search: the current column, perturbed when tiny.\n\nPerturbation: a 1×1 pivot with |d| < ε becomes d ← sign · ε, where ε is pivot_epsilon (default_pivot_epsilon if unset), multiplied by max |aᵢⱼ| of nzval when pivot_epsilon_alg = \"algo1\" (scaled; \"default\" and \"algo2\" are static), and sign is opts.pivot_sign[row] for the original row of that pivot when given and nonzero, else the sign of d (+1 for d == 0; for complex symmetric T, d/|d|).\n\nFills numeric.factor (unit-lower panels), numeric.d, numeric.piv, numeric.pivot_kind and the per-front numeric.stats (npos, nneg, nzero, nperturbed, n2x2, info; nzero counts pivots that were exactly zero before their perturbation; npos/nneg count the signs of D after perturbation, a 2×2 block with negative determinant counting one of each, and stay 0 for complex symmetric matrices, which have no inertia). An exactly zero 1×1 pivot is always tiny; with ε = 0 its perturbation leaves it zero and the factorization fails there: the front's info statistic is the failed local column (assembled order, the first in pivot order), the following divisions are left to IEEE arithmetic (the factor is unusable), and the return value is the original column of the failed pivot with the smallest factor column, as for the Cholesky oracle. Otherwise returns info = 0: with static perturbation ε > 0 the factorization always completes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ref_ldlt!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ref_lu!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, Vector{T}}, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"ref_lu!(numeric::Numeric{T, Vector{T}}, symbolic, nzval; opts = Options()) -> info\n\nReference multifrontal L D U of the general matrix whose stored values are nzval (structure \"G\", same CSR pattern and index base as the analysis) into the host numeric. The supernodes of the symmetric pattern of A + Aᵀ are processed in postorder: the full f×f front is assembled (A through the amap, whose negative offsets are the entries above the diagonal; the children's full contribution blocks through relind), its w fully-summed columns are factored with row interchanges inside the fully-summed rows, and the contribution block is updated once with F₂₂ ← F₂₂ − (L₂₁ D) U₁₂.\n\nPivot choice at step k (opts.pivot_type, see lu_pivoting): with γ = maxᵢ |F[i, k]| over all remaining rows of the front (fully-summed or not), the diagonal is kept when |F[k, k]| ≥ u γ (u = pivot_threshold), otherwise the fully-summed row r ≥ k with the largest |F[r, k]| (the first one) is swapped into row k; it is taken even when it fails the threshold (static pivoting: no delayed pivots). A pivot with |d| < ε becomes d ← sign(d) ε (+ε for d = 0; d/|d| for complex T), with ε as in ref_ldlt! (pivot_epsilon, scaled by max |aᵢⱼ| with pivot_epsilon_alg = \"algo1\"); pivot_sign is not used by LU.\n\nFills numeric.factor (unit-lower L panels), numeric.ufactor (unit-lower Uᵀ panels), numeric.d, numeric.piv (local row order), numeric.pivot_kind (PIVOT_KIND_1X1 or PIVOT_KIND_PERTURBED) and the per-front numeric.stats (nzero, nperturbed; npos, nneg and n2x2 stay 0: a general matrix has no inertia). An exactly zero pivot is always tiny; with ε = 0 it stays zero and the factorization fails there: the front's info statistic is the failed local column and the return value the original column of the failed pivot with the smallest factor column (as ref_ldlt!). Otherwise returns 0: with static perturbation ε > 0 the factorization always completes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ref_lu!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ref_solve!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, Vector{T}}, AbstractVecOrMat}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"ref_solve!(X, symbolic, numeric, B) -> X\n\nSolve A X = B with the reference factor P A Pᵀ = L Lᴴ of ref_factorize!: Y = B[perm, :], forward sweep L Z = Y over the supernodes in snpost order (trsm on the diagonal block, gemm update of the rows below), backward sweep Lᴴ W = Z in reverse order, X[perm, :] = W. B and X are host vectors or n × nrhs matrices (X === B is allowed). For structures \"S\"/\"H\" this is the LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ solve of ref_solve_ldlt! with a factor of ref_ldlt!, for \"G\" the LU solve of ref_solve_lu! with a factor of ref_lu!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ref_solve!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ref_solve_ldlt!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, Vector{T}}, AbstractVecOrMat}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"ref_solve_ldlt!(X, symbolic, numeric, B) -> X\n\nSolve A X = B with the reference factor of ref_ldlt!: Y = B[perm, :]; forward sweep over the supernodes in snpost order (reorder the supernode's slice of Y by its local pivot order piv, unit-lower trsm, update of the rows below); diagonal step Y ← D⁻¹ Y (1×1 and 2×2 blocks); backward sweep in reverse order (update from the rows below, trsm with L₁₁ᴴ (ᵀ for complex symmetric), undo the local pivot order); X[perm, :] = Y. Called by ref_solve! for structures \"S\"/\"H\". X === B is allowed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ref_solve_ldlt!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.ref_solve_lu!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}, AbstractVecOrMat}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"ref_solve_lu!(X, symbolic, numeric, B; transpose = false) -> X\n\nHost solve of A X = B (Aᵀ X = B when transpose) with the LU factor in numeric through extract_lu (sparse triangular solves; tests and debugging). B, X: vectors or matrices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.ref_solve_lu!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.refine!-Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, AbstractVecOrMat, SparseDirectSolver.RefinementWorkspace, SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"refine!(X, B, W, ws, symbolic, numeric, nzval; nsteps, tol = 0, transposed = false,\n        b_transposed = transposed, conjugate = false, deterministic = false,\n        interrupt = nothing, progress = Ref(0), transpose_matrix = false, scaling = nothing) -> steps\n\nPlain iterative refinement of the solution X of op(A) X = B (op(A) = M, or conj(M) when conjugate; Mᵀ or conj(Mᵀ) with transpose_matrix, LU only), at most nsteps steps of\n\nR = B - op(A) X            (residual!)\nstop if tol > 0 and maxₖ ‖Rₖ‖₂ / ‖Bₖ‖₂ ≤ tol   (residual_norms!, one host synchronization)\nX += op(A)⁻¹ R             (permute, forward / diagonal / backward sweeps, add_correction!)\n\nwith the factor in numeric, the solve workspace ws and the refinement workspace W (with the permutations and scalings of matching when scaling is a SolveScaling, solve_io; the residual is always the one of the original matrix values nzval). tol = 0 (the default of ir_tol) never stops early and never synchronizes. interrupt (a Threads.Atomic{Bool} or nothing) is polled before every step (InterruptedError; X then holds the last completed iterate). Returns the number of corrections applied, which is also kept in progress[] after every step (so it survives an interrupt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.refine!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.refinement_map-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.FullPatternMap}","page":"Internals","text":"refinement_map(F::FullPatternMap; transpose = false) -> (rowptr, colval, src)\n\nHost CSR of the full matrix of F over its contributions (duplicates are not merged; their products are summed by the SpMV): row i holds the entries rowptr[i]:rowptr[i+1]-1, column colval[c], value nzval[src[c]], conjugated when src[c] < 0. 1-based. With transpose = true (structure \"G\") the rows of Mᵀ follow as rows n+1:2n (rowptr has 2n + 1 entries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.refinement_map"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.relative_indices-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition}","page":"Internals","text":"relative_indices(sp::SupernodePartition) -> (relind_ptr, relind)\n\nExtend-add maps: for every supernode c with a parent p the positions of snrows(sp, c)[w_c+1:end] inside snrows(sp, p) (they are a subset, both sorted), concatenated; empty for roots and fronts without a contribution block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.relative_indices"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.residual!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.RefinementWorkspace, AbstractVector, AbstractVecOrMat, AbstractVecOrMat}","page":"Internals","text":"residual!(W, nzval, X, B; nrhs, transposed = false, b_transposed = transposed, conjugate = false,\n          bm = nothing, transpose_matrix = false) -> W.R\n\nW.R[:, 1:nrhs] = B - M X (conj(M) when conjugate; Mᵀ when transpose_matrix, which needs a \"G\" map) with the full matrix M of the map of W and the user values nzval; X, B in the layouts of rhs_count (row-major when transposed/b_transposed) with nrhs right-hand sides. Uniform batch (BatchMap bm): nzval holds the values of the bm.nbatch members, and the residuals of the active members go to the compact columns 1:(bm.nrhs bm.nact) of W.R. One launch, one work item per (row, right-hand side), gather-based (no atomics). Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.residual!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.residual_norms!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.RefinementWorkspace, AbstractVecOrMat}","page":"Internals","text":"residual_norms!(W, B; nrhs, transposed = false, bm = nothing) -> W.norms_host\n\n‖W.R[:, k]‖₂² and ‖B[:, k]‖₂² for the compact columns k (1:nrhs, or the active members' columns of the BatchMap bm) reduced on the device (one workgroup per right-hand side, @localmem tree reduction) into W.norms, then copied to W.norms_host (one host synchronization).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.residual_norms!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.resolve_subtree_budgets-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"resolve_subtree_budgets(budgets, max_local) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe regime-A budgets an analysis uses on a backend with max_local bytes of static local memory per workgroup (max_local_bytes, issue #60), sorted. The default budgets (DEFAULT_SUBTREE_BUDGETS, 16, 32 and 48 KiB) are clamped to max_local (duplicates dropped), so the defaults work on every backend; an explicit budget above max_local raises InvalidValueError, since its kernel could not be launched there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.resolve_subtree_budgets"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.resolved_pivot_epsilon-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Options, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"resolved_pivot_epsilon(opts::Options, T) -> Float64\n\nopts.pivot_epsilon, or default_pivot_epsilon(T) when it was not set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.resolved_pivot_epsilon"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.rhs_count-Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"rhs_count(B, n; transposed = false) -> nrhs\n\nNumber of right-hand sides of the user array B for a system of size n: 1 for an n vector, size(B, 2) for an n × nrhs matrix (size(B, 1) for a transposed, i.e. row-major, nrhs × n one), length(B) ÷ n for a strided vector. Raises DimensionMismatch when the shape does not fit n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.rhs_count"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.scale_values!-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"scale_values!(out, weights, nzval) -> out\n\nout[k + (m - 1) nnz] = weights[k] nzval[k + (m - 1) nnz] for the nnz = length(weights) stored entries of every batch member m (one launch, asynchronous): the values rᵢ aᵢⱼ cⱼ the numeric phase factors with matching.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.scale_values!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.scatter_A!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, AbstractVector, Integer, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"scatter_A!(numeric, symbolic, nzval, first, count) -> numeric\n\nAssemble the stored values nzval (on the backend of numeric) into the zeroed panels of the fronts symbolic.group_nodes[first:(first + count - 1)] through amap_ptr/amap_src/amap (one workgroup per front, one work item per nonzero; duplicated entries are summed by one work item in nzval order, negative offsets add the conjugate). Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.scatter_A!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.schedule_elsize-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"schedule_elsize(structure, T) -> Int\n\nBytes per packed entry of the schedule's local-memory and update-stack budgets: sizeof(T), or 2 sizeof(T) for structure \"G\" (LU fronts and contribution blocks are two packed triangles, L and Uᵀ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.schedule_elsize"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.schur_flags-Tuple{Any, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"schur_flags(indices, n) -> BitVector\n\nThe Schur set of user_schur_indices (n flags 0/1, 1 = the row and column belong to the Schur complement) as a BitVector; raises InvalidValueError when indices is missing, has the wrong length or selects no index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.schur_flags"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.schur_matrix!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractMatrix, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric{T, VT} where VT<:AbstractVector{T}, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"schur_matrix!(dest, numeric, symbolic; transposed = false) -> dest\n\nWrite the Schur complement assembled by assemble_schur! into dest: an ns × ns dense matrix on the backend of numeric (every entry), or a CSR whose rowptr and colval already hold the pattern to export (the solver writes them, see getparam(solver, \"schur_matrix\")): only its values are written. transposed: the solver's matrix is a transposed CSR (a CSC input), whose stored Schur complement is the transpose of the user's. One launch; asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.schur_matrix!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.schur_pattern-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, AbstractVector{Bool}}","page":"Internals","text":"schur_pattern(P::SymmetricPattern, schur::AbstractVector{Bool}) -> (rowptr, colval)\n\nSymbolic pattern of the Schur complement S = A₂₂ − A₂₁ A₁₁⁻¹ A₁₂ of the flagged rows and columns (A₂₂), in their increasing original order, as 1-based CSR arrays with sorted columns, both triangles and the whole diagonal: (i, j) is in the pattern when i == j, when (i, j) is in P, or when both are adjacent to one connected component of the graph of the other vertices (a fill path through eliminated vertices only). P is the symmetric pattern of the analysis (A + Aᵀ for \"G\"), so for LU the pattern may hold entries that are numerically zero by structure (explicit zeros).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.schur_pattern"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.schur_supernode_partition","page":"Internals","text":"schur_supernode_partition(P::SymmetricPattern, perm, ns, opts::Options = Options()) -> SupernodePartition\n\nsupernode_partition for Schur complement mode (PLAN §3.6): the last ns columns of perm (the Schur block, compute_schur_ordering) form one dense root supernode, the last one, with its columns in the order of perm; its etree is the chain of those columns and its column counts are the dense ones (ns, ns - 1, …, 1). The other columns are partitioned as usual (fundamental supernodes and amalgamate on their elimination forest, so nothing is merged into the Schur root); a supernode whose last column has its etree parent in the Schur block becomes a child of the Schur root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.schur_supernode_partition"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.select_impl-Tuple{Symbol, AbstractArray, Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"select_impl(op::Symbol, X::AbstractArray, impl::Symbol) -> Symbol\n\nResolve impl for dense op op on the backend and element type of X: :auto becomes the first entry of dense_impls; an explicit :generic or :vendor the capability table does not list raises NotSupportedError; anything else raises InvalidValueError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.select_impl"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.set_log_level!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"SparseDirectSolver.set_log_level!(level) -> Int\n\nSet the verbosity of the solver's log messages: 0/\"none\" (default; the messages are @debug records), 1/\"info\" (phase summaries: analysis, factorization, refinement steps performed, as @info), 2/\"debug\" (also the relative residual of every refinement step). The initial value comes from the environment variable SDS_LOG_LEVEL. Returns the previous level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.set_log_level!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.set_members!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"set_members!(numeric, members) -> numeric\n\nMake members (1-based, increasing, a subset of 1:numeric.nbatch) the active batch members of the next phases: copied to numeric.members (a host-to-device copy, only when they change) and split into runs of consecutive members. Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.set_members!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.sncols-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"sncols(sp, s) -> UnitRange{Int}\n\nColumns of supernode s (supernodal numbering).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.sncols"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.snrows-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"snrows(sp, s)\n\nSorted rows of supernode s (a view), its own columns first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.snrows"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.snwidth-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"snwidth(sp, s) -> Int\n\nNumber of columns w of supernode s; its panel is length(snrows(sp, s)) × w.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.snwidth"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.solve_bwd_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"solve_bwd_kernel!(backend, WG)(Y, U, factor, piv, list, bm, count, subtree_ptr, subtree_nodes, super_ptr,\n                               rowptr, rowval, front_ptr, front_nrows, front_ncols, child_ptr, Val(SUB),\n                               Val(LDL), Val(CONJ), Val(WG); ndrange = WG * count * ncols)\n\nBackward sweep Lᴴ x = z (Lᵀ when CONJ = false) of right-hand side r (of batch member _bm_cmember(bm, r)) by workgroup G = g + count (r - 1) over its fronts (the subtree list[bm.first + g - 1] in reverse processing order, SUB = true, or one front): gather the finished rows below, GEMV with L21ᴴ, TRSV with L11ᴴ, then (LDL) restore the order before the front's local pivoting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.solve_bwd_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.solve_conjugated-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Structure, Type{T}, Bool, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"solve_conjugated(structure, T, csc_input::Bool, solve_mode) -> Bool\n\nWhether op(A) (solve_mode 0: A, 1: Aᵀ, 2: Aᴴ) of the user matrix is the conjugate of the stored, factorized matrix M (A = M, or A = Mᵀ for a CSC input). Real T: never. Complex symmetric \"S\" (M = Mᵀ): for Aᴴ. Hermitian \"H\"/\"HPD\" (Mᵀ = conj(M)): for Aᵀ of a CSR input, and for A and Aᴴ of a CSC input. General \"G\": for Aᴴ (op(A) = conj(Mᵀ) or conj(M), see solve_transposed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.solve_conjugated"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.solve_fwd_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"solve_fwd_kernel!(backend, WG)(Y, U, factor, piv, list, bm, count, subtree_ptr, subtree_nodes, super_ptr,\n                               rowptr, rowval, front_ptr, front_nrows, front_ncols, child_ptr, child_list,\n                               relind_ptr, relind, Val(SUB), Val(DET), Val(LDL), Val(WG);\n                               ndrange = WG * count * ncols)\n\nForward sweep L z = y of right-hand side r (a column of Y, of batch member _bm_cmember(bm, r)) by workgroup G = g + count (r - 1) over its fronts: the subtree list[bm.first + g - 1], its supernodes in processing order (SUB = true, regime A), or the single front list[bm.first + g - 1] (regime B). Per front: (deterministic, DET) zero its update buffer and pull the children's; (LDL) reorder its columns of Y by its local pivot order piv; TRSV with L11 on its columns of Y; GEMV with L21 into its update buffer or (atomic variant) into the rows of Y below.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.solve_fwd_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.solve_io-Tuple{Any, Nothing, Bool}","page":"Internals","text":"solve_io(S::Symbolic, scaling, transpose::Bool) -> (inperm, inscale, outperm, outscale)\n\nThe permutations and scalings of the right-hand side (permute_rhs!) and of the solution (unpermute_solution!, add_correction!) of a solve with the factored matrix M (with Mᵀ when transpose): S.perm both ways and no scaling without matching (scaling === nothing); with a SolveScaling (rperm, rscale, cperm, cscale) for M and (cperm, cscale, rperm, rscale) for Mᵀ = Qᵀ Dc Aᵀ Dr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.solve_io"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.solve_memory-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, Type{T}}, Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, Type{T}, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"solve_memory(symbolic, T, nrhs = 1) -> Int64\n\nBytes of the SolveWorkspace of nrhs right-hand sides.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.solve_memory"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.solve_transposed-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Structure, Bool, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"solve_transposed(structure, csc_input::Bool, solve_mode) -> Bool\n\nWhether the solve of op(A) (solve_mode 0: A, 1: Aᵀ, 2: Aᴴ) uses the transpose Mᵀ of the stored, factorized matrix M (A = M, or A = Mᵀ for a CSC input), conjugated or not (solve_conjugated). Only for structure \"G\" (LU): Mᵀ for Aᵀ/Aᴴ of a CSR input and for A of a CSC input. Symmetric and Hermitian structures never need it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.solve_transposed"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.strided_batch-Tuple{AbstractVector, Vararg{Integer, 5}}","page":"Internals","text":"strided_batch(buf, offset, m, n, stride, count) -> AbstractArray{T,3}\n\nThe uniform batch of count column-major m × n matrices stored in the flat vector buf, the first starting after offset entries and each stride ≥ m n entries after the previous one, as an m × n × count view sharing buf (no copy). stride must be a multiple of m, and when stride > m n the buffer must extend over the full stride of the last member too (offset + stride * count ≤ length(buf)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.strided_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.structural_zero_pivots","page":"Internals","text":"structural_zero_pivots(cands::PairCandidates, P::SymmetricPattern, perm, pairs = []) -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe candidates of cands, not in pairs, whose pivot is structurally zero when A[perm, perm] is factored column by column, in elimination order.\n\nWith generic values, the k-th pivot is nonzero exactly when the leading block A[perm[1:k], perm[1:k]] is structurally nonsingular (has a perfect matching on its pattern) given that the previous one is; the diagonal of a candidate counts as absent. The test runs incrementally: a perfect matching of the prefix is kept, a non-candidate is matched to its own diagonal, and a candidate looks for an augmenting path from its row to its column through earlier columns (|aᵢⱼ| > 0 edges, and the diagonal of a non-candidate row that an earlier path moved off it). A candidate without one is reported and matched to itself, as if a partner fixed it. A pair (a, c) with c right after a is matched crosswise (a 2×2 block). An elimination-tree leaf candidate is always reported; so is a candidate whose earlier neighbours are all used up by earlier candidates (two dual rows coupled to the same single primal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.structural_zero_pivots"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.subtree_capacity","page":"Internals","text":"subtree_capacity(budget, elsize, reserve = SUBTREE_LOCAL_RESERVE) -> Int\n\nEntries of elsize bytes a regime-A subtree may keep in local memory under budget: subtree_local_bytes minus reserve (the kernel's other local memory, subtree_local_reserve), divided by elsize (0 when the budget is too small).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.subtree_capacity"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.subtree_cholesky_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"subtree_cholesky_kernel!(backend, WG)(factor, stack, info, nzval, amap, amap_ptr, amap_src, trees, bm,\n                                      subtree_ptr, subtree_nodes, front_ptr, front_nrows, front_ncols, cb_ptr,\n                                      local_front, local_cb, child_ptr, child_list, relind_ptr, relind,\n                                      Val(NE), Val(WG); ndrange = WG * count * bm.nact)\n\nFused regime-A kernel: workgroup G takes subtree t = trees[bm.first + g - 1] of batch member k (g, k from the BatchMap bm) and processes its supernodes subtree_nodes[subtree_ptr[t]:(subtree_ptr[t+1]-1)] in order with the serial stack of packed fronts and contribution blocks in a @localmem buffer of NE entries (offsets local_front, local_cb): zero, scatter A, extend-add the children, factor the front's first w columns (Cholesky of F11, L21, Schur complement), write the panel and info[v], then move the contribution block down, or, for the subtree root (cb_ptr > 0), write it to the update stack (packed lower triangle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.subtree_cholesky_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.subtree_ldlt_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"subtree_ldlt_kernel!(backend, WG)(factor, stack, info, stats, d, piv, pivot_kind, psign, perm, aux, nzval, amap,\n                                  amap_ptr, amap_src, trees, bm, subtree_ptr, subtree_nodes, super_ptr,\n                                  front_ptr, front_nrows, front_ncols, cb_ptr, local_front, local_cb, child_ptr,\n                                  child_list, relind_ptr, relind, prm, Val(NE), Val(WG);\n                                  ndrange = WG * count * bm.nact)\n\nLDLᵀ/LDLᴴ kernel of regime A: workgroup G takes subtree t = trees[bm.first + g - 1] of batch member k and processes its supernodes in order with the serial stack of packed fronts and contribution blocks in a @localmem buffer of NE entries (as subtree_cholesky_kernel!): zero, scatter A, extend-add the children, factor the front's first w columns with the pivoted LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ of front_ldlt_kernel!, update the trailing contribution block, write the unit-lower panel, D, piv, the pivot kinds and the statistics, then move the block down or (subtree root) write it to the update stack.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.subtree_ldlt_kernel!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.subtree_local_bytes-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"subtree_local_bytes(budget) -> Int\n\n@localmem bytes of the regime-A kernel of a budget class: the largest of SUBTREE_LOCAL_SIZES (8, 16, 32, 48, 64 KiB) that is ≤ budget, 0 below 8 KiB. The size is a Val parameter of the kernel, so a short list of instances serves every budget.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.subtree_local_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.subtree_local_layout-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.SupernodePartition, SparseDirectSolver.Schedule}","page":"Internals","text":"subtree_local_layout(sp, schedule) -> (local_front, local_cb, local_len)\n\nLocal-memory layout (1-based entry offsets) of the regime-A subtrees: a serial stack in each subtree's processing order. The packed front of v (f(f+1)/2 entries) starts at local_front[v], right above the contribution blocks of its children (which are the top of the stack, contiguous); after the factorization its packed m(m+1)/2 contribution block (the trailing triangle of the front) moves down to local_cb[v], the start of the first child's block, or of the front when it has no children. Subtree roots keep local_cb = 0 (their block goes to the update stack). local_len[t] is the peak of subtree t in entries (schedule.subtree_peak[t] / elsize).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.subtree_local_layout"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.subtree_local_reserve-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"subtree_local_reserve(structure) -> Int\n\nSUBTREE_LOCAL_RESERVE_LDLT for the LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ structures \"S\"/\"H\", else SUBTREE_LOCAL_RESERVE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.subtree_local_reserve"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.subtree_lu_kernel!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"subtree_lu_kernel!(backend, WG)(factor, ufactor, stack, ustack, info, stats, d, piv, pivot_kind, aux, nzval, amap,\n                                amap_ptr, amap_src, trees, bm, subtree_ptr, subtree_nodes, super_ptr, front_ptr,\n                                front_nrows, front_ncols, cb_ptr, local_front, local_cb, child_ptr, child_list,\n                                relind_ptr, relind, prm, Val(NE), Val(WG); ndrange = WG * count * bm.nact)\n\nLU kernel of regime A: workgroup G takes subtree t = trees[bm.first + g - 1] of batch member k and processes its supernodes in order with the serial stacks of the packed L and Uᵀ structures in two @localmem buffers of NE entries each (the layout of subtree_local_layout, shared by both): zero, scatter A, extend-add the children, factor the front's first w columns with the pivoted L D U of front_lu_kernel!, update the trailing contribution blocks, write the unit-lower panels, D, piv, the pivot kinds and the statistics, then move both blocks down or (subtree root) write them to the update stacks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.subtree_lu_kernel!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.supernode_partition","page":"Internals","text":"supernode_partition(P::SymmetricPattern, perm, opts::Options = Options()) -> SupernodePartition\n\nThe whole supernode step for the ordering perm (e.g. compute_ordering(P, opts).perm): etree, postorder, colcounts, fundamental_supernodes, amalgamate with opts.amalgamation unless opts.use_superpanels == 0, and the supernodal symbolic factorization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.supernode_partition"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.sweep_solve!-Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, SparseDirectSolver.SolveWorkspace, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, AbstractVecOrMat}","page":"Internals","text":"sweep_solve!(X, ws, symbolic, numeric, B; transposed = false, deterministic = false, impl = :auto,\n             transpose = false) -> X\n\nSolve A X = B with the factor P A Pᵀ = L Lᴴ (or the LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factor with its local pivot orders) of factorize!: permute_rhs! into ws.Y, forward_sweep!, diagonal_sweep!, backward_sweep!, unpermute_solution! into X. B and X are device arrays in the layouts of rhs_count (vector, n × nrhs matrix, strided vector; row-major with transposed = true), with at most max_rhs(ws) right-hand sides; X === B is allowed. Uniform batch: B and X hold the right-hand sides of all numeric.nbatch members (member after member), only the active members are solved. LU (\"G\"): transpose = true solves Aᵀ X = B. Asynchronous; allocates nothing on the device.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.sweep_solve!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.symbolic_analysis","page":"Internals","text":"symbolic_analysis(A::CSR, structure, view = 'F'; opts = Options(), T = eltype(A), max_local = typemax(Int))\n    -> Symbolic{Int, Vector{Int}}\n\nThe whole host analysis of PLAN §2.3: SymmetricPattern, compute_ordering (with the 2×2 pivot pair candidates or fixed pairs of analysis_pairs for \"S\"/\"H\"), supernode_partition on factor_pattern, build_schedule for element type T, build_layout and the maps of Symbolic. A.rowptr/A.colval are copied to the host once; A.nzval too when pairs are looked for (pairs_enabled), and only then. max_local: the bytes of local memory per workgroup of the backend the analysis is for (max_local_bytes), which bound the regime-A budgets (resolve_subtree_budgets); the default leaves them unbounded (host analyses, the CPU backend).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.symbolic_analysis"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.syrk!-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"syrk!(C, A, α, β; uplo = :L, impl = :auto) -> C\n\nSymmetric rank-k update of the uplo triangle of C: C ← α A Aᵀ + β C (plain transpose, also for complex T; see herk! for A Aᴴ). :vendor and :ka leave the other triangle untouched; :generic (mul!) overwrites it with the same symmetric update.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.syrk!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.takes_c_path-Tuple{Any, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"takes_c_path(schedule, s) -> Bool\n\nWhether supernode s runs on the regime-C path of the numeric phase (vendor or KA dense calls per front): regime C, or a regime-B bin wider than REGIME_B_MAX_WIDTH (no fused kernel).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.takes_c_path"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.tree_height-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"tree_height(parent) -> Vector{Int}\n\nHeight of every node of a forest given by parent (0 = root) with parent[s] > s: leaves 1, otherwise one more than the highest child.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.tree_height"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.tree_levels-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:Integer}}","page":"Internals","text":"tree_levels(parent) -> (height::Vector{Int}, nlevels::Int)\n\nHeight of every node of the forest parent (leaves have height 1, a node is one above its highest child) and the number of levels, the maximum height (0 for an empty forest). Nodes of equal height can be eliminated concurrently, so nlevels is the length of the critical path in the level schedule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.tree_levels"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.trsm!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, AbstractMatrix, AbstractMatrix}","page":"Internals","text":"trsm!(side, uplo, trans, diag, α, A, B; impl = :auto) -> B\n\nTriangular solve with multiple right-hand sides, BLAS trsm: B ← α op(A)⁻¹ B for side = :L, B ← α B op(A)⁻¹ for side = :R; A is lower (uplo = :L) or upper triangular, with unit diagonal if diag = :U; trans ∈ (:N, :T, :C).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.trsm!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.trsm_strided_batched!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}, AbstractArray{<:Any, 3}}","page":"Internals","text":"trsm_strided_batched!(side, uplo, trans, diag, α, A, B; impl = :auto) -> B\n\nUniform strided batch of trsm! on 3-D arrays A (n × n × count) and B. Implementations: :vendor, :ka.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.trsm_strided_batched!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.unpermute_solution!-Tuple{AbstractVecOrMat, AbstractMatrix, AbstractVector}","page":"Internals","text":"unpermute_solution!(X, Y, perm; transposed = false, conjugate = false, bm = nothing, scale = nothing) -> X\n\nX[perm[k], r] = Y[k, r] (conjugated when conjugate; times scale[perm[k]] when a scale vector is given): the inverse of permute_rhs!, from the first nrhs columns of Y into the user array X (same layouts; with a BatchMap bm, only the columns of the active members of X are written). One launch; asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.unpermute_solution!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.vendor_gemm!","page":"Internals","text":"vendor_gemm!(transA::Char, transB::Char, α, A, B, β, C) -> C\nvendor_syrk!(uplo::Char, α, A, β, C) -> C                  # uplo triangle of α A Aᵀ + β C\nvendor_herk!(uplo::Char, α, A, β, C) -> C                  # uplo triangle of α A Aᴴ + β C (complex)\nvendor_trsm!(side, uplo, trans, diag, α, A, B) -> B\nvendor_potrf!(uplo::Char, A) -> info::Int\nvendor_potrf_info!(uplo::Char, A, info, idx) -> info       # LAPACK info into the device Int32 vector info[idx]\nvendor_getrf!(A, ipiv) -> info::Int\nvendor_sytrf!(uplo::Char, A, ipiv) -> info::Int            # Bunch–Kaufman (capability probe only)\nvendor_gemm_strided_batched!(transA, transB, α, A, B, β, C) -> C     # 3-D arrays\nvendor_trsm_batched!(side, uplo, trans, diag, α, A, B) -> B          # 3-D arrays\nvendor_potrf_batched!(uplo, A, info) -> info                         # 3-D A, Int32 info\nvendor_getrf_batched!(A, ipiv, info) -> info                         # 3-D A, Int32 ipiv matrix\nvendor_batch_pointers(A) -> ptrs                                     # device vector of the member pointers of 3-D A\nvendor_trsm_batched_ptrs!(side, uplo, trans, diag, α, m, n, Aptrs, lda, Bptrs, ldb, count)\nvendor_potrf_batched_ptrs!(uplo, n, Aptrs, lda, info, count) -> info # Int32 info, count entries\n\nThe _ptrs variants take device vectors of member pointers built once by vendor_batch_pointers (the uniform-batch regime C, T17: no allocation in the numeric phase); vendor_trsm_batched!/vendor_potrf_batched! build them per call.\n\nVendor dense routines behind the impl = :vendor path of the dense interface. The generic methods throw NotSupportedError; backends add methods for their array types (host BLAS/LAPACK for Arrays, cuBLAS/cuSOLVER for CuArrays).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.vendor_gemm!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.zero_fronts!-Tuple{SparseDirectSolver.Numeric, SparseDirectSolver.Symbolic, Integer, Integer}","page":"Internals","text":"zero_fronts!(numeric, symbolic, first, count) -> numeric\n\nZero the panels and the update-stack contribution blocks of the count fronts symbolic.group_nodes[first:(first + count - 1)] (one workgroup per front). Asynchronous.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.zero_fronts!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/internals/#SparseDirectSolver.zero_pivot_pairs!-Tuple{Vector{Tuple{Int64, Int64}}, SparseDirectSolver.PairCandidates, SparseDirectSolver.SymmetricPattern, AbstractVector{<:Integer}, Real}","page":"Internals","text":"zero_pivot_pairs!(pairs, cands::PairCandidates, P::SymmetricPattern, perm, u) -> (added, unmatched)\n\nOne round of the pivot_pairs = \"default\" selection (issue #66): pair the 2×2 candidates whose pivot is structurally zero under the ordering perm (structural_zero_pivots), appending (partner, candidate) to pairs (kept sorted by candidate).\n\nWhy only those: every other candidate receives a structurally nonzero update of its diagonal from earlier columns before it is eliminated, and in-front pivoting deals with it. A structurally zero pivot stays zero (or tiny) whatever the values, so it needs a partner in its fully-summed block. On MadNLP K2 systems this pairs about a fifth of the candidates and costs 1.3–1.4× nnz(L) (instead of ~2× for every candidate, pivot_pairs), and leaves a few dozen perturbed pivots instead of about a thousand.\n\nPartners: a neighbour j that is in no pair yet with |aᵢⱼ| ≥ u · maxₖ≠ᵢ |aᵢₖ| (and aᵢⱼ ≠ 0), another candidate allowed (a [0 a; a 0] block is a good 2×2 pivot, the slack–dual case of K2 systems). The candidates are taken by increasing number of such free partners at the start of the round, then by position in perm; each takes the free partner with the largest |aᵢⱼ| (ties: earliest in perm), as a weak coupling makes a poor 2×2 pivot. Returns the number of pairs added and the number of structurally zero pivots left without a partner.\n\nPairing moves the partner, so the analysis re-orders on the compressed graph and calls it again until no pair is added (compute_ordering).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.zero_pivot_pairs!"},{"category":"section","location":"man/parameters/#Parameters","page":"Parameters","text":"Parameters are set with setparam! and read with getparam (or getparam! into a preallocated buffer), with the parameter strings of CUDSS.jl. They come in three groups.\n\nConfiguration parameters (CONFIG_PARAMETERS, cuDSS's cudssConfigParam_t): algorithm choices and tolerances, read by the next phase that uses them.\nData parameters (DATA_PARAMETERS and CUDSS08_DATA_PARAMETERS, cuDSS's cudssDataParam_t): inputs provided by the user (user_perm, user_schur_indices, user_host_interrupt, …) and outputs computed by the solver (info, inertia, diag, perm_row, …).\nExtensions (EXTRA_PARAMETERS): parameters cuDSS does not have, such as pivot_sign, pivot_stats, ir_mode and pivot_pairs.\n\nEvery parameter is validated when it is set: an unknown name raises an ArgumentError, a bad value an InvalidValueError, and a parameter that exists in cuDSS but is not implemented (the hybrid memory and multi-GPU modes, for example) a NotSupportedError.\n\nusing SparseDirectSolver\nopts = Options(ir_n_steps = 2, pivot_threshold = 0.1)\ngetparam(opts, \"ir_n_steps\"), getparam(opts, \"reordering_alg\")\n(2, \"default\")","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"man/parameters/#Configuration-parameters","page":"Parameters","text":"The fields of Options with their defaults are listed in its docstring. The ones that matter most in practice:\n\nparameter values effect\nreordering_alg \"default\", \"algo1\"–\"algo5\" automatic, AMD (\"algo3\"), nested dissection (\"algo4\", needs using Metis), natural (\"algo5\"); see SparseDirectSolver.ReorderingAlg\nfactorization_alg \"default\", \"algo1\", \"algo2\" automatic, small-front kernels only, vendor dense calls for large fronts\nmatching_alg \"default\" (none), \"algo1\"–\"algo6\" MC64 jobs 1–5 (\"algo6\": automatic) before the ordering\npivot_type 'A', 'N', 'D', 'L', 'B' automatic, none, diagonal, local block, Bunch–Kaufman; global pivoting ('C', 'R') is not supported\npivot_threshold 0.0–1.0, default 0.01 acceptance threshold of a pivot\npivot_epsilon default 1e-13 (Float64), 1e-5 (Float32) tiny pivots are perturbed to this size\nsolve_mode 0, 1, 2 solve with A, A^T or A^H\nir_n_steps, ir_tol integer, real iterative refinement steps and early-exit tolerance\nir_mode \"ir\", \"fgmres\" plain refinement or FGMRES (needs using Krylov)\ndeterministic_mode 0, 1 1 forbids the atomic forward solve\nschur_mode 0, 1 Schur complement mode","title":"Configuration parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"man/parameters/#Data-parameters","page":"Parameters","text":"The outputs and when they become available are tabulated in the docstring of getparam. For a uniform batch the per-factorization outputs are vectors with one entry per member.","title":"Data parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"man/parameters/#Differences-from-cuDSS","page":"Parameters","text":"\"info\" reports the original column of the first failed pivot.\ngetparam! replaces the set-buffer-then-get protocol of cuDSS for vector outputs.\npivot_sign chooses the sign of the perturbation of each row, so that a perturbed KKT system keeps the inertia an interior-point method expects.\npivot_stats returns (npos, nneg, nzero, nperturbed, n2x2) in one read.","title":"Differences from cuDSS"},{"category":"section","location":"man/cudss/#Migrating-from-CUDSS.jl","page":"Migrating from CUDSS.jl","text":"The handle API keeps the names of the parameters and phases of CUDSS.jl; the functions are renamed after Julia conventions.\n\nCUDSS.jl SparseDirectSolver.jl\nCudssSolver(A, structure, view) DirectSolver(A, structure, view)\ncudss(phase, solver, x, b) execute!(phase, solver, x, b)\ncudss_set(solver, name, value) setparam!(solver, name, value)\ncudss_get(solver, name) getparam(solver, name) (or getparam! into a buffer)\ncudss_update(solver, A) update!(solver, A)\nCudssMatrix, CudssData, CudssConfig CSR, MatrixDescriptor, Options\ncholesky, ldlt, lu on CuSparseMatrixCSR the same functions, see LinearAlgebra interface\n\nStructures (\"G\", \"S\", \"H\", \"SPD\", \"HPD\"), views ('L', 'U', 'F'), phase strings and parameter strings are identical, so a port is mostly a renaming:\n\n# CUDSS.jl\nsolver = CudssSolver(A_gpu, \"S\", 'L')\ncudss_set(solver, \"pivot_threshold\", 0.01)\ncudss(\"analysis\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)\ncudss(\"factorization\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)\ninertia = cudss_get(solver, \"inertia\")\ncudss(\"solve\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)\n\n# SparseDirectSolver.jl\nsolver = DirectSolver(A_gpu, \"S\", 'L')\nsetparam!(solver, \"pivot_threshold\", 0.01)\nexecute!(\"analysis\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)\nexecute!(\"factorization\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)\ninertia = getparam(solver, \"inertia\")\nexecute!(\"solve\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)","title":"Migrating from CUDSS.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"man/cudss/#Differences","page":"Migrating from CUDSS.jl","text":"cuDSS parameters that are not implemented (hybrid memory and execution modes, multiple devices, non-uniform batches) raise a NotSupportedError instead of being ignored.\n\"factorization_alg\" and \"solve_alg\" keep their spellings but select this package's own algorithms (see SparseDirectSolver.FactorizationAlg and SparseDirectSolver.SolveAlg).\nGlobal pivoting (pivot_type 'C'/'R') is not supported: pivots are chosen inside each front, and failed pivots are perturbed rather than delayed.\nExtensions beyond cuDSS: pivot_sign, pivot_stats, ir_mode = \"fgmres\", pivot_pairs, amalgamation, schedule (EXTRA_PARAMETERS).\nThe matrix and the vectors may live on any KernelAbstractions backend, not only CUDA.","title":"Differences"},{"category":"section","location":"man/backends/#Backends","page":"Backends","text":"The solver is written once in KernelAbstractions.jl. The matrix given to DirectSolver decides the backend: the CSR arrays are wrapped without a copy and every numeric phase runs where they live.\n\nbackend input dense kernels for large fronts status\nCPU (KernelAbstractions.CPU()) SparseMatrixCSC, CSR of Vectors KernelAbstractions kernels tested\nCUDA (using CUDA) CuSparseMatrixCSR, CuSparseMatrixCSC, CSR of CuArrays cuBLAS, cuSOLVER tested\nAMDGPU (using AMDGPU) ROCSparseMatrixCSR, ROCSparseMatrixCSC, CSR of ROCArrays rocBLAS, rocSOLVER tested (MI300X)\noneAPI, Metal CSR of device arrays KernelAbstractions kernels planned extensions\n\nto_backend moves a SparseMatrixCSC to a backend:\n\nusing KernelAbstractions\nA_dev = to_backend(A, CUDABackend())     # or CPU(), ROCBackend(), …\n\nThe kernels follow portability rules that let them run on every backend: 1-D workgroups, @localmem reductions and no warp intrinsics, no atomics in assembly (an atomic-free variant exists for the one atomic kernel, selected by deterministic_mode = 1), and no allocation or host synchronization inside the numeric and solve phases. The symbolic analysis runs on the host.\n\nElement types are Float32, Float64, ComplexF32 and ComplexF64; index types are Int32 and Int64.","title":"Backends"},{"category":"section","location":"lib/amdgpu/#AMDGPU-extension","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"Loaded with using AMDGPU. It accepts the sparse matrices of rocSPARSE, routes the dense operations of large fronts to rocBLAS and rocSOLVER, and lets the regime-A subtree kernels use the 64 KiB of local memory (LDS) per workgroup of AMD GPUs.","title":"AMDGPU extension"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSR-Union{Tuple{CSR{T, INT, <:ROCArray{INT, 1}, <:ROCArray{T}}}, Tuple{INT}, Tuple{T}} where {T, INT}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"ROCSparseMatrixCSR(A::CSR)\n\nThe stored CSR matrix of A (the transposed flag is not applied) as a ROCSparseMatrixCSR. One-based arrays are shared; zero-based index arrays are copied and rebased. Batches (nbatch(A) > 1) are rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSR"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#SparseDirectSolver.CSR-Tuple{AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSC}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"CSR(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSC)\n\nWrap A (m × n) without copying as the CSR of transpose(A) (n × m, transposed = true), sharing colPtr, rowVal and nzVal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.CSR"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#SparseDirectSolver.CSR-Tuple{AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSR}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"CSR(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR)\n\nWrap A without copying: the CSR shares rowPtr, colVal and nzVal (one-based, transposed = false).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.CSR"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#SparseDirectSolver.DirectSolver-Tuple{AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSR, Any, Any}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"DirectSolver(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR, structure::String, view::Char; index = 'O')\nDirectSolver(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSC, structure::String, view::Char; index = 'O')\n\nSolver on A's arrays, without copies (the ROCm twin of DirectSolver(::CuSparseMatrixCSR, …)). A ROCSparseMatrixCSC is read as the CSR of its transpose with the view flipped, see DirectSolver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.DirectSolver"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/amdgpu/#LinearAlgebra.cholesky","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"cholesky(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR, NoPivot(); view = 'F', check = false) -> DirectSolver\ncholesky(Symmetric(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR)) / cholesky(Hermitian(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR))\n\nLLᵀ/LLᴴ factorization of A on the AMD GPU; the wrappers pass their uplo as the view. See cholesky(::CSR).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cholesky"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#LinearAlgebra.ldlt-Tuple{AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSR}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"ldlt(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR; view = 'F', check = false) -> DirectSolver\nldlt(Symmetric(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR)) / ldlt(Hermitian(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR))\n\nLDLᵀ/LDLᴴ factorization of A on the AMD GPU; the wrappers pass their uplo as the view. See ldlt(::CSR).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.ldlt"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#LinearAlgebra.lu-Tuple{AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSR}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"lu(A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR; check = false) -> DirectSolver\n\nL D U factorization (structure \"G\") of A on the AMD GPU. See lu(::CSR).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.lu"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#SparseDirectSolver.max_local_bytes-Tuple{ROCBackend}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"max_local_bytes(::ROCBackend) -> Int\n\nThe local data share (LDS) of one workgroup on the current HIP device (hipDeviceAttributeMaxSharedMemoryPerBlock, 64 KiB on CDNA): the regime-A kernels may use the 64 KiB class there (issue #60).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.max_local_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#SparseDirectSolver.to_backend-Tuple{SparseMatrixCSC, ROCBackend}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"to_backend(A::SparseMatrixCSC, ::ROCBackend; index = 'O') -> CSR\n\nCSR arrays of A built on the host, then uploaded as ROCVectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.to_backend"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/amdgpu/#SparseDirectSolver.update!-Tuple{DirectSolver, Union{AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSC, AMDGPU.rocSPARSE.ROCSparseMatrixCSR}}","page":"AMDGPU extension","text":"update!(solver::DirectSolver, A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR)\nupdate!(solver::DirectSolver, A::ROCSparseMatrixCSC)\n\nPoint solver at the arrays of A, see update!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.update!"},{"category":"section","location":"lib/solver/#Solver","page":"Solver","text":"","title":"Solver"},{"category":"section","location":"lib/solver/#Parameters","page":"Solver","text":"","title":"Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"lib/solver/#Errors","page":"Solver","text":"","title":"Errors"},{"category":"module","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver","page":"Solver","text":"SparseDirectSolver\n\nPortable sparse direct solver (LLᵀ/LLᴴ, LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ, LDU) for GPUs on KernelAbstractions.jl, with the parameter names and phases of CUDSS.jl. See PLAN.md for the design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.AbstractDirectSolver","page":"Solver","text":"AbstractDirectSolver{T, INT} <: LinearAlgebra.Factorization{T}\n\nSupertype of the solvers of SparseDirectSolver.jl (≅ AbstractCudssSolver).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.AbstractDirectSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.DirectSolver","page":"Solver","text":"DirectSolver{T, INT, M, B, SY, NU, WS, RF, MS} <: AbstractDirectSolver{T, INT}\n\nSparse direct solver handle (≅ CudssSolver), PLAN §3.1–§3.2.\n\nDirectSolver(A::CSR, structure::String, view::Char; index = A.index)\nDirectSolver(rowptr, colval, nzval, structure::String, view::Char; index = 'O')\nDirectSolver(A::SparseMatrixCSC, structure::String, view::Char; index = 'O')\nDirectSolver(A::CuSparseMatrixCSR, structure, view; index = 'O')   # CUDA extension\nDirectSolver(A::CuSparseMatrixCSC, structure, view; index = 'O')   # CUDA extension\n\nstructure is \"G\", \"S\", \"H\", \"SPD\" or \"HPD\" and view is 'L', 'U' or 'F' (the triangle of the matrix that is read; 'F' reads the lower triangle of a symmetric or Hermitian structure, and for \"G\" the view is ignored and every stored entry is read, as in cuDSS), as in CUDSS.jl; index ('O'/'Z') is the base of the row pointers and column indices. The CSR arrays are wrapped without copies and live on a KernelAbstractions backend (the solver's backend), except for a SparseMatrixCSC, which is converted to host CSR arrays once. A CSC matrix (CuSparseMatrixCSC, or a CSR with transposed = true, e.g. from csr_of_transpose) is read as the CSR of its transpose with the view flipped ('L' ↔ 'U'); for complex Hermitian matrices that transpose is the conjugate, and the solve phases conjugate the right-hand side and the solution (as for solve_mode, see solve_conjugated).\n\nImplemented at this point: structures \"SPD\" (real T), \"HPD\", \"S\" (LDLᵀ; complex symmetric for complex T), \"H\" (LDLᴴ) and \"G\" (LU on the symmetric pattern of A + Aᵀ with row pivoting inside the fully-summed block of each front, P_r P A Pᵀ = L D U; any view, ignored), single matrices and uniform batches (see below), the phases \"reordering\", \"symbolic_factorization\", \"analysis\", \"factorization\", \"refactorization\", \"solve\", \"solve_fwd_perm\", \"solve_fwd\", \"solve_diag\", \"solve_bwd\", \"solve_bwd_perm\" and \"solve_refinement\" (execute!), and the Schur complement mode with \"solve_fwd_schur\" and \"solve_bwd_schur\" (below).\n\nUniform batch (PLAN §1.6, §3.5, ≅ CUDSS.jl's uniform batch): nbatch matrices with the pattern of rowptr/colval and values nzval, either a vector of nbatch · nnz entries (member after member) or an nnz × nbatch matrix; nbatch is deduced from the values (length(nzval) ÷ length(colval)), and \"ubatch_size\" must be 0 (deduced) or equal to it. One analysis serves every member; each member has its own factor, \"info\", \"inertia\", \"npivots\" and \"pivot_stats\" (vectors over the members). Right-hand sides and solutions hold nrhs columns per member, member after member: a strided vector of n · nrhs · nbatch entries, an n × (nrhs · nbatch) matrix, an n × nrhs × nbatch array or a MatrixDescriptor with nbatch. \"ubatch_index\" (0-based member, -1 = all) and \"ubatch_mask\" (one 0/1 flag per member) restrict the factorization and solve phases to a subset of the members: the other members' factors and solutions are left untouched. \"pivot_sign\" and the other options apply to every member; the 2×2 pivot pairs of \"S\"/\"H\" (\"pivot_pairs\") come from the first member's values.\n\nFields: A (the current CSR, re-pointed by update!), structure, view (as given), options (Options, set through setparam!), backend, nbatch, fresh_factorization (true until the first \"factorization\" after an analysis; the LinearAlgebra layer then switches cholesky! to \"refactorization\"), info (the \"info\" data parameter), the analysis (ordering, host host_symbolic, device symbolic), the numeric storage numeric, the solve workspace, the refinement storage refinement (allocated by the first solve that refines) and ir_steps (the \"ir_n_steps\" data parameter: refinement steps of the last solve, -1 before one) and schur (the SchurState of an analysis with schur_mode = 1, else nothing), host_matching (the host Matching of matching_alg ≠ \"default\", else nothing), analysis_colval (the column indices the analysis runs on: those of (Dr A Dc)[:, q] for \"G\" with matching, else host_colval) and matching (the device MatchingState, or nothing).\n\nMatching and scaling (PLAN §1.3, M9, \"matching_alg\" = \"algo1\"–\"algo6\" before the analysis; compute_matching, from the first batch member's values): \"G\" factors M = (Dr A Dc)[:, q], whose diagonal holds the matched entries (job 5: |mᵢᵢ| = 1 ≥ |mᵢⱼ|), and the symmetric structures factor D A D (same inertia as A; for \"S\"/\"H\" with \"algo5\"/\"algo6\" the 2×2 pivot pairs come from the cycles of the matching, matching_pairs; jobs 1–4 have no scaling and keep the pairs of \"pivot_pairs\" without matching). The solve phases apply the permutation and the scalings (\"solve_fwd_perm\": Dr, \"solve_bwd_perm\": Dc Q, as in cuDSS) and refinement measures the residual of A itself; \"diag\" and the pivot statistics are those of the scaled matrix. Not with the Schur complement mode.\n\nSchur complement mode (PLAN §3.6, ≅ CUDSS.jl's Schur complement): with \"schur_mode\" = 1 and \"user_schur_indices\" (n flags 0/1) set before the analysis, the flagged rows and columns are ordered last and the factorization stops before them: getparam(solver, \"schur_shape\") and getparam(solver, \"schur_matrix\") export S = A₂₂ − A₂₁ A₁₁⁻¹ A₁₂ (2 = the flagged block), and the solve phases are \"solve_fwd_schur\", \"solve_diag\" and \"solve_bwd_schur\" (see execute!). Single matrices only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.DirectSolver"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.execute!","page":"Solver","text":"execute!(phase::String, solver::DirectSolver, X, B; asynchronous = true) -> nothing\n\nExecute phase on solver (≅ cudss(phase, solver, x, b)), PLAN §1.2:\n\n\"reordering\": fill-reducing ordering on the host (reordering_alg, user_perm, …);\n\"symbolic_factorization\": supernodes, schedule, layout and device maps; allocates the numeric storage and the solve workspace (needs \"reordering\");\n\"analysis\": both;\n\"factorization\": numeric factorization with the current values of the matrix (needs the analysis); sets \"info\" (0 or, for \"SPD\"/\"HPD\", the original column of the first non-positive pivot; LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ and LU perturb tiny pivots instead and report 0, see \"npivots\", except for an exactly zero pivot with pivot_epsilon = 0, whose original column they report) and clears fresh_factorization;\n\"refactorization\": the same with the analysis and storage reused, \"info\" reset first (needs a previous \"factorization\");\n\"solve\": X = op(A)⁻¹ B (solve_mode 0: A, 1: Aᵀ, 2: Aᴴ), followed by ir_n_steps steps of iterative refinement; the sub-phases \"solve_fwd_perm\" (permute B into the workspace), \"solve_fwd\" (forward sweep), \"solve_diag\" (D⁻¹ of LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ, the identity for Cholesky), \"solve_bwd\" (backward sweep), \"solve_bwd_perm\" (inverse permutation into X) and \"solve_refinement\" (refine X in place, refine!) do the same in six calls with the same X, B (bitwise equal to \"solve\").\n\nX and B are only read by the solve phases (pass anything, e.g. nothing, to the others): vectors, n × nrhs matrices, strided vectors or MatrixDescriptors (row-major when transposed) on the solver's backend; X === B is allowed in every phase but \"solve_refinement\" (it needs the original B; \"solve\" keeps a copy of B when they alias). A failed factorization does not throw (as in cuDSS): check getparam(solver, \"info\"); solving with it gives garbage.\n\nRefinement (PLAN §2.5): each step computes the residual R = B - op(A) X with the solver's current matrix values (KA SpMV over the full pattern), and, when ir_tol > 0, stops once ‖Rₖ‖₂ ≤ ir_tol ‖Bₖ‖₂ for every right-hand side k (this costs one host synchronization per step; ir_tol = 0, the default, never stops early and never synchronizes), then adds the correction op(A)⁻¹ R. The steps performed are the data parameter \"ir_n_steps\" (getparam). With ir_mode = \"fgmres\" (needs using Krylov, else NotSupportedError) the refinement is FGMRES on the correction system, right-preconditioned by the factorization (fgmres_refine!): at most ir_n_steps iterations (one SpMV and one solve each, as a plain step), stopping once every relative residual is below ir_tol (ir_tol = 0: all ir_n_steps iterations); \"ir_n_steps\" then reports the FGMRES iterations.\n\nThe flag \"user_host_interrupt\" (a Threads.Atomic{Bool}, setparam!) is polled at the start of the analysis phases, between the launch groups of the factorization and between refinement steps: when set, the phase raises InterruptedError. An interrupted factorization leaves the solver analyzed (the next phase must be \"factorization\"); an interrupted refinement leaves the last completed iterate in X and reports the steps completed in \"ir_n_steps\".\n\nSchur complement mode (schur_mode = 1 at the analysis, PLAN §3.6, as in the CUDSS.jl docs): \"factorization\"/\"refactorization\" factor A₁₁ (the rows and columns outside \"user_schur_indices\") and assemble the Schur complement S (getparam \"schur_matrix\"). The solve phases then read B and write X (X === B allowed), with intermediate vectors in factor order, where the last ns entries are the Schur block in increasing original order:\n\n\"solve_fwd_schur\" (permutation and forward sweep): the last ns entries of X are the condensed right-hand side bₛ = b₂ − A₂₁ A₁₁⁻¹ b₁;\n\"solve_diag\": D⁻¹ of LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ on the factored part (the identity on the Schur block, and for Cholesky and LU, whose diagonal is applied by \"solve_bwd_schur\" as in cuDSS);\n\"solve_bwd_schur\" (backward sweep and inverse permutation): with the solution x₂ of S x₂ = bₛ in the last ns entries of B, X is the solution of A x = b.\n\nsolve_mode 1/2 solve with Aᵀ/Aᴴ (condensed system Sᵀ/Sᴴ); the other solve phases raise NotSupportedError in Schur mode, and the Schur phases without it FactorizationError. Unknown phase strings raise ArgumentError. Executing a phase before the phases it depends on raises FactorizationError. With asynchronous = false the backend is synchronized before returning (KernelAbstractions.synchronize). Phase summaries are logged (see SparseDirectSolver.set_log_level!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.execute!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.analyze!","page":"Solver","text":"analyze!(solver, X = nothing, B = nothing; asynchronous = true) -> solver\n\nexecute!(\"analysis\", solver, X, B; asynchronous).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.analyze!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.factorize!","page":"Solver","text":"factorize!(numeric, symbolic, nzval; impl = :auto, opts = Options()) -> info::Int\n\nStructures \"S\"/\"H\": factorize_ldlt! with the pivoting options opts (impl selects its regime-C GEMMs); structure \"G\": factorize_lu! (impl is not used). Both return info (nonzero only for a zero pivot with pivot_epsilon = 0). Structures \"SPD\"/\"HPD\": multifrontal Cholesky P A Pᵀ = L Lᴴ on the device: nzval are the stored values of A (same pattern, view and index base as the analysis) on the backend of numeric, and symbolic has its maps on that backend (adapt(backend, symbolic, INT)). For every launch group of the schedule, in order: a regime-A group (budget class) is one factorize_subtrees! launch (one workgroup per subtree: its fronts assembled, factored and extend-added in local memory, panels and the root's contribution block written to global memory); a regime-B group (width class W ≤ 64) is one factorize_fronts_b! launch (fused assembly, F11 Cholesky in local memory, trsm, syrk/herk into the front's contribution block); a regime-C group is one launch each of zero_fronts!, scatter_A! and extend_add! over its fronts, then per front potrf_info!, trsm! and herk!/syrk! (into the workspace, then pack_add! into its packed contribution block) through the dense interface (impl is passed on: :auto, :generic, :vendor, :ka, resolved once per call; :auto means :ka when the analysis used factorization_alg = \"algo1\", no vendor calls). Then cholesky_stats! and one read of the reduced status.\n\nReturns info = 0 on success, else the original column of the smallest non-positive pivot of the factor (as ref_factorize!); fronts above a failed one hold garbage. Uniform batch (numeric.nbatch > 1, PLAN §3.5): the active members of numeric (set_members!) are factorized by the same launches with the batch index as one more grid dimension (nzval holds the values of every member, member after member), a regime-C front by strided-batched potrf/trsm/gemm over each run of consecutive members (vendor potrf/trsm on the member pointers built at allocation; per member with impl = :generic); the return value is the first nonzero member info (see member_info! for all of them). opts.user_host_interrupt is polled before every launch group (a host read): when set, InterruptedError is raised and the factor is incomplete. Structure \"SPD\" (real) or \"HPD\". Nothing is allocated on the device; the panels are bitwise reproducible for a fixed impl and backend (regimes A and B are deterministic by construction).\n\n\n\n\n\nfactorize!(solver, X = nothing, B = nothing; asynchronous = true) -> solver\n\nexecute!(\"factorization\", solver, X, B; asynchronous).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.factorize!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.refactorize!","page":"Solver","text":"refactorize!(solver, X = nothing, B = nothing; asynchronous = true) -> solver\n\nexecute!(\"refactorization\", solver, X, B; asynchronous).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.refactorize!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.solve!","page":"Solver","text":"solve!(solver, X, B; asynchronous = true) -> X\n\nexecute!(\"solve\", solver, X, B; asynchronous).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.solve!"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.update!","page":"Solver","text":"update!(desc::MatrixDescriptor, x::AbstractArray) -> desc\n\nPoint desc at x without copying (≅ cudss_update(matrix, x)). x must hold nrows * ncols * nbatch entries of type T: either as a strided vector, or with the descriptor's shape ((nrows, ncols), (ncols, nrows) when transposed, plus a trailing nbatch dimension for 3-D arrays; a uniform batch also accepts the nrows × (ncols nbatch) matrix with the same memory, e.g. n × nbatch for MatrixDescriptor(T, n; nbatch), as CUDSS.jl). Anything else throws InvalidValueError.\n\n\n\n\n\nupdate!(solver::DirectSolver, A)\nupdate!(solver::DirectSolver, rowptr, colval, nzval)\n\nPoint solver at new matrix data without copying (≅ cudss_update): a CSR of the same type, a SparseMatrixCSC (converted on the host), a CuSparseMatrixCSR/CuSparseMatrixCSC (CUDA extension), or raw CSR arrays (with the index base and orientation of the solver's matrix). The sparsity pattern must be the one of the analysis (only the sizes are checked, as in cuDSS); new values are used by the next \"factorization\" or \"refactorization\". Raises InvalidValueError for a different size, number of stored entries, index base, orientation or array type.\n\n\n\n\n\nupdate!(solver::DirectSolver, A::CuSparseMatrixCSR)\nupdate!(solver::DirectSolver, A::CuSparseMatrixCSC)\n\nPoint solver at the arrays of A (≅ cudss_update(solver, A)), see update!.\n\n\n\n\n\nupdate!(solver::DirectSolver, A::ROCSparseMatrixCSR)\nupdate!(solver::DirectSolver, A::ROCSparseMatrixCSC)\n\nPoint solver at the arrays of A, see update!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.update!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.setparam!-Tuple{DirectSolver, AbstractString, Any}","page":"Solver","text":"setparam!(solver::DirectSolver, name::String, value)\n\nSet a configuration or user-input data parameter of solver (≅ cudss_set): everything setparam!(::Options, …) accepts, stored in solver.options and used by the next phase that reads it, plus the data parameter \"info\" (an integer, or for a uniform batch an integer for every member or a vector of nbatch integers; CUDSS.jl resets it before a refactorization). Uniform batch: \"ubatch_size\" (0 or the batch size), \"ubatch_index\" (0-based member, -1 = all) and \"ubatch_mask\" (nbatch flags 0/1, or nothing) select the members of the next phases; the index and the mask are checked against the batch size when a phase runs. \"pivot_sign\" (PLAN §1.7: a vector of n entries in (-1, 0, 1), host or device, or nothing) is checked against the size of the matrix; the next \"factorization\"/\"refactorization\" copies it to the device; it steers the perturbation of LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ only and is ignored for \"G\" (LU perturbs with the sign of the pivot) and the Cholesky structures. Setting \"ir_n_steps\" (the number of refinement steps requested) makes getparam report that value again until the next solve. \"schur_matrix\" (Schur complement mode, after \"reordering\"/\"analysis\") registers the destination that getparam(solver, \"schur_matrix\") fills (≅ cudss_set(solver, \"schur_matrix\", S) then cudss_get): a dense ns × ns matrix on the solver's backend (or a MatrixDescriptor holding one), a CSR (or a backend CSR matrix, e.g. CuSparseMatrixCSR) with ns + 1 row pointers and room for exactly the entries of the exported pattern, or a tuple (csr, view) with view 'L', 'U' or 'F' (the default) to export one triangle; nothing unregisters it. Computed data parameters (\"lu_nnz\", \"diag\", \"perm_row\", …) cannot be set (ArgumentError): read them with getparam or getparam!, which replaces cuDSS's set-buffer-then-get protocol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.setparam!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.getparam-Tuple{DirectSolver, AbstractString}","page":"Solver","text":"getparam(solver::DirectSolver, name::String)\n\nValue of a parameter of solver (≅ cudss_get). Configuration and user-input parameters come from solver.options (getparam(::Options, …)). The data parameters computed by the solver:\n\nname value available after\n\"info\" Int: 0, or the original column (1-based) of the first failed pivot (Cholesky: non-positive; LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ and LU: exactly zero with pivot_epsilon = 0, the other tiny pivots are perturbed) always\n\"ubatch_mask\" the \"ubatch_mask\" set (or nothing) always\n\"ir_n_steps\" Int: refinement steps performed by the last \"solve\"/\"solve_refinement\" (≤ the configured ir_n_steps, fewer when ir_tol stopped early); the configured value before the first solve and after setparam!(solver, \"ir_n_steps\", k) always\n\"lu_nnz\" Int64: nonzeros of L (diagonal included, amalgamation zeros excluded) analysis\n\"flops\" Float64: factorization flops of the stored panels analysis\n\"nsuperpanels\" Int: supernodes after amalgamation analysis\n\"memory_estimates\" Vector{Int64} (16 entries, see memory_estimates) analysis\n\"perm_reorder_row\", \"perm_reorder_col\" Vector{Int}: the fill-reducing permutation, 1-based (perm[k] = original index of the k-th pivot) reordering\n\"perm_row\", \"perm_col\" Vector{Int}: the final permutation of the factor (= the reordering for Cholesky and LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ); LU (\"G\") after a factorization: perm_row[k] is the original row of factor row k (the reordering composed with the local row interchanges of batch member 1), perm_col the reordering (with matching: composed with the matching, perm_matching[reordering]), so A[perm_row, perm_col] = L D U (with matching Dr[perm_row] A[perm_row, perm_col] Dc[perm_col] = L D U, the scalings Dr, Dc of \"scale_row\"/\"scale_col\" as diagonal matrices in the original numbering). For a CSC input (CuSparseMatrixCSC, csr_of_transpose) A is the stored CSR matrix, the transpose of the matrix given analysis\n\"perm_matching\" Vector{Int}: the matching permutation (Matching): row i is matched to column perm_matching[i] (\"G\": A[:, perm_matching] has the matched entries on its diagonal; symmetric structures: only its cycles are used, for the 2×2 pivot pairs) reordering (matching_alg ≠ \"default\")\n\"scale_row\", \"scale_col\" Vector{real(T)}: the row and column scaling factors Dr, Dc of the factored matrix, in the original numbering (ones for \"algo1\"–\"algo4\"; equal for the symmetric structures) reordering (matching_alg ≠ \"default\")\n\"diag\" vector of T on the solver's backend: the diagonal of L (Cholesky), of D (LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ; for a 2×2 block its two diagonal entries) or of D in L D U (LU, = the diagonal of U in L U) in factor order; Schur complement mode: 1 on the (unfactored) Schur block factorization\n\"npivots\" INT: perturbed pivots (LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ, LU; 0 for Cholesky); valid when \"info\" is 0 (after a failed front the counts of the fronts above it are stale) factorization\n\"inertia\" Tuple{INT, INT}: (npos, nneg) of D (after perturbation, so the inertia of A + E; read it with \"npivots\"); (0, 0) for complex symmetric \"S\" and for \"G\"; Cholesky: (number of positive pivots, 0); valid when \"info\" is 0, as \"npivots\" factorization\n\"pivot_stats\" NamedTuple (npos, nneg, nzero, nperturbed, n2x2) of Int64 (PLAN §1.7); in Schur complement mode \"npivots\", \"inertia\" and \"pivot_stats\" count the factored block A₁₁ only; valid when \"info\" is 0, as \"npivots\" factorization\n\"schur_shape\" (nrows, ncols, nnz) of Int64: the size ns of the Schur complement and the nonzeros of its symbolic pattern (schur_pattern); for the symmetric structures one triangle with the diagonal (as the sparse export of one triangle) analysis (schur_mode = 1)\n\"schur_matrix\" the Schur complement S = A₂₂ − A₂₁ A₁₁⁻¹ A₁₂ of the rows and columns of \"user_schur_indices\" (in their increasing order), written into the destination registered with setparam! and returned; without one, a new dense ns × ns matrix on the solver's backend. Symmetric structures: a dense destination gets the full matrix, a sparse one the triangle of its view factorization (schur_mode = 1)\n\nUniform batch (nbatch > 1): \"info\", \"npivots\", \"inertia\" and \"pivot_stats\" are vectors with one entry per batch member, \"diag\" is the n · nbatch vector of the members' diagonals one after the other; the analysis outputs are shared by the members. A factorization restricted by \"ubatch_index\"/\"ubatch_mask\" updates the active members only: the others keep the \"info\", \"npivots\", \"inertia\" and \"pivot_stats\" of their last factorization. \"perm_row\" of an LU batch is batch member 1's row order, by design: cuDSS returns the vector outputs of a uniform batch (\"diag\") for member 1 only and does not implement \"perm_row\" at all (cuDSS 0.8). \"diag\" is the deliberate exception and returns every member.\n\nThe pivot statistics are reduced on the device (reduce_stats!) and copied to the host when read (one synchronization). The reordering permutation after \"reordering\" alone is the ordering algorithm's; \"symbolic_factorization\" composes it with the supernodal renumbering, and from then on both permutations are the one the factor uses. Data parameters of later tasks (\"nd_partition_tree\", \"hybrid_device_memory_min\") raise NotSupportedError; reading one before the phase that computes it raises FactorizationError, and the matching outputs without matching InvalidValueError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.getparam"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.getparam!","page":"Solver","text":"getparam!(buffer, solver::DirectSolver, name::String) -> buffer\n\nWrite the vector-valued data parameter name (\"perm_reorder_row\", \"perm_reorder_col\", \"perm_row\", \"perm_col\", \"diag\", \"memory_estimates\", \"user_perm\") of getparam into buffer, a host or device vector of the right length (converted to its element type), without the C-style set-buffer-then-get protocol of CUDSS.jl. \"schur_matrix\": buffer is any destination setparam! accepts for it (dense matrix, CSR, (csr, view)), filled without registering it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.getparam!"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.Options","page":"Solver","text":"Options(; kwargs...)\n\nEvery configuration parameter of PLAN §1.3 and §1.7 plus the user-provided data parameters (user_perm, user_schur_indices, user_nd_partition_tree, user_host_interrupt, ubatch_mask, pivot_sign), in typed fields with the defaults below. Keyword arguments are applied through setparam!, so Options(ir_n_steps = 2) validates like setparam!(opts, \"ir_n_steps\", 2).\n\nField Default Meaning of the default\nreordering_alg REORDERING_DEFAULT automatic AMD/ND choice\nfactorization_alg FACTORIZATION_DEFAULT automatic regime choice\nsolve_alg SOLVE_DEFAULT level-batched sweeps\nmatching_alg MATCHING_NONE no matching\nsolve_mode 0 solve with A (1: Aᵀ, 2: Aᴴ)\nir_n_steps 0 no refinement (cuDSS parity)\nir_tol 0.0 no early exit\npivot_type PIVOT_AUTO Bunch–Kaufman for S/H, none for SPD/HPD\npivot_threshold 0.01 pivot acceptance threshold; with pivot_pairs = \"default\" also the partner threshold of the 2×2 pairs chosen at analysis (a value set after \"analysis\" changes the in-front test, not the pairs already chosen)\npivot_epsilon nothing default_pivot_epsilon(T)\npivot_epsilon_alg PIVOT_EPSILON_DEFAULT \nmax_lu_nnz -1 no limit (any negative value)\nhybrid_memory_mode 0 device memory only\nhybrid_device_memory_limit 0 automatic\nuse_cuda_register_memory 1 pinned host memory allowed\nhost_nthreads 0 Threads.nthreads()\nhybrid_execute_mode 0 device execution only\nnd_nlevels 10 minimum nested-dissection levels\nubatch_size 0 deduced from the matrix values (1 for a single matrix)\nubatch_index -1 all batch members\nuse_superpanels 1 supernode amalgamation on\nschur_mode 0 off\ndeterministic_mode 0 atomic forward solve allowed\nnd_ubfactor -1 ordering library default\nir_mode IR_PLAIN plain iterative refinement\nfactor_precision nothing factors in the input precision\namalgamation (max_width = 32, zero_fraction = 0.25, min_width = 8) \nschedule SCHEDULE_AUTO \npivot_pairs PIVOT_PAIRS_DEFAULT 2×2 pivot pairs in the analysis of \"S\"/\"H\" (structurally zero pivots; \"all\": every candidate; with matching \"algo5\"/\"algo6\": from the cycles of the matching); decided from the values present at analysis: an all-zero nzval gives no pairs, an undefined one arbitrary pairs\npivot_pair_tolerance 1e-6 (PIVOT_PAIR_TOLERANCE) relative diagonal size below which a row is a 2×2 candidate\nregime_c_width 64 fronts wider than this go to regime C (vendor dense calls)\nregime_c_rows 512 fronts with more rows than this go to regime C\nsubtree_budgets [16384, 32768, 49152] regime A local-memory budgets in bytes (kernel classes of 8 to 64 KiB); the defaults are clamped to the backend's local memory per workgroup, an explicit budget above it is an InvalidValueError; empty disables regime A\nsubtree_parallelism 4096 a regime-A subtree does at most 1/subtree_parallelism of the factorization flops (0: no limit)\nsubtree_max_fronts 0 a regime-A subtree has at most this many fronts (0: no limit); the serial walk of a subtree is its workgroup's critical path\nmemory_budget -1 update-stack bytes per level chunk (negative: no limit, no chunking)\nuser_perm, user_schur_indices, user_nd_partition_tree, ubatch_mask, pivot_sign nothing not provided\nuser_host_interrupt nothing not provided\n\nVectors are stored as host copies; user_host_interrupt is stored by reference because it is polled while a phase runs.\n\nThe last five rows are analysis tuning knobs of the schedule (PLAN §2.3 step 5, TASKS T07). They are not cuDSS parameter strings, so setparam! does not know them; set them with Options(; regime_c_width = 128) (validated) or by assigning the field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.Options"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.setparam!-Tuple{Options, AbstractString, Any}","page":"Solver","text":"setparam!(opts::Options, name::String, value)\n\nSet the configuration or user-input data parameter name (a CUDSS.jl name from CONFIG_PARAMETERS/DATA_PARAMETERS or a name from EXTRA_PARAMETERS) after validating value.\n\nAccepted values:\n\nalgorithms (\"reordering_alg\", \"factorization_alg\", \"solve_alg\", \"matching_alg\", \"pivot_epsilon_alg\"): \"default\", \"algoN\" or the integer N;\n\"pivot_type\": 'A', 'N', 'D', 'L', 'B' ('C'/'R' raise NotSupportedError); LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ: 'A', 'B', 'L' Bunch–Kaufman, 'D' 1×1 only, 'N' none; LU (\"G\", lu_pivoting): 'A', 'B', 'L' in-block threshold row pivoting, 'N', 'D' diagonal pivots;\ninteger and flag parameters: an Integer in the documented range;\n\"ir_tol\", \"pivot_threshold\", \"pivot_epsilon\": a finite real ≥ 0 (nothing resets \"pivot_epsilon\" to default_pivot_epsilon);\n\"ir_mode\": \"ir\" or \"fgmres\"; \"schedule\": \"auto\", \"subtree+level\", \"syncfree\";\n\"pivot_pairs\" (beyond cuDSS): \"default\" (for \"S\"/\"H\", a row with a zero or negligible diagonal whose pivot would be structurally zero is ordered together with a 2×2 pivot partner, see zero_pivot_pairs!), \"all\" (every such row, see pivot_pairs; about 2× nnz(L) on KKT systems) or \"none\"; ignored for the other structures, with user_perm and with the natural ordering; with matching_alg = \"algo5\"/\"algo6\" the pairs come from the cycles of the scaled symmetric matching instead (matching_pairs). Pairs are decided from the values present at \"analysis\" (the first batch member): an all-zero nzval gives no pairs and an undefined one arbitrary pairs, so run \"analysis\" after the first assembly of the matrix (MadNLP: after the first KKT assembly); \"all\" reads the values as well (its candidates and partners come from them);\n\"pivot_threshold\" also shapes the ordering with pivot_pairs = \"default\": partners are accepted at analysis down to pivot_threshold · maxₖ |aᵢₖ| (zero_pivot_pairs!); a value set after \"analysis\" changes the in-front pivot test but not the pairs already chosen;\n\"pivot_pair_tolerance\" (beyond cuDSS): a finite real ≥ 0, the relative diagonal size τ below which a row is a 2×2 candidate (|aᵢᵢ| ≤ τ maxⱼ≠ᵢ |aᵢⱼ|, on the scaled matrix with matching; default PIVOT_PAIR_TOLERANCE);\n\"factor_precision\": Float32, Float64 or nothing;\n\"amalgamation\": a NamedTuple with any of max_width, zero_fraction, min_width;\n\"user_perm\", \"user_nd_partition_tree\": an integer vector (host or device), or nothing;\n\"user_schur_indices\", \"ubatch_mask\": a vector of 0/1 flags, or nothing;\n\"pivot_sign\": a vector with entries in (-1, 0, 1), or nothing;\n\"user_host_interrupt\": a Threads.Atomic{Bool}, or nothing.\n\nUnknown names raise ArgumentError; values of the wrong type or range raise InvalidValueError; \"device_count\" ≠ 1, \"device_indices\", \"comm_device\", \"comm_host\" and global pivoting raise NotSupportedError. Parameters computed by a solver (\"lu_nnz\", \"inertia\", …) cannot be set (ArgumentError). The hybrid-mode parameters are stored but have no effect yet; a non-default value warns once. reordering_alg = \"algo1\"/\"algo2\" (COLAMD-based orderings with global pivoting) is accepted and warns once that the symmetric-pattern path is used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.setparam!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.getparam-Tuple{Options, AbstractString}","page":"Solver","text":"getparam(opts::Options, name::String)\n\nValue of the configuration or user-input data parameter name, in the form setparam! accepts: algorithm and mode strings (\"algo3\", \"fgmres\"), Char for \"pivot_type\", Int/Float64 for numbers, a copy of stored vectors, nothing for unset optional values (\"pivot_epsilon\", \"factor_precision\", user vectors). \"device_count\" is always 1.\n\nUnknown names raise ArgumentError, as do data parameters computed by a solver (query those on the solver). \"device_indices\", \"comm_device\" and \"comm_host\" raise NotSupportedError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.getparam"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.CONFIG_PARAMETERS","page":"Solver","text":"CONFIG_PARAMETERS\n\nThe configuration parameter names of CUDSS.jl (CUDSS_CONFIG_PARAMETERS), verbatim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.CONFIG_PARAMETERS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.DATA_PARAMETERS","page":"Solver","text":"DATA_PARAMETERS\n\nThe data parameter names of CUDSS.jl (CUDSS_DATA_PARAMETERS), verbatim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.DATA_PARAMETERS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.CUDSS08_DATA_PARAMETERS","page":"Solver","text":"CUDSS08_DATA_PARAMETERS\n\ncuDSS 0.8 data parameters that CUDSS.jl does not list in DATA_PARAMETERS (PLAN §1.4): \"ir_n_steps\" (refinement steps performed; the configuration parameter of the same name is the number requested), \"ubatch_mask\" and \"flops\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.CUDSS08_DATA_PARAMETERS"},{"category":"constant","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.EXTRA_PARAMETERS","page":"Solver","text":"EXTRA_PARAMETERS\n\nParameters beyond cuDSS (PLAN §1.7): the data parameters \"pivot_sign\" (input) and \"pivot_stats\" (output), and the configuration parameters \"ir_mode\", \"factor_precision\", \"amalgamation\", \"schedule\", \"pivot_pairs\" and \"pivot_pair_tolerance\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.EXTRA_PARAMETERS"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.default_pivot_epsilon","page":"Solver","text":"default_pivot_epsilon(T) -> Float64\n\nDefault pivot_epsilon for element type T, as in cuDSS: 1e-5 for Float32/ComplexF32, 1e-13 for Float64/ComplexF64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.default_pivot_epsilon"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.set_log_level!","page":"Solver","text":"SparseDirectSolver.set_log_level!(level) -> Int\n\nSet the verbosity of the solver's log messages: 0/\"none\" (default; the messages are @debug records), 1/\"info\" (phase summaries: analysis, factorization, refinement steps performed, as @info), 2/\"debug\" (also the relative residual of every refinement step). The initial value comes from the environment variable SDS_LOG_LEVEL. Returns the previous level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.set_log_level!"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.SparseDirectSolverError","page":"Solver","text":"SparseDirectSolverError <: Exception\n\nAbstract supertype of the exceptions raised by SparseDirectSolver.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.SparseDirectSolverError"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.NotSupportedError","page":"Solver","text":"NotSupportedError(msg)\n\nA parameter, value or feature that SparseDirectSolver.jl does not provide (for example \"device_count\" > 1, or global pivoting pivot_type = 'C').\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.NotSupportedError"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.InvalidValueError","page":"Solver","text":"InvalidValueError(msg)\n\nA value of the wrong type or outside the admissible range for a parameter, structure, view, index base or phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.InvalidValueError"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.FactorizationError","page":"Solver","text":"FactorizationError(info, msg = \"\")\n\nA factorization that could not be completed, or a phase called out of order. info follows the \"info\" data parameter: k > 0 is the first failed pivot (1-based, original numbering); for uniform batches it is a vector with one entry per member.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.FactorizationError"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/solver/#SparseDirectSolver.InterruptedError","page":"Solver","text":"InterruptedError(msg = \"interrupted through \"user_host_interrupt\"\")\n\nRaised when the \"user_host_interrupt\" flag is set while a phase runs. The solver stays usable: the phase can be executed again once the flag is cleared.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.InterruptedError"},{"category":"section","location":"lib/matrices/#Matrices","page":"Matrices","text":"update! also replaces the data of a MatrixDescriptor.","title":"Matrices"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/matrices/#SparseDirectSolver.CSR","page":"Matrices","text":"CSR{T,INT,VI,VT}\n\nCompressed sparse row matrix on any backend: rowptr::VI, colval::VI (VI <: AbstractVector{INT}), nzval::VT (VT <: AbstractVecOrMat{T}), nrows, ncols, index::IndexBase and transposed::Bool.\n\nindex is the base of rowptr/colval (INDEX_ONE or INDEX_ZERO); the arrays are never rebased in place.\ntransposed = true means the stored CSR arrays describe transpose(M) of the matrix M the caller means (for example a CSC matrix reinterpreted as the CSR of its transpose, see csr_of_transpose). size, nnz and SparseMatrixCSC(A) refer to the stored CSR matrix; consumers honor the flag.\nA uniform batch shares rowptr/colval; its values are either a vector of length nbatch * nnz (members one after the other) or an nnz × nbatch matrix.\n\nConstructors:\n\nCSR(rowptr, colval, nzval[, nrows, ncols]; index = 'O', transposed = false)\nCSR(A::SparseMatrixCSC; index = 'O')\n\nThe first wraps the arrays without copying (square nrows = ncols = length(rowptr) - 1 when the sizes are omitted); the second builds the CSR arrays of A on the host. Only lengths are validated, never array contents, so no device memory is read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.CSR"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/matrices/#SparseDirectSolver.csr_of_transpose","page":"Matrices","text":"csr_of_transpose(A::SparseMatrixCSC) -> CSR\n\nZero-copy reinterpretation of the CSC matrix A (m × n) as the CSR matrix of transpose(A) (n × m): A.colptr becomes rowptr, A.rowval becomes colval, the arrays are shared and transposed = true records that the caller means A. This is how MadNLP passes colPtr/rowVal (PLAN §1.1). A solver on such a matrix reads it with the view flipped ('L' ↔ 'U'; for \"G\" the view is ignored). Raises InvalidValueError when A.rowval or A.nzval is longer than nnz(A) (a SparseMatrixCSC may keep spare capacity, which the shared CSR arrays cannot describe).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.csr_of_transpose"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/matrices/#SparseDirectSolver.to_backend","page":"Matrices","text":"to_backend(A, backend) -> CSR\n\nA (a SparseMatrixCSC or a CSR) as a CSR whose arrays live on the KernelAbstractions backend. A SparseMatrixCSC is first converted on the host (index keyword as in CSR(A; index)); arrays already on backend are not copied.\n\n\n\n\n\nto_backend(A::SparseMatrixCSC, ::CUDABackend; index = 'O') -> CSR\n\nCSR arrays of A built on the host, then uploaded as CuVectors.\n\n\n\n\n\nto_backend(A::SparseMatrixCSC, ::ROCBackend; index = 'O') -> CSR\n\nCSR arrays of A built on the host, then uploaded as ROCVectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.to_backend"},{"category":"function","location":"lib/matrices/#SparseDirectSolver.nbatch","page":"Matrices","text":"nbatch(A::CSR)\nnbatch(desc::MatrixDescriptor)\n\nNumber of matrices in a uniform batch: size(nzval, 2) for matrix values, length(nzval) ÷ nnz for vector values (1 for a plain matrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.nbatch"},{"category":"type","location":"lib/matrices/#SparseDirectSolver.MatrixDescriptor","page":"Matrices","text":"MatrixDescriptor{T,A}\n\nDense right-hand side / solution descriptor (≅ CudssMatrix for dense data): data::Union{Nothing,A}, nrows, ncols, nbatch, transposed. nrows × ncols is the logical size of one batch member. With transposed = true the data is row-major, i.e. a column-major array of size (ncols, nrows), as in CUDSS.jl.\n\nMatrixDescriptor(T, n; nbatch = 1)                       # n × 1, no data yet\nMatrixDescriptor(T, m, n; nbatch = 1, transposed = false) # as CUDSS.jl: transposed gives n × m\nMatrixDescriptor(x::AbstractArray; transposed = false)    # vector, matrix or (n, p, nbatch) array\n\nupdate! re-points the descriptor to a new buffer without copying. Descriptors created without data accept any AbstractArray{T}; descriptors created from an array accept arrays of the same type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseDirectSolver.MatrixDescriptor"},{"category":"section","location":"man/performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"Time of SparseDirectSolver.jl divided by the time of cuDSS per feature and phase (BenchmarkTools medians), geometric mean over the benchmark matrices (below 1 is faster). Features whose task is not done yet are empty.\n\n(Image: SparseDirectSolver.jl vs cuDSS time ratio per feature)\n\nThe per-matrix numbers are in bench/comparison/comparison.md. The benchmark harness and the cuDSS baselines are described in bench/README.md; the comparison is regenerated by hand:\n\njulia --project=bench bench/compare.jl --solver=cudss\njulia --project=bench bench/compare.jl --solver=sds --backend=cuda\njulia --project=bench/report bench/compare_report.jl","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"#SparseDirectSolver.jl","page":"Home","text":"SparseDirectSolver.jl is a portable sparse direct solver for GPUs, written in Julia on KernelAbstractions.jl and GPUArrays.jl. It factors\n\nsymmetric and Hermitian positive definite matrices: A = L L^T / L L^H (structures \"SPD\", \"HPD\"),\nsymmetric and Hermitian indefinite matrices: A = L D L^T / L D L^H with 1×1 and 2×2 pivots (structures \"S\", \"H\"),\ngeneral matrices: A = L D U with threshold partial pivoting (structure \"G\"),\n\nand solves with them on the GPU. It keeps the parameter names and the phases of CUDSS.jl, the Julia interface of NVIDIA cuDSS, so that MadNLP and other cuDSS users can switch to it mechanically (Migrating from CUDSS.jl). Unlike cuDSS it is open source and not tied to one vendor: it runs on CUDA, AMDGPU, oneAPI, Metal and the KernelAbstractions CPU backend.\n\nwarning: Status\nVersion 0.1 is under construction. The CPU backend, CUDA and AMDGPU are tested; the oneAPI and Metal extensions, non-uniform batches and mixed precision are not there yet. Unsupported structures, phases and parameters raise NotSupportedError rather than falling back silently.","title":"SparseDirectSolver.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Analysis on the host: AMD or nested dissection (METIS, through the Metis extension) ordering, elimination tree, supernodes with GPU-tuned amalgamation, a static factor layout and device assembly maps. For symmetric indefinite matrices the analysis pairs structurally zero pivots with a partner so that the in-front pivoting can form the 2×2 blocks of KKT systems.\nMultifrontal factorization on the device in three regimes: fused subtree-per-workgroup kernels for the many small fronts at the bottom of the elimination tree, level-batched per-front kernels for medium fronts, and dense potrf/getrf/trsm/syrk calls (cuBLAS and cuSOLVER on CUDA, KernelAbstractions kernels elsewhere) for large fronts.\nPivoting: Bunch–Kaufman 1×1/2×2 pivoting for LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ, threshold partial pivoting inside the fully-summed block of each front for LU, static perturbation of tiny pivots with a user-chosen sign per row (pivot_sign, which cuDSS does not have), inertia and pivot statistics.\nSolves with multiple right-hand sides, transposed and conjugated systems (solve_mode), iterative refinement or FGMRES refinement (with Krylov.jl), and every solve sub-phase of cuDSS.\nSchur complement mode, uniform batches, matching and scaling (MC64 jobs 1–5).\nA LinearAlgebra layer: cholesky, ldlt, lu, their in-place variants, ldiv!, \\, logabsdet.","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"The package is not registered yet. Julia 1.13 or later is required.\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/exanauts/SparseDirectSolver.jl\")\n\nLoading CUDA.jl enables the CUDA extension (CuSparseMatrixCSR and CuSparseMatrixCSC inputs, cuBLAS and cuSOLVER dense kernels); loading AMDGPU.jl enables the AMDGPU extension (ROCSparseMatrixCSR and ROCSparseMatrixCSC inputs, rocBLAS and rocSOLVER dense kernels). Loading Metis.jl enables nested dissection ordering; Krylov.jl enables FGMRES refinement.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Quick start: factor and solve on the CPU backend and on CUDA.\nPhases and Parameters: the handle API, phase by phase.\nSymmetric indefinite systems: KKT matrices, pivoting and inertia.\nPerformance: comparison with cuDSS.\nThe design documents in the repository: PLAN.md (architecture and API), TASKS.md (implementation tasks and their reports) and RESEARCH.md (state of the art).","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#Quick-start","page":"Quick start","text":"","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#On-the-CPU-backend","page":"Quick start","text":"A DirectSolver is created from a sparse matrix, a structure string and the triangle that is read. A SparseMatrixCSC is converted to CSR arrays on the host and factored on the KernelAbstractions CPU backend.\n\nusing SparseDirectSolver, SparseArrays, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(666)\n\nn = 500\nA = sprand(n, n, 0.01); A = A * A' + n * I    # symmetric positive definite\nb = rand(n); x = similar(b)\n\nsolver = DirectSolver(tril(A), \"SPD\", 'L')\nexecute!(\"analysis\", solver, x, b)            # ordering + symbolic factorization\nexecute!(\"factorization\", solver, x, b)\nexecute!(\"solve\", solver, x, b)\ngetparam(solver, \"info\"), norm(A * x - b) / norm(b)\n(0, 2.8856135147957805e-16)\n\nThe analysis results can be inspected before the numeric phases run:\n\ngetparam(solver, \"lu_nnz\"), getparam(solver, \"nsuperpanels\")\n(52630, 49)\n\nNew values with the same pattern reuse the analysis:\n\nA2 = A + 2I\nupdate!(solver, tril(A2))\nexecute!(\"refactorization\", solver, x, b)\nexecute!(\"solve\", solver, x, b)\nnorm(A2 * x - b) / norm(b)\n2.806183546299379e-16","title":"On the CPU backend"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#On-CUDA","page":"Quick start","text":"With CUDA.jl loaded, the solver takes a CuSparseMatrixCSR (or CuSparseMatrixCSC) and right-hand sides in CuArrays; every numeric phase runs on the device.\n\nusing SparseDirectSolver, SparseArrays, LinearAlgebra, Random\nusing CUDA, CUDA.CUSPARSE\nRandom.seed!(666)\n\nn = 2000\nA = sprand(n, n, 0.002); A = A * A' + n * I\nA_gpu = CuSparseMatrixCSR(tril(A))\nb_gpu = CuVector(rand(n)); x_gpu = similar(b_gpu)\n\nsolver = DirectSolver(A_gpu, \"SPD\", 'L')\nsetparam!(solver, \"ir_n_steps\", 1)            # one step of iterative refinement\nexecute!(\"analysis\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)\nexecute!(\"factorization\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)\nexecute!(\"solve\", solver, x_gpu, b_gpu)\nnorm(CuSparseMatrixCSR(A) * x_gpu - b_gpu) / norm(b_gpu)\n1.5641347800703364e-16","title":"On CUDA"},{"category":"section","location":"quickstart/#The-LinearAlgebra-layer","page":"Quick start","text":"cholesky, ldlt and lu run the analysis and the factorization at once and return the solver, which then acts as a factorization object. Solvers created this way perform two steps of iterative refinement per solve.\n\nF = cholesky(A_gpu; view = 'L')\nx_gpu = F \\ b_gpu\nlogdet(F) ≈ logdet(cholesky(Symmetric(Matrix(A))))\ntrue\n\nK = [A sprand(n, 50, 0.05); sprand(50, n, 0.05) -I]    # symmetric indefinite\nK = (K + K') / 2\nF = ldlt(CuSparseMatrixCSR(tril(K)); view = 'L')\ngetparam(F, \"inertia\")\n(2000, 50)\n\ncholesky!, ldlt! and lu! refactor in place with new values: cholesky!(F, A_gpu_new).","title":"The LinearAlgebra layer"},{"category":"section","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra-interface","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"A DirectSolver is a LinearAlgebra.Factorization. The functions below take a CSR matrix; the CUDA extension adds methods for CuSparseMatrixCSR, the AMDGPU extension for ROCSparseMatrixCSR.","title":"LinearAlgebra interface"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.cholesky-Tuple{CSR, NoPivot}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"cholesky(A::CSR, NoPivot(); view = 'F', check = false) -> DirectSolver\n\nLLᵀ (real T, structure \"SPD\") or LLᴴ (complex T, \"HPD\") factorization of the sparse matrix A on its backend: a DirectSolver after \"analysis\" and \"factorization\" (synchronized), with ir_n_steps = 2 steps of iterative refinement in its solves (the handle layer's default is 0; change it with setparam!). view selects the triangle of A that is read ('L', 'U', 'F'). As in CUDSS.jl a failed factorization does not throw unless check = true (then FactorizationError); otherwise check getparam(solver, \"info\"). A uniform batch (values of several members in A.nzval, see DirectSolver) is factorized member by member with one analysis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cholesky"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.ldlt-Tuple{CSR}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"ldlt(A::CSR; view = 'F', check = false) -> DirectSolver\n\nLDLᵀ (real T, structure \"S\") or LDLᴴ (complex T, \"H\") factorization of the sparse symmetric/Hermitian matrix A on its backend (≅ CUDSS.jl's ldlt): a DirectSolver after \"analysis\" and \"factorization\" (synchronized, ir_n_steps = 2 as cholesky), with in-front Bunch–Kaufman pivoting and static perturbation of tiny pivots (read getparam(solver, \"npivots\") and \"inertia\"). view as in cholesky. The factorization does not fail (\"info\" stays 0), so check has no effect; it is accepted for symmetry with cholesky.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.ldlt"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.lu-Tuple{CSR}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"lu(A::CSR; check = false) -> DirectSolver\n\nL D U factorization (structure \"G\") of the general sparse matrix A on its backend (≅ CUDSS.jl's lu): a DirectSolver after \"analysis\" and \"factorization\" (synchronized, ir_n_steps = 2 as cholesky), on the symmetric pattern of A + Aᵀ with row pivoting inside the fully-summed block of each front and static perturbation of tiny pivots (read getparam(solver, \"npivots\")). The factorization does not fail (\"info\" stays 0), so check has no effect. ldiv!/`solve withA;setparam!(solver, \"solve_mode\", 1)(or2) solves withAᵀ(Aᴴ`).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.lu"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.cholesky!-Tuple{DirectSolver, Any}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"cholesky!(solver::DirectSolver, A; check = false) -> solver\n\nFactorize the new values A (a CSR, SparseMatrixCSC, raw-array triple through update!, or a device sparse matrix of an extension) reusing the analysis of solver: update!, then \"factorization\" the first time after an analysis (solver.fresh_factorization) and \"refactorization\" afterwards, synchronized. check as in cholesky.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cholesky!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.ldlt!-Tuple{DirectSolver, Any}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"ldlt!(solver::DirectSolver, A; check = false) -> solver\n\nFactorize the new values A reusing the analysis of solver, as cholesky! (\"factorization\" the first time after an analysis, \"refactorization\" afterwards, synchronized).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.ldlt!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.lu!-Tuple{DirectSolver, Any}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"lu!(solver::DirectSolver, A; check = false) -> solver\n\nFactorize the new values A reusing the analysis of solver, as cholesky! (\"factorization\" the first time after an analysis, \"refactorization\" afterwards, synchronized).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.lu!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.ldiv!-Tuple{DirectSolver, AbstractVector}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"ldiv!(solver::DirectSolver, B) -> B\nldiv!(X, solver::DirectSolver, B) -> X\n\nSolve A X = B with the factorization in solver (\"solve\", synchronized), in place in B or into X. B, X: vectors, matrices, n × nrhs × nbatch arrays (uniform batch) or MatrixDescriptors on the solver's backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.ldiv!"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#Base.:\\-Tuple{DirectSolver, AbstractVector}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"solver \\ B\n\nldiv!(similar(B), solver, B) for a vector, matrix or n × nrhs × nbatch array B.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:\\"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.diag-Tuple{DirectSolver}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"diag(solver::DirectSolver)\n\nDiagonal of the factor L (Cholesky) or of D (LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ) in factor order (the \"diag\" data parameter), a vector on the solver's backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.diag"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#SparseArrays.nnz-Tuple{DirectSolver}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"nnz(solver::DirectSolver)\n\nNonzeros of the factor L (the \"lu_nnz\" data parameter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SparseArrays.nnz"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.logabsdet-Tuple{DirectSolver}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"logabsdet(solver::DirectSolver) -> (log|det A|, sign)\n\nCholesky: from the diagonal of the factor, log|det A| = 2 Σ log Lₖₖ, sign = one(T) (A is positive definite). LDLᵀ/LDLᴴ: det A = det D (the permutations cancel, det L = 1), the product of the 1×1 pivots and of the determinants of the 2×2 blocks; sign is ±1 (real or Hermitian) or det D / |det D| (complex symmetric). With perturbed pivots (\"npivots\" > 0) this is the determinant of A + E. Raises FactorizationError when the factorization failed (\"info\" ≠ 0) and NotSupportedError for a uniform batch (read the members' diagonals with getparam(solver, \"diag\")). LU (\"G\"): det A = ± det D, the sign of the local row interchanges times the product of the pivots (sign is ±1 for real T, a unit complex number otherwise).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.logabsdet"},{"category":"method","location":"lib/linearalgebra/#LinearAlgebra.logdet-Tuple{DirectSolver}","page":"LinearAlgebra interface","text":"logdet(solver::DirectSolver)\n\nlog det A of the positive definite matrix factorized in solver (the first value of logabsdet).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.logdet"},{"category":"section","location":"man/phases/#Phases","page":"Phases","text":"The handle API follows cuDSS: a DirectSolver holds the matrix, the parameters and every intermediate result, and execute! runs one phase on it, named by the same string as in CUDSS.jl.\n\nphase what it does needs\n\"reordering\" fill-reducing ordering on the host (AMD or nested dissection, user_perm), matching —\n\"symbolic_factorization\" elimination tree, supernodes, schedule, factor layout, device maps; allocates the numeric storage and the solve workspace \"reordering\"\n\"analysis\" \"reordering\" and \"symbolic_factorization\" —\n\"factorization\" numeric factorization with the current values; sets \"info\" analysis\n\"refactorization\" the same, reusing the analysis and the storage a factorization\n\"solve\" X = operatornameop(A)^-1 B, then ir_n_steps steps of refinement a factorization\n\"solve_fwd_perm\", \"solve_fwd\", \"solve_diag\", \"solve_bwd\", \"solve_bwd_perm\", \"solve_refinement\" the six parts of \"solve\", bitwise equal to it when run in sequence a factorization\n\"solve_fwd_schur\", \"solve_bwd_schur\" the solve phases of the Schur complement mode a factorization with schur_mode = 1\n\nThe named wrappers analyze!, factorize!, refactorize! and solve! are the corresponding execute! calls.","title":"Phases"},{"category":"section","location":"man/phases/#Typical-sequence","page":"Phases","text":"solver = DirectSolver(A, \"S\", 'L')          # A: CSR on a backend, or a backend sparse matrix\nsetparam!(solver, \"pivot_threshold\", 0.01)  # configuration before the phase that reads it\nexecute!(\"analysis\", solver, X, B)          # once per sparsity pattern\nfor k in 1:iterations\n    update!(solver, A_k)                    # new values, same pattern\n    execute!(k == 1 ? \"factorization\" : \"refactorization\", solver, X, B)\n    getparam(solver, \"info\") == 0 || error(\"factorization failed\")\n    execute!(\"solve\", solver, X, B)\nend","title":"Typical sequence"},{"category":"section","location":"man/phases/#Synchronization-and-errors","page":"Phases","text":"The phases are asynchronous on GPU backends, as in cuDSS: execute! returns once the work is queued, unless asynchronous = false. A failed factorization does not throw: getparam(solver, \"info\") is 0 on success and otherwise the column of the first failed pivot (a non-positive pivot for Cholesky; an exactly zero pivot with pivot_epsilon = 0 for LDLᵀ and LU, which perturb the other tiny pivots and count them in \"npivots\").\n\nRunning a phase before the phases it depends on raises a FactorizationError; an unknown phase string raises an ArgumentError. The flag \"user_host_interrupt\" (a Threads.Atomic{Bool}) is polled between launch groups and raises an InterruptedError when set.","title":"Synchronization and errors"},{"category":"section","location":"man/phases/#Logging","page":"Phases","text":"SDS_LOG_LEVEL=1 (or SparseDirectSolver.set_log_level!(1)) prints a summary of every phase; 2 adds the residual of every refinement step. With the default level the messages are @debug records, visible with JULIA_DEBUG=SparseDirectSolver.","title":"Logging"},{"category":"section","location":"man/phases/#Schur-complement-mode","page":"Phases","text":"With schur_mode = 1 and user_schur_indices (one 0/1 flag per row) set before the analysis, the flagged rows and columns are ordered last and the factorization stops before them. getparam(solver, \"schur_matrix\") returns S = A_22 - A_21 A_11^-1 A_12, dense or sparse (one triangle or the full matrix), and \"solve_fwd_schur\", \"solve_diag\" and \"solve_bwd_schur\" solve with the factored block around a solve with S done by the caller. See execute! for the vector layout.","title":"Schur complement mode"},{"category":"section","location":"man/phases/#Uniform-batches","page":"Phases","text":"A matrix whose values hold nbatch sets of values for one pattern (a vector of nbatch · nnz entries or an nnz × nbatch matrix) gives a batched solver: one analysis, one factor per member, and per-member \"info\", \"inertia\", \"npivots\" and \"pivot_stats\". \"ubatch_index\" and \"ubatch_mask\" restrict the factorization and the solves to some of the members. See DirectSolver.","title":"Uniform batches"},{"category":"section","location":"man/indefinite/#Symmetric-indefinite-systems","page":"Symmetric indefinite systems","text":"The KKT systems of interior-point methods,\n\nK = beginbmatrix H + Sigma  J^T  J  -delta I endbmatrix\n\nare symmetric and indefinite. They are factored with the structures \"S\" (L D L^T; complex symmetric for complex element types) and \"H\" (L D L^H), where D holds 1×1 and 2×2 pivots.","title":"Symmetric indefinite systems"},{"category":"section","location":"man/indefinite/#Pivoting","page":"Symmetric indefinite systems","text":"Inside every front the factorization uses Bunch–Kaufman pivoting: a 1×1 pivot is accepted when it is at least pivot_threshold (default 0.01) times the largest entry of its column, otherwise a 2×2 pivot is tried. Pivots are only exchanged within the fully-summed block of a front, so a pivot that fails there is not delayed to the parent: it is perturbed to pivot_epsilon and counted in \"npivots\". Two mechanisms keep perturbations rare on KKT matrices:\n\n2×2 pivot pairs at the analysis (pivot_pairs, default \"default\"): a row whose diagonal is structurally zero (or below pivot_pair_tolerance) is paired with a partner and the pair is kept in one supernode, so that the in-front pivoting can form the 2×2 block.\nMatching (matching_alg = \"algo5\" or \"algo6\"): the 2×2 pairs come from the cycles of a maximum-product matching, and the matrix is scaled symmetrically (the inertia is preserved).","title":"Pivoting"},{"category":"section","location":"man/indefinite/#Perturbation-sign-and-inertia","page":"Symmetric indefinite systems","text":"cuDSS perturbs a tiny pivot with the sign of the pivot. pivot_sign (one entry in (-1, 0, 1) per row) chooses the sign instead, for example +1 for the primal rows and -1 for the dual rows, so that the factored matrix K + E has the inertia the interior-point method expects.\n\nsolver = DirectSolver(K_gpu, \"S\", 'L')\nsetparam!(solver, \"pivot_sign\", Int8[fill(1, nh); fill(-1, nj)])\nexecute!(\"analysis\", solver, x, b)\nexecute!(\"factorization\", solver, x, b)\ngetparam(solver, \"inertia\")       # (npos, nneg), as MadNLP reads it\ngetparam(solver, \"pivot_stats\")   # (npos, nneg, nzero, nperturbed, n2x2)\n\n\"inertia\" is the inertia of D after perturbation; read it together with \"npivots\". With perturbed pivots, iterative refinement (ir_n_steps, or ir_mode = \"fgmres\" after using Krylov) recovers the accuracy of the solve.","title":"Perturbation sign and inertia"},{"category":"section","location":"man/indefinite/#General-matrices","page":"Symmetric indefinite systems","text":"The structure \"G\" factors P_r P A P^T = L D U on the symmetric pattern of A + A^T, with threshold partial pivoting by rows inside the fully-summed block of each front (pivot_threshold) and static perturbation of tiny pivots. \"perm_row\" and \"perm_col\" return the final permutations, and solve_mode solves with A^T or A^H. For matrices with zero or small diagonal entries, matching (matching_alg = \"algo5\") moves large entries to the diagonal and scales rows and columns before the ordering.","title":"General matrices"}]
}
